var e={"ap-blood-lymph":{f:[{e:`MCV (fL) = (Haematocrit percent x 10) / RBC count in millions per cu mm`,c:`Mean corpuscular volume. Normal 80 to 100 fL.`},{e:`MCH (pg) = (Hb in g/dL x 10) / RBC in millions per cu mm ; MCHC (percent) = (Hb x 100) / Haematocrit`,c:`MCH normal 27 to 32 pg; MCHC normal 32 to 36 percent (g/dL). Low MCV plus low MCHC means microcytic hypochromic anaemia, classically iron deficiency.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`WBC differential in decreasing order`,x:`Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas: Neutrophil > Lymphocyte > Monocyte > Eosinophil > Basophil.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Donor rules`,x:`O negative is the universal donor of packed red cells; AB positive is the universal recipient. For plasma the rule reverses - AB is the universal plasma donor.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Calcium is factor IV`,x:`Calcium is needed at almost every step of both pathways, which is why chelating it with EDTA, citrate or oxalate prevents clotting in a test tube. There is no factor VI - the name was withdrawn.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Vitamin K dependent factors`,x:`Think 1972: factors II, VII, IX and X, plus the natural anticoagulants protein C and protein S. All need gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues, the step warfarin blocks.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Spleen versus lymph node`,x:`The spleen has efferent lymphatics but no afferent lymphatics, so it filters blood and not lymph. It is the graveyard of red cells, performs culling and pitting, and in the fetus it is a site of haemopoiesis. Thymus is the primary lymphoid organ for T cells; bone marrow for B cells.`},{k:`exam`,t:`ESR movers`,x:`ESR rises in pregnancy, tuberculosis, rheumatoid arthritis, multiple myeloma and any acute phase response, mainly because fibrinogen and globulins promote rouleaux. It falls in polycythaemia, sickle cell disease and congestive cardiac failure.`}]},"ap-cardiovascular":{f:[{e:`Cardiac output (CO) = Stroke volume (SV) x Heart rate (HR)`,c:`Normal CO about 5 L/min = 70 mL x 72 per minute.`},{e:`SV = End diastolic volume - End systolic volume ; Ejection fraction = SV / EDV x 100`,c:`EDV about 130 mL, ESV about 60 mL, so SV about 70 mL and ejection fraction about 55 to 70 percent.`},{e:`Mean arterial pressure (MAP) = Diastolic BP + (Pulse pressure / 3) ; Pulse pressure = Systolic - Diastolic`,c:`For 120/80 mmHg, pulse pressure is 40 mmHg and MAP is about 93 mmHg.`},{e:`Cardiac index = Cardiac output / Body surface area`,c:`Normal 2.5 to 4.0 L/min/m2. Used to compare output between people of different size.`},{e:`CO (Fick) = O2 consumption / (Arterial O2 content - Venous O2 content)`,c:`Example: 250 mL/min divided by (0.20 - 0.15) mL O2 per mL blood = 5000 mL/min = 5 L/min.`},{e:`MAP = Cardiac output x Total peripheral resistance ; Resistance is proportional to 1 / r^4 (Poiseuille)`,c:`Halving the radius of an arteriole raises its resistance 16-fold, which is why arterioles are the main resistance vessels.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Valve cusps`,x:`LAB RAT: Left Atrium Bicuspid, Right Atrium Tricuspid.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Blood supply of the heart`,x:`The left coronary artery divides into the left anterior descending and circumflex branches; the LAD supplies the anterior interventricular septum and is the artery most often occluded in myocardial infarction. Venous blood returns through the coronary sinus into the right atrium.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Baroreceptor reflex`,x:`Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus (glossopharyngeal nerve, IX) and aortic arch (vagus, X) sense a rise in BP, increase their firing, and the vasomotor centre responds with vagal stimulation and sympathetic withdrawal - so heart rate, contractility and peripheral resistance all fall and BP returns to normal. It is a fast, seconds-to-minutes negative feedback loop.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Where does each ECG deflection actually come from`,x:`The T wave is ventricular repolarisation, not relaxation, and it is upright even though repolarisation is opposite in direction, because repolarisation spreads from epicardium to endocardium. A prolonged PR interval means first degree heart block; a widened QRS means bundle branch block.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Numbers worth memorising`,x:`Normal central venous pressure 0 to 8 mmHg, pulmonary capillary wedge pressure 6 to 12 mmHg, left ventricular systolic pressure 120 mmHg, right ventricular systolic pressure 25 mmHg, aortic diastolic pressure 80 mmHg.`}]},"ap-cell-tissue":{f:[{e:`Na+/K+ ATPase: 3 Na+ pumped out + 2 K+ pumped in per 1 ATP`,c:`It is electrogenic (net one positive charge leaves), which contributes about -4 mV to the resting membrane potential.`},{e:`E(ion) = (61 / z) x log10 ([ion] outside / [ion] inside) mV, at 37 degrees C`,c:`Nernst equation. E is the equilibrium potential, z the valency of the ion. E(K+) is about -90 mV, E(Na+) about +60 mV.`},{e:`Plasma osmolality (mOsm/kg) = 2 x [Na+] + [glucose] + [urea], all in mmol/L`,c:`Normal 285 to 295 mOsm/kg. Sodium and its anions supply about 90 percent of plasma osmolality.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Four tissue types`,x:`Every Cell Must Nourish: Epithelial, Connective, Muscular, Nervous.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Repeat one-liners`,x:`Mitochondrion is the powerhouse of the cell; lysosome is the suicide bag; ribosome is the protein factory; Golgi is the traffic police of the cell.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Positive feedback is not always harmful`,x:`Physiological positive feedback loops include oxytocin release during labour (Ferguson reflex), the platelet plug and clotting cascade, the LH surge before ovulation, and the Na+ influx phase of the action potential. Do not tick negative feedback for these.`},{k:`exam`,t:`High yield`,x:`Aquaporin-2 in the collecting duct is the ADH-sensitive water channel; GLUT4 in muscle and adipose is the only insulin-dependent glucose transporter.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Pumps and their inhibitors`,x:`Na+/K+ ATPase is inhibited by digoxin and ouabain; H+/K+ ATPase of the gastric parietal cell by omeprazole; Na+-K+-2Cl- symporter of the thick ascending limb by furosemide; Na+-Cl- symporter of the distal tubule by thiazides.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Isotonic is not isosmotic`,x:`0.9 percent NaCl is both isosmotic and isotonic. A 1.8 percent urea solution is isosmotic with plasma but not isotonic, because urea crosses the membrane freely and causes haemolysis. Tonicity counts only the non-penetrating solutes.`}]},"ap-digestive":{f:[{e:`Daily GIT fluid: 2 L intake + 7 L secretions = 9 L presented; 8 L absorbed by small intestine, 0.9 L by colon, 0.1 L lost in faeces`,c:`This is why secretory diarrhoea dehydrates so fast - only a small fraction of the load has to escape absorption.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`One-line recall`,x:`Vitamin B12 needs intrinsic factor from stomach parietal cells and is absorbed in the terminal ileum; iron is absorbed in the duodenum; bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Small intestine order`,x:`Dow Jones Index: Duodenum, Jejunum, Ileum.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which enzyme is secreted active?`,x:`Proteases are all secreted as inactive zymogens (pepsinogen, trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase) to protect the gland itself. Amylase and lipase are secreted already active because they cannot digest the gland.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Numbers worth memorising`,x:`Gastric juice 2 to 3 L/day at pH 1 to 2; pancreatic juice about 1.5 L/day at pH 8; bile 700 to 1200 mL/day; succus entericus 1.8 L/day; total absorptive area of small intestine about 200 square metres.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Portal blood does not carry fat`,x:`Short and medium chain fatty acids do enter portal blood, but long chain fatty acids are re-esterified and leave as chylomicrons in lymph, bypassing the liver on first pass. This is the basis of using medium chain triglycerides in malabsorption.`}]},"ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive":{f:[{e:`Creatine phosphate + ADP -> ATP + creatine (creatine kinase); usable ATP store supports only 2 to 3 seconds of maximal work`,c:`Creatine phosphate extends this to about 10 seconds, after which glycolysis and then oxidative phosphorylation take over. Oxygen debt is the extra oxygen consumed afterwards to clear lactate and restore stores.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Straight recall`,x:`206 bones in an adult and about 270 at birth; the femur is the longest bone and the stapes the smallest; there are 33 vertebrae (7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 fused sacral, 4 fused coccygeal) and 12 pairs of ribs.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Vertebrae count`,x:`Breakfast at 7, lunch at 12, dinner at 5 - cervical 7, thoracic 12, lumbar 5.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Oestrogen feedback flips`,x:`Oestrogen normally exerts negative feedback, but sustained high oestrogen for about 36 hours in the late follicular phase switches to positive feedback and triggers the LH surge. This is the one physiological positive feedback loop in the endocrine system besides oxytocin in labour.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Numbers that get asked`,x:`Basal body temperature rises about 0.5 degrees C after ovulation because of progesterone; pregnancy lasts about 280 days from the last menstrual period; the corpus luteum survives about 14 days without hCG.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Calcium in smooth versus skeletal muscle`,x:`Skeletal muscle Ca2+ acts on troponin C, a thin filament protein, and comes almost entirely from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Smooth muscle has no troponin at all - Ca2+ acts on calmodulin to regulate the thick filament, and much of it enters from outside, which is why calcium channel blockers relax vascular smooth muscle but do not weaken skeletal muscle.`}]},"ap-nervous":{f:[{e:`Nernst potential E(ion) = 61 / z x log10 ([ion]outside / [ion]inside)`,c:`E in millivolts at 37 degrees C, z the valency. Equilibrium potential is about +60 mV for Na+ and -90 mV for K+; the resting potential lies close to E(K+) because the membrane is far more permeable to potassium.`},{e:`Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz: Vm = 61 x log10 ( (PK[K]o + PNa[Na]o + PCl[Cl]i) / (PK[K]i + PNa[Na]i + PCl[Cl]o) )`,c:`P is permeability. At rest PK is roughly 100 times PNa, so Vm sits near E(K). At the peak of the spike PNa exceeds PK by about 20 times, so Vm swings towards E(Na).`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Brain and meninges`,x:`The three coverings of the brain from outside in are Dura, Arachnoid, Pia - remember DAP. Cerebrospinal fluid flows between arachnoid and pia, in the subarachnoid space.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Straight recall facts`,x:`Resting membrane potential -70 mV; threshold about -55 mV; there are 12 pairs of cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves; CSF volume is about 150 mL and is produced by the choroid plexus.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Cranial nerves`,x:`Names: Oh Oh Oh To Touch And Feel Very Green Vegetables AH. Type: Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter More (S sensory, M motor, B both).`},{k:`trap`,t:`Rods versus cones`,x:`Rods are more numerous (about 120 million) and far more sensitive but give no colour and poor acuity; cones number about 6 million, are concentrated at the fovea and need bright light. Vitamin A deficiency hits rods first, giving night blindness.`},{k:`exam`,t:`High-yield numbers`,x:`Adult brain weight about 1400 g; brain uses 20 percent of resting oxygen; normal CSF pressure 60 to 150 mm H2O; sound is amplified about 22 times by the ossicular lever and area ratio; human hearing range 20 to 20000 Hz.`}]},"ap-renal-endocrine":{f:[{e:`Net filtration pressure = Glomerular capillary pressure - (Capsular pressure + Plasma oncotic pressure)`,c:`Typically 60 - (18 + 32) = about 10 mm Hg. Filtration stops if capsular pressure rises (obstruction) or if oncotic pressure rises (dehydration).`},{e:`GFR = Kf x Net filtration pressure; Clearance of X = (Ux x V) / Px`,c:`Kf is the filtration coefficient. Ux is urine concentration, V urine flow rate in mL/min, Px plasma concentration. Inulin clearance equals GFR because inulin is freely filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted.`},{e:`Effective renal plasma flow = clearance of PAH; Renal blood flow = ERPF / (1 - haematocrit)`,c:`PAH is filtered and almost completely secreted in one pass, so its clearance (about 625 mL/min) estimates plasma flow. With a haematocrit of 0.45, RBF is about 1136 mL/min.`},{e:`Free water clearance CH2O = V - Cosm, where Cosm = (Uosm x V) / Posm`,c:`A positive value means dilute urine is being made (water diuresis, diabetes insipidus); a negative value means water is being conserved (high ADH, SIADH).`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Anterior pituitary hormones`,x:`FLAT PiG - FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH (the tropic ones) and Prolactin, GH (the direct ones).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Straight recall`,x:`Normal urine output 1 to 1.5 L/day; urine pH about 6 (range 4.5 to 8); GFR about 125 mL/min; renal blood flow about 1200 mL/min, which is 20 to 25 percent of cardiac output.`},{k:`trap`,t:`The posterior pituitary makes nothing`,x:`ADH and oxytocin are synthesised in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus and merely stored and released by the posterior pituitary, which is nervous tissue, not glandular.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Values examiners love`,x:`Filtered load of Na+ about 25000 mEq/day with more than 99 percent reabsorbed; maximum urine osmolality 1200 to 1400 mOsm/kg and minimum about 50 mOsm/kg; obligatory urine volume about 500 mL/day.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Aldosterone escape`,x:`Continued aldosterone excess does not cause endless oedema. Volume expansion raises atrial natriuretic peptide and pressure natriuresis, so sodium excretion resets - Conn syndrome presents with hypertension and hypokalaemia but characteristically without oedema.`}]},"ap-respiratory":{f:[{e:`IC = TV + IRV ; FRC = ERV + RV ; VC = IRV + TV + ERV ; TLC = VC + RV = IRV + TV + ERV + RV`,c:`Learn these four relations - most numerical questions are one substitution away.`},{e:`Alveolar ventilation = (Tidal volume - Dead space) x Respiratory rate`,c:`Example: (500 - 150) x 12 = 4200 mL/min. Deep slow breathing gives better alveolar ventilation than rapid shallow breathing at the same minute volume.`},{e:`Compliance = Change in volume / Change in pressure`,c:`Normal lung compliance about 200 mL per cm H2O. It falls in fibrosis and in surfactant deficiency, and rises in emphysema where elastic tissue is destroyed.`},{e:`Laplace law for an alveolus: P = 2T / r`,c:`P is collapsing pressure, T surface tension, r radius. A small alveolus would collapse into a large one; surfactant prevents this by lowering T more in small alveoli.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Phrenic nerve`,x:`C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Pleural pressure is negative`,x:`Intrapleural pressure stays subatmospheric throughout the cycle - about -5 cm H2O at end expiration and -8 cm H2O at end inspiration. If air enters the pleural space (pneumothorax) the pressure becomes atmospheric and the lung collapses.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Right shift`,x:`CADET, face Right: CO2, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temperature all shift the curve to the right.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Regulation of respiration`,x:`The central chemoreceptors in the medulla respond to CSF H+ generated from CO2 and are the main minute-to-minute drive. Peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies respond to a fall in pO2 below about 60 mmHg. In chronic CO2 retention the hypoxic drive takes over, which is why uncontrolled oxygen is dangerous in severe COPD.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Residual volume cannot be measured by spirometry`,x:`Because RV never leaves the lung, spirometry cannot measure RV, FRC or TLC. These need helium dilution, nitrogen washout or body plethysmography.`}]},"bc-carbohydrate":{f:[{e:`ATP per NADH = 2.5 ; ATP per FADH2 = 1.5`,c:`Modern consensus values used by current editions of Harper and Lehninger. Older texts use 3 and 2, which shifts all totals upward.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Only the liver can release free glucose`,x:`Glucose-6-phosphatase is present in liver and kidney but absent from muscle. That is why muscle glycogen cannot contribute directly to blood glucose.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Coenzymes of pyruvate dehydrogenase`,x:`Tender Loving Care For Nancy: Thiamine pyrophosphate, Lipoic acid, Coenzyme A, FAD, NAD+. The same five serve the alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase complexes.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate`,x:`This is a regulator, not a pathway intermediate. It activates phosphofructokinase-1 and inhibits fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, so it switches the liver towards glycolysis in the fed state. Insulin raises it; glucagon lowers it.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Rate-limiting enzymes of glycogen metabolism`,x:`Glycogen synthase for synthesis and glycogen phosphorylase for breakdown. Both are controlled by phosphorylation, but in opposite directions: phosphorylation activates phosphorylase and inactivates synthase. Glycogen phosphorylase requires pyridoxal phosphate.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Fructose and galactose disorders`,x:`Essential fructosuria (fructokinase deficiency) is harmless; hereditary fructose intolerance (aldolase B) is dangerous. Galactokinase deficiency causes cataract only; classical galactosaemia (galactose-1-phosphate uridyl transferase) causes liver and brain damage.`}]},"bc-enzymes":{f:[{e:`v = (Vmax x [S]) / (Km + [S])`,c:`v = initial velocity; Vmax = maximum velocity at saturating substrate; [S] = substrate concentration; Km = Michaelis constant, in mol/L.`},{e:`1/v = (Km/Vmax) x (1/[S]) + 1/Vmax`,c:`Lineweaver-Burk (double reciprocal) form. Slope = Km/Vmax, y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = -1/Km.`},{e:`Km(apparent) = Km x (1 + [I]/Ki)`,c:`Competitive inhibition. [I] = inhibitor concentration; Ki = dissociation constant of the EI complex. Vmax is unchanged.`},{e:`Vmax(apparent) = Vmax / (1 + [I]/Ki)`,c:`Pure non-competitive inhibition. Km is unchanged; Vmax falls by the same factor.`},{e:`kcat = Vmax / [E]total   and   catalytic efficiency = kcat / Km`,c:`kcat is the turnover number, molecules of substrate converted per enzyme molecule per second. kcat/Km at 10^8 to 10^9 per M per s means the enzyme is diffusion limited (catalytically perfect).`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Enzyme activity units`,x:`One International Unit (IU) is the amount of enzyme converting 1 micromole of substrate per minute under defined conditions. The SI unit is the katal (kat), 1 mole of substrate per second. 1 IU = 16.67 nanokatal.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Order of the six classes`,x:`Over The Hill Like In Louisiana: Oxidoreductase, Transferase, Hydrolase, Lyase, Isomerase, Ligase.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Competitive inhibition and Vmax`,x:`Vmax is never reduced by a competitive inhibitor because enough substrate can always outcompete it. Only the apparent Km rises. If a question says Vmax fell, it cannot be purely competitive.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The LDH flip`,x:`Normally serum LDH-2 exceeds LDH-1. After myocardial infarction the ratio flips so that LDH-1 exceeds LDH-2. Cardiac troponin I and T are now the preferred markers, but the flipped ratio is still a standard exam fact.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Ribozymes and abzymes`,x:`Not every catalyst is a protein. Ribozymes are catalytic RNA molecules; peptidyl transferase activity of the ribosome resides in the 23S rRNA. Abzymes are catalytic antibodies.`}]},"bc-lipid":{f:[{e:`Cycles = (n/2) - 1 ; Acetyl-CoA formed = n/2`,c:`n = number of carbons in a saturated even-chain fatty acid. Each cycle gives 1 FADH2 and 1 NADH.`},{e:`Net ATP = 10(n/2) + 4[(n/2) - 1] - 2`,c:`Using 2.5 ATP per NADH and 1.5 per FADH2, 10 ATP per acetyl-CoA in the TCA cycle, minus 2 for activation. For palmitate (n = 16) this gives 106 ATP.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Beta-hydroxybutyrate is not a ketone`,x:`Chemically it carries a hydroxyl group, not a keto group, so it does not respond to the nitroprusside (Rothera) test even though it is the most abundant ketone body in blood. Acetone is the one exhaled and responsible for the fruity breath.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Two HMG-CoA synthases`,x:`The mitochondrial enzyme feeds ketogenesis; the cytosolic enzyme feeds cholesterol synthesis. Same intermediate, two compartments, two fates.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Apoprotein functions`,x:`Apo C-II activates lipoprotein lipase, apo A-I activates LCAT, apo B-100 is the ligand for the LDL receptor, and apo E mediates hepatic uptake of remnants.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Regulation of HMG-CoA reductase`,x:`Four controls: transcriptional induction through SREBP-2 when sterols are low, feedback inhibition by cholesterol and mevalonate, inactivation by AMPK phosphorylation, and competitive inhibition by statins, which resemble mevalonate.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Fatty liver and lipotropic factors`,x:`Fat accumulates when triacylglycerol export as VLDL fails, usually from lack of phospholipid or apoprotein. Lipotropic factors that prevent it are choline, methionine, betaine, inositol and vitamin B12 and folate, all of which support phosphatidylcholine synthesis.`}]},"bc-nucleic-acid":{f:[{e:`Tm rises with (G + C) content`,c:`Tm is the melting temperature at which half the DNA is denatured. G-C pairs have three hydrogen bonds against two in A-T, so a higher GC fraction raises Tm.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Pyrimidines`,x:`CUT the Py: Cytosine, Uracil and Thymine are the pyrimidines. Thymine is in DNA only, uracil in RNA only.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Initiator amino acid`,x:`Prokaryotes start with N-formylmethionine and use the Shine-Dalgarno sequence to position the ribosome; eukaryotes start with ordinary methionine and use cap-dependent scanning. Both use AUG.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Purine degradation`,x:`Humans lack uricase, so the end product is uric acid, which is poorly soluble and causes gout. Xanthine oxidase, a molybdenum and FAD enzyme, catalyses the last two steps and is inhibited by allopurinol and febuxostat. Complete HGPRT deficiency gives Lesch-Nyhan syndrome, X-linked, with hyperuricaemia and self-mutilation; adenosine deaminase deficiency gives severe combined immunodeficiency.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Orotic aciduria`,x:`Hereditary orotic aciduria results from deficiency of UMP synthase and is treated with oral uridine, which bypasses the block and switches off CPS-II by feedback. Orotic aciduria with hyperammonaemia, however, points to ornithine transcarbamoylase deficiency instead.`}]},"bc-protein-aminoacid":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Essential amino acids`,x:`PVT TIM HALL: Phenylalanine, Valine, Threonine, Tryptophan, Isoleucine, Methionine, Histidine, Arginine, Leucine, Lysine.`},{k:`trap`,t:`CPS-I versus CPS-II`,x:`CPS-I is mitochondrial, uses ammonia as the nitrogen source, needs N-acetylglutamate and serves the urea cycle. CPS-II is cytosolic, uses glutamine, needs no activator and serves pyrimidine synthesis.`},{k:`exam`,t:`PKU essentials`,x:`Phenylalanine hydroxylase needs tetrahydrobiopterin, so a BH4 defect gives a malignant variant. Phenylalanine is shunted to phenylpyruvate, phenyllactate and phenylacetate. Tyrosine becomes an essential amino acid. Screening is by the Guthrie bacterial inhibition assay or tandem mass spectrometry, and treatment is a phenylalanine-restricted diet.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why OTC deficiency raises orotic acid`,x:`Carbamoyl phosphate that cannot be used by the blocked urea cycle leaks into the cytosol and is diverted into pyrimidine synthesis, so orotic acid rises. This is the single distinguishing test between CPS-I and OTC deficiency, and OTC deficiency is the only X-linked urea cycle disorder.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Selenocysteine, the twenty-first amino acid`,x:`It is inserted co-translationally at a UGA codon read by a special tRNA in the presence of a SECIS element, not by post-translational modification. It is found in glutathione peroxidase and iodothyronine deiodinase.`}]},"bc-vitamins-minerals":{f:[{e:`P/O ratio = ATP formed per atom of oxygen reduced`,c:`NADH gives 2.5 and FADH2 gives 1.5 on modern values; older texts quote 3 and 2. Roughly 4 protons are needed per ATP exported, 3 for the synthase and 1 for phosphate and nucleotide transport.`},{e:`delta G naught prime = -n x F x delta E naught prime`,c:`n = electrons transferred; F = Faraday constant, 96.5 kJ per volt per mole; delta E = difference in standard redox potential. For NADH (-0.32 V) to oxygen (+0.82 V), n = 2 and delta E = 1.14 V, giving about -220 kJ/mol.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Where the chain lives`,x:`All four respiratory complexes and ATP synthase are in the inner mitochondrial membrane, which is folded into cristae to increase area. The outer membrane is freely permeable to small molecules; the matrix holds the TCA cycle enzymes.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Vitamin K dependent factors`,x:`Clotting factors II, VII, IX and X plus proteins C and S. Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, which is why its effect is reversed by vitamin K. Mnemonic: 1972, for factors 10, 9, 7 and 2.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Order of carriers`,x:`NADH to FMN to coenzyme Q to cytochrome b to c1 to c to a to a3 to oxygen. Note the odd ordering: cytochrome c1 comes before cytochrome c.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Vitamin B12 absorption`,x:`B12 is bound in the stomach to intrinsic factor secreted by parietal cells and is absorbed in the terminal ileum. Deficiency therefore follows gastrectomy, autoimmune atrophic gastritis (pernicious anaemia), ileal resection and fish tapeworm infestation, not merely poor intake. Folate corrects the anaemia but does not stop the neurological damage.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Antioxidant defence`,x:`Superoxide dismutase needs copper and zinc in the cytosol and manganese in mitochondria; catalase is haem-based; glutathione peroxidase needs selenium and reduced glutathione, which is regenerated by glutathione reductase using NADPH from the HMP shunt. This is why G6PD deficiency causes oxidative haemolysis.`}]},"bp-absorption":{f:[{e:`dQ/dt = (D × A × Kp / h) × (Cgi − Cp)`,c:`Fick's first law of diffusion. dQ/dt = rate of absorption (mg/h); D = diffusion coefficient; A = surface area of absorbing membrane; Kp = partition coefficient of drug between membrane and gut fluid; h = membrane thickness; Cgi = drug concentration in gut fluid; Cp = drug concentration in plasma.`},{e:`For a weak acid:  pH = pKa + log ([ionised] / [unionised])`,c:`Henderson–Hasselbalch equation for an acid HA. [ionised] = concentration of the salt or conjugate base A−; [unionised] = concentration of free acid HA; pKa = negative log of the acid dissociation constant.`},{e:`For a weak base:  pH = pKa + log ([unionised] / [ionised])`,c:`Henderson–Hasselbalch equation for a base B. [unionised] = free base B; [ionised] = protonated form BH+. Note the numerator and denominator swap compared with an acid.`},{e:`% unionised acid = 100 / (1 + 10^(pH − pKa))       % unionised base = 100 / (1 + 10^(pKa − pH))`,c:`Direct working formulae. The exponent is (pH − pKa) for acids and (pKa − pH) for bases; the result is the fraction available for passive absorption, expressed as a percentage.`},{e:`dC/dt = (D × A / h) × (Cs − C)`,c:`Noyes–Whitney (modified) dissolution rate equation. dC/dt = dissolution rate; D = diffusion coefficient of drug; A = effective surface area of the solid; h = thickness of the stagnant diffusion layer; Cs = saturation solubility; C = concentration in bulk at time t. Under sink conditions C is negligible and the rate is proportional to Cs.`},{e:`Do = (Dose / V0) / Cs        An = Peff × (π × R × L) / Q        Dn = t(residence) / t(dissolution)`,c:`Dimensionless numbers. Do = dose number (V0 = 250 mL of water taken with the dose, Cs = saturation solubility). An = absorption number (Peff = effective permeability, R and L = radius and length of the intestine, Q = flow). Dn = dissolution number. Absorption is good when Do is low and An and Dn are high.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The one-line fact`,x:`The majority of drugs are absorbed by passive diffusion, which is non-saturable, needs no energy and no carrier, and follows first-order kinetics. This single statement has been asked in almost every GPAT paper in some form.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Absorption is not bioavailability`,x:`A drug can be 100 percent absorbed from the gut and still have low bioavailability if the liver destroys most of it on first pass (for example propranolol, lignocaine, morphine).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Rate-limiting physiology`,x:`Because absorption occurs mainly from the small intestine, gastric emptying is generally the rate-limiting physiological step for the onset of action of an orally administered drug. Rapid emptying is desirable except for drugs degraded in acid or absorbed by a saturable window (riboflavin, iron, levodopa).`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Order of release speed`,x:`Solution beats Suspension beats Emulsion beats capSule beats Tablet beats Coated tablet: remember "So Sweet Every Student Takes Chocolate".`},{k:`exam`,t:`Biowaiver logic`,x:`A biowaiver (approval on dissolution data instead of a human bioequivalence study) is granted for BCS Class I immediate-release products that dissolve rapidly, and has been extended to Class III drugs with very rapid dissolution. Class II formulation science (micronisation, solid dispersion) gives the biggest bioavailability gain.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Food effects go both ways`,x:`Food delays absorption of most drugs but increases the extent of absorption of griseofulvin, carbamazepine and lipophilic drugs by stimulating bile secretion; it reduces absorption of tetracyclines (chelation with calcium) and of penicillins and rifampicin (acid degradation with longer gastric residence).`}]},"bp-bioavailability-be":{f:[{e:`Absolute bioavailability, F = [AUC(oral) / AUC(iv)] × [Dose(iv) / Dose(oral)]`,c:`AUC = area under the plasma concentration–time curve from zero to infinity; Dose(iv) and Dose(oral) are the doses actually given by the two routes. F has no units and lies between 0 and 1 (or 0 to 100 percent).`},{e:`Relative bioavailability, Fr = [AUC(test) / AUC(standard)] × [Dose(standard) / Dose(test)]`,c:`Used when no intravenous form exists. The standard is a marketed reference product or an oral solution. Relative bioavailability can exceed 100 percent if the test product is better absorbed than the reference.`},{e:`90 percent confidence interval of the test / reference ratio of log-transformed AUC and Cmax must lie within 80.00 to 125.00 percent`,c:`The two one-sided tests procedure at alpha = 0.05. AUC and Cmax are log-transformed because they are log-normally distributed; the limits are asymmetric (80 to 125) because 0.80 and 1.25 are reciprocals on the log scale. tmax is compared non-parametrically and only where rapid onset matters.`},{e:`Dose dumping risk assessed as:  Cmax(fed) / Cmax(fasted)  and by  f2 similarity factor = 50 × log {[1 + (1/n) Σ (Rt − Tt)^2]^(−0.5) × 100}`,c:`f2 = similarity factor for dissolution profiles; n = number of time points; Rt and Tt = percentage dissolved of reference and test at time t. f2 between 50 and 100 means the profiles are similar; f1 (difference factor) should be 0 to 15.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Why bioavailability can be low`,x:`Incomplete release from the dosage form, poor aqueous solubility, degradation in gastric acid or by gut enzymes, complexation with food, efflux by P-glycoprotein, gut wall metabolism, and hepatic first-pass metabolism. Only the last of these is unrelated to how much drug crossed the gut wall.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Urinary data can substitute for plasma data`,x:`When the drug is largely excreted unchanged, bioavailability can be measured from cumulative urinary excretion Xu(infinity) instead of AUC, using the same ratio formula. The peak excretion rate replaces Cmax.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The number to remember`,x:`Bioequivalence is accepted when the 90 percent confidence interval for the geometric mean ratio of the log-transformed AUC and Cmax falls entirely within 80 to 125 percent. Not 95 percent CI, and not 80 to 120 percent.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Superimposable curves are not required`,x:`Two products can differ visibly in tmax and still be bioequivalent, provided the confidence intervals for AUC and Cmax pass. Conversely, equal AUC alone is not enough: Cmax must also fall within limits, because rate matters for single-dose therapy.`},{k:`exam`,t:`f2 conditions`,x:`The f2 test is valid only when at least 3 to 4 time points are used, not more than one point beyond 85 percent dissolution is included, and the coefficient of variation is under 20 percent at the earliest point and under 10 percent later.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Bioavailability is about the unchanged drug`,x:`The parent drug is measured, not the metabolite, unless the parent is unmeasurable or the metabolite carries the activity (as with a prodrug). Measuring the metabolite blunts differences in absorption rate because the metabolite curve is smoothed by the conversion step.`}]},"bp-compartment-models":{f:[{e:`C = C0 × e^(−Kel × t)        log C = log C0 − (Kel × t) / 2.303`,c:`One-compartment intravenous bolus. C = plasma concentration at time t; C0 = concentration at time zero (extrapolated); Kel = first-order elimination rate constant (per hour); t = time. A plot of log C against t is a straight line of slope −Kel/2.303.`},{e:`t½ = 0.693 / Kel        Kel = 0.693 / t½`,c:`Biological half-life for a first-order process. t½ = time for the plasma concentration to fall by half (hours); 0.693 = natural logarithm of 2. Half-life is independent of dose for first-order kinetics.`},{e:`Cl = Kel × Vd        AUC(0 to infinity) = C0 / Kel = Dose / Cl`,c:`Cl = total body clearance (L/h); Kel = elimination rate constant (per hour); Vd = apparent volume of distribution (L); AUC = area under the plasma concentration–time curve (mg·h/L).`},{e:`C = (R0 / Cl) × (1 − e^(−Kel × t))        Css = R0 / Cl = R0 / (Kel × Vd)`,c:`R0 = infusion rate (mg/h); Cl = clearance (L/h); Kel = elimination rate constant; Vd = volume of distribution; Css = steady-state plasma concentration reached when input rate equals output rate. Time to steady state depends only on t½, never on R0.`},{e:`Loading dose = Css × Vd        and with an infusion running, LD = Css × Vd (given as a bolus)`,c:`A loading dose fills the volume of distribution immediately so the target concentration is reached at once; the infusion then maintains it. Css = desired steady-state concentration; Vd = apparent volume of distribution.`},{e:`C = [(F × X0 × Ka) / (Vd × (Ka − Kel))] × (e^(−Kel × t) − e^(−Ka × t))`,c:`Bateman equation for first-order absorption into a one-compartment model. F = bioavailable fraction; X0 = dose; Ka = first-order absorption rate constant (per hour); Kel = elimination rate constant; Vd = apparent volume of distribution.`},{e:`tmax = 2.303 × log (Ka / Kel) / (Ka − Kel)        Cmax = (F × X0 / Vd) × e^(−Kel × tmax)`,c:`tmax = time of peak concentration; it depends only on the rate constants, not on the dose. Cmax = peak plasma concentration and is proportional to F and to dose. Increasing Ka lowers tmax and raises Cmax but does not change AUC.`},{e:`Wagner–Nelson:  (Xa / V)t = Ct + Kel × AUC(0 to t)        Fraction absorbed = [Ct + Kel × AUC(0−t)] / [Kel × AUC(0−infinity)]`,c:`Xa = cumulative amount absorbed up to time t; V = volume of distribution; Ct = plasma concentration at time t; Kel = elimination rate constant; AUC(0−t) = area up to time t; AUC(0−infinity) = total area.`},{e:`Trapezoidal rule:  AUC(t1 to t2) = (C1 + C2) × (t2 − t1) / 2        AUC(tlast to infinity) = Clast / Kel`,c:`C1 and C2 = plasma concentrations at consecutive times t1 and t2. Total AUC = sum of all trapezoids plus the extrapolated tail Clast/Kel, where Clast is the last measurable concentration.`},{e:`C = A × e^(−alpha × t) + B × e^(−beta × t)`,c:`Biexponential decline for a two-compartment open model given intravenously. A and B = zero-time intercepts of the distribution and elimination phases; alpha = hybrid distribution (fast) rate constant; beta = hybrid elimination (slow, terminal) rate constant, so t½(beta) = 0.693/beta. C0 = A + B.`},{e:`alpha × beta = K10 × K21        alpha + beta = K12 + K21 + K10        K21 = (A×beta + B×alpha)/(A + B)`,c:`Relations between hybrid and micro rate constants. K12 = transfer from central to peripheral compartment; K21 = peripheral to central; K10 = elimination from the central compartment. Vc = Dose / (A + B) is the central compartment volume.`},{e:`MRT = AUMC / AUC        Vd(ss) = Dose × AUMC / (AUC)^2        Cl = Dose / AUC        MRT = 1 / Kel (one compartment, IV bolus)`,c:`AUMC = area under the first-moment curve (plot of concentration × time against time); AUC = area under the curve; MRT = mean residence time, the average time a molecule stays in the body. For oral dosing, MAT = MRT(oral) − MRT(iv) = 1/Ka.`},{e:`Rate method:  log (dXu/dt) = log (Ke × X0) − Kel × t / 2.303        Sigma-minus:  log (Xu(infinity) − Xu) = log Xu(infinity) − Kel × t / 2.303`,c:`Urinary excretion methods. dXu/dt = rate of urinary excretion at the mid-point of the collection interval; Ke = renal excretion rate constant; X0 = dose; Xu = cumulative amount excreted up to time t; Xu(infinity) = total amount finally excreted. Both plots give a slope of −Kel/2.303.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The four to five half-life rule`,x:`After 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 half-lives, 50, 75, 87.5, 93.75 and 96.9 percent of the process is complete. So a drug is essentially eliminated, or a constant-rate infusion has essentially reached steady state, in about 4 to 5 half-lives, regardless of the dose or infusion rate.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Kel is not clearance`,x:`Kel is a fractional rate (the proportion of the body content removed per hour) and depends on Vd; clearance is a volume per hour and is the true measure of eliminating organ efficiency.`},{k:`exam`,t:`What each parameter tells you`,x:`Cmax and tmax describe the rate of absorption; AUC describes the extent of absorption. A formulation change that alters Cmax and tmax but leaves AUC unchanged has altered rate only, and the two products remain equally bioavailable in extent.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Flip-flop kinetics`,x:`Normally Ka is much larger than Kel and the terminal slope gives Kel. If absorption is slower than elimination (Ka less than Kel), as in a sustained-release or depot product, the terminal slope actually gives Ka and the residual line gives Kel. The two constants have swapped roles.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Rate versus sigma-minus`,x:`The sigma-minus (amount remaining to be excreted) method is more accurate because it is less sensitive to fluctuation in urine flow, but it demands urine collection until practically all the drug is out, that is at least 7 half-lives. The rate method needs a shorter collection but scatters more.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which half-life is quoted?`,x:`For a two-compartment drug the half-life reported in textbooks is the terminal (beta) half-life, not 0.693/K10. Using K10 will overestimate how quickly the drug leaves the body because it ignores the drug returning from the peripheral compartment.`}]},"bp-distribution-protein-binding":{f:[{e:`Vd = Dose (amount of drug in body) / C  =  X0 / C0`,c:`Apparent volume of distribution. X0 = dose administered intravenously (mg); C0 = plasma concentration at time zero obtained by back-extrapolation (mg/L); Vd is in litres, or L/kg when normalised to body weight.`},{e:`Vd (extrapolated) = X0 / C0        Vd (area) = X0 / (Kel × AUC)        Vd = Cl / Kel`,c:`X0 = intravenous dose; C0 = extrapolated zero-time plasma concentration; Kel = first-order elimination rate constant (per hour); AUC = area under the plasma concentration–time curve from 0 to infinity; Cl = total body clearance (L/h). Vd(area) is the value normally quoted for multi-compartment drugs.`},{e:`Loading dose = Vd × Css (target)  /  F        Maintenance dose rate = Cl × Css / F`,c:`Vd = volume of distribution (L); Css = target steady-state plasma concentration (mg/L); F = bioavailable fraction; Cl = clearance (L/h). Loading dose depends only on Vd, maintenance dose only on clearance.`},{e:`fu = Cu / C(total)        % bound = 100 × (C − Cu) / C`,c:`fu = fraction unbound (free); Cu = unbound plasma concentration; C = total plasma concentration. Only Cu is available for pharmacological effect, glomerular filtration and hepatic metabolism.`},{e:`Vd = Vp + Vt × (fu / fu,t)`,c:`Physiological (Oie–Tozer type) expression. Vp = plasma volume (about 3 L); Vt = volume of tissue water outside plasma (about 39 L); fu = fraction unbound in plasma; fu,t = fraction unbound in tissue. Vd rises when plasma binding falls (fu up) and falls when tissue binding falls (fu,t up).`},{e:`r = (n × Ka × [Df]) / (1 + Ka × [Df])       and       r / [Df] = n × Ka − r × Ka`,c:`Langmuir binding isotherm and its Scatchard rearrangement. r = moles of drug bound per mole of protein; n = number of binding sites per protein molecule; Ka = association constant (per molar); [Df] = free drug concentration. A Scatchard plot of r/[Df] against r is linear for one class of sites, slope = −Ka and x-intercept = n.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Reading a Vd value`,x:`Vd near 3 L means the drug stays in plasma, usually because it is highly bound to plasma albumin. Vd far greater than 42 L means extensive tissue binding or accumulation in fat. A Vd of several hundred litres does not violate physiology because it is only an apparent proportionality constant.`},{k:`trap`,t:`High binding is not the same as long action`,x:`Extensive plasma binding restricts the drug to plasma, giving a small Vd and often a long half-life, but the bound fraction is pharmacologically inactive. It is tissue binding, not plasma binding, that produces a large Vd.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The 95 to 90 rule`,x:`For a drug that is 95 percent bound, free fraction is 5 percent. If displacement reduces binding to 90 percent, the free fraction is 10 percent, that is the pharmacologically active concentration has doubled after only a 5 percent change in binding. This calculation is asked repeatedly.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Displacement is usually transient`,x:`A displacement interaction is clinically important only when the drug has a narrow therapeutic index, high binding (over 90 percent), small Vd and a low extraction ratio. For most drugs the extra free drug is simply cleared faster, so total concentration falls and free concentration returns to normal.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Alpha-1-acid glycoprotein is an acute-phase reactant`,x:`Its concentration rises in myocardial infarction, surgery, trauma, inflammation, Crohn disease and malignancy, and falls in nephrotic syndrome and in the neonate. Total lignocaine or propranolol levels therefore rise after infarction while free drug stays constant.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Ion trapping in milk and fetus`,x:`Breast milk (pH about 6.6) and the fetal compartment are slightly more acidic than maternal plasma (pH 7.4). Basic drugs become protonated and are trapped there, so basic drugs concentrate in milk while acidic drugs do not.`}]},"bp-metabolism-excretion":{f:[{e:`Clearance = Rate of elimination / Plasma concentration        Cl (total) = Cl(hepatic) + Cl(renal) + Cl(other)`,c:`Clearance is the volume of plasma completely cleared of drug per unit time (L/h or mL/min). Rate of elimination is in mg/h and plasma concentration in mg/L. Clearances from parallel organs are additive.`},{e:`ER = (Ca − Cv) / Ca        Cl(hepatic) = Q × ER        F(max) = 1 − ER`,c:`ER = hepatic extraction ratio (0 to 1); Ca = drug concentration in blood entering the liver (portal plus arterial); Cv = concentration leaving in the hepatic vein; Q = hepatic blood flow, about 1.5 L/min or 90 L/h in an adult; F(max) = maximum oral bioavailability possible when absorption is complete.`},{e:`Cl(hepatic) = Q × (fu × Cl(int)) / (Q + fu × Cl(int))`,c:`Well-stirred (venous equilibrium) model. Q = hepatic blood flow; fu = fraction unbound in blood; Cl(int) = intrinsic clearance, the maximum ability of the enzymes to metabolise drug in the absence of flow and binding limitations.`},{e:`Cl(renal) = (Cu × Vu) / Cp = Rate of urinary excretion / Plasma concentration`,c:`Cu = drug concentration in urine; Vu = urine flow rate (mL/min); Cp = plasma concentration at the mid-point of the collection; Cu × Vu = rate of urinary excretion (mg/min).`},{e:`CrCl (mL/min) = [(140 − age in years) × body weight in kg] / (72 × serum creatinine in mg/dL)      × 0.85 for females`,c:`Cockcroft–Gault equation for creatinine clearance, used as a surrogate for glomerular filtration rate when adjusting doses of renally cleared drugs such as aminoglycosides, digoxin and vancomycin.`},{e:`AUC(oral) = F × Dose / Cl = (Fa × Fg × Fh × Dose) / (fu × Cl(int))`,c:`For a low extraction drug given orally the fu terms cancel, so oral AUC depends only on intrinsic clearance. Fa = fraction absorbed; Fg = fraction escaping gut wall metabolism; Fh = fraction escaping hepatic first pass; fu = unbound fraction; Cl(int) = intrinsic clearance.`},{e:`Cl (from urine data) = Ae(infinity) × Kel / AUC        fe = Ae(infinity) / (F × Dose)`,c:`Ae(infinity) = total amount of unchanged drug recovered in urine; Kel = elimination rate constant; AUC = area under the plasma curve; fe = fraction of the absorbed dose excreted unchanged. Renal clearance = fe × total clearance.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The excretion equation to memorise`,x:`Rate of excretion = rate of filtration + rate of secretion − rate of reabsorption. Renal clearance greater than the filtration clearance (fu × GFR) proves net secretion; renal clearance less than it proves net reabsorption.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Clearance and half-life are not the same thing`,x:`Half-life depends on both clearance and volume of distribution. A patient can have unchanged clearance and a longer half-life purely because Vd increased (for example oedema), so half-life alone is a poor index of eliminating capacity.`},{k:`exam`,t:`First-pass effect`,x:`A drug with a high extraction ratio undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism, so oral bioavailability is low and variable and the oral dose is far larger than the parenteral dose (propranolol 40 mg oral versus 1 mg intravenous). Routes that bypass the portal circulation are sublingual, rectal (lower two-thirds), transdermal, inhalational and parenteral.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Enzyme inducers`,x:`"CRAP GPS": Carbamazepine, Rifampicin, Alcohol (chronic), Phenytoin, Griseofulvin, Phenobarbitone, Sulphonylureas and Smoking.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The classic reasoning question`,x:`For an orally administered low-extraction drug, a displacement interaction raises the free fraction but does not change the average free steady-state concentration, because clearance of unbound drug (Cl(int)) is unchanged. Total measured concentration falls, which is why therapeutic drug monitoring of phenytoin in uraemia must use free levels.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Biliary excretion thresholds`,x:`Biliary excretion favours molecules with molecular weight above about 500 (in humans 300 to 500 is borderline), polar anions and cations and conjugates. Below about 300 the compound is reabsorbed and returns to plasma, so it appears in urine instead.`}]},"bp-nonlinear-multiple-dosing":{f:[{e:`−dC/dt = (Vmax × C) / (Km + C)`,c:`Michaelis–Menten equation applied to elimination. Vmax = maximum rate of metabolism (mg/day or mg/h); Km = Michaelis constant, the drug concentration at which the rate is half of Vmax (mg/L); C = plasma concentration.`},{e:`Accumulation ratio, R = 1 / (1 − e^(−Kel × tau))`,c:`R = ratio of steady-state concentration to first-dose concentration at the same time point; Kel = elimination rate constant; tau = dosing interval. When tau equals one half-life, e^(−Kel×tau) = 0.5 and R = 2.`},{e:`Css(average) = (F × X0) / (Cl × tau) = (F × X0) / (Kel × Vd × tau) = 1.44 × t½ × F × X0 / (Vd × tau)`,c:`Css(average) = average plasma concentration at steady state over one interval; F = bioavailable fraction; X0 = maintenance dose; Cl = clearance; tau = dosing interval; Vd = volume of distribution; 1.44 = 1/0.693.`},{e:`Css(max) = (F × X0 / Vd) × 1/(1 − e^(−Kel × tau))        Css(min) = Css(max) × e^(−Kel × tau)`,c:`Peak and trough at steady state for an intravenous bolus multiple-dose regimen. F = bioavailability; X0 = dose; Vd = volume of distribution; Kel = elimination rate constant; tau = dosing interval.`},{e:`Fluctuation = (Css(max) − Css(min)) / Css(average)        Loading dose = Maintenance dose × R = Maintenance dose / (1 − e^(−Kel × tau))`,c:`R = accumulation ratio. When tau equals one half-life the loading dose is exactly twice the maintenance dose. Fluctuation falls as the dosing interval is shortened or as a sustained-release form is used.`},{e:`Dosing rate at steady state, R = (Vmax × Css) / (Km + Css)        rearranged:  Css = (Km × R) / (Vmax − R)`,c:`R = daily maintenance dose rate (mg/day, corrected for bioavailability and salt factor); Vmax = maximum metabolic capacity (typically 7 mg/kg/day for phenytoin in adults); Km = Michaelis constant (typically 4 mg/L for phenytoin); Css = steady-state plasma concentration.`},{e:`Direct linear (two-point) method:  Vmax = R1 + Km × (R1 / Css1)  with  Km = (R2 − R1) / [(R1/Css1) − (R2/Css2)]`,c:`R1 and R2 = two different daily dose rates administered to the same patient; Css1 and Css2 = the corresponding steady-state concentrations. Solving the pair of Michaelis–Menten equations gives that patient specific Vmax and Km, which then predicts the dose needed for a target level.`},{e:`Time to reach 90 percent of a new steady state (Michaelis–Menten) = [Km × Vmax / (Vmax − R)^2] × [2.3 × Vmax − 0.9 × R] × Vd / Vmax`,c:`An approximate expression showing that the time lengthens sharply as the dose rate R approaches Vmax. Km = Michaelis constant; Vmax = maximum elimination rate; R = dosing rate; Vd = volume of distribution. The practical message is that a phenytoin level should not be rechecked for at least 1 to 2 weeks after a dose change.`},{e:`Css(max, oral) = [F × X0 / (Vd × (1 − e^(−Kel × tau)))] × e^(−Kel × tmax)`,c:`Multiple-dose oral peak. F = bioavailability; X0 = dose; Vd = apparent volume of distribution; Kel = elimination rate constant; tau = dosing interval; tmax = time of peak after each dose. The trough is obtained by multiplying by e^(−Kel × (tau − tmax)).`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Signature of non-linearity`,x:`A more than proportional rise in AUC and steady-state concentration with dose, a half-life that lengthens as the dose rises, and a clearance that falls with dose. Ethanol is the classic pure zero-order example, eliminated at about 10 mL (or 10 g) per hour irrespective of the amount drunk.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Changing the dose does not change the time to steady state`,x:`A bigger dose gives a higher steady-state level but reaches it in the same 4 to 5 half-lives. Only a loading dose gets you there faster, and only a change in half-life changes the time required.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Phenytoin numbers`,x:`Therapeutic range 10 to 20 mg/L (mcg/mL); typical adult Km about 4 mg/L and Vmax about 7 mg/kg/day. Because Css is in the saturated region, a small dose increment (300 to 400 mg/day) can more than double the plasma level and cause nystagmus, ataxia and confusion.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Half-life has no fixed meaning in non-linear kinetics`,x:`For a Michaelis–Menten drug the apparent half-life increases as the concentration rises, so quoting a single half-life is meaningless. Time to a new steady state after a dose change can therefore be far longer than the usual 4 to 5 half-lives, often 1 to 2 weeks for phenytoin.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Choosing tau by therapeutic index`,x:`The largest sensible dosing interval is set by the ratio of the maximum safe to the minimum effective concentration: tau(maximum) = (1/Kel) × ln (Cmax(safe) / Cmin(effective)) = 3.32 × t½ × log (Cmax/Cmin). Drugs with a narrow index need short intervals or a sustained-release form.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Salt factor and bioavailability must both be applied`,x:`Phenytoin sodium contains only 92 percent phenytoin (S = 0.92), so a 300 mg capsule delivers about 276 mg of acid. Forgetting S, or forgetting F, is the commonest arithmetic error in dose-rate calculations.`}]},"bs-descriptive":{f:[{e:`Mean = sum of all values / n`,c:`n = number of observations. Also written x-bar for a sample.`},{e:`Range = highest value - lowest value`,c:`Simplest measure of spread; uses only two observations, so it is unstable.`},{e:`s2 = sum (x - x-bar)^2 / (n - 1)`,c:`s2 = sample variance, x = each observation, x-bar = sample mean, n = sample size. The divisor (n - 1) is the degrees of freedom.`},{e:`s = square root of s2`,c:`s = sample standard deviation, in the same units as the original data.`},{e:`CV (%) = (s / x-bar) x 100`,c:`CV = coefficient of variation, a unitless relative measure of spread used to compare variability across different units or magnitudes.`},{e:`SEM = s / square root of n`,c:`SEM = standard error of the mean, the SD of the sampling distribution of the mean. It shrinks as n grows; SD does not.`},{e:`Pearson coefficient of skewness = (mean - mode) / SD`,c:`An alternative form is 3 x (mean - median) / SD. Zero indicates a symmetrical distribution.`},{e:`Mode = 3 x median - 2 x mean`,c:`Karl Pearson's empirical relation for a moderately skewed unimodal distribution.`},{e:`Quartile deviation = (Q3 - Q1) / 2`,c:`Q1 and Q3 are the first and third quartiles. Also called the semi-interquartile range; it is robust to outliers.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Degrees C is interval, not ratio`,x:`Twenty degrees Celsius is not twice as hot as ten, because zero Celsius is an arbitrary point, not absolute absence of heat. Kelvin is a ratio scale. This exact distinction is a favourite GPAT item.`},{k:`exam`,t:`SD versus SEM`,x:`SD describes the spread of individual observations. SEM describes the precision of the estimated mean. SEM is always smaller than SD, and SEM keeps falling as sample size rises.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Lepto is leaping, platy is a plateau`,x:`Leptokurtic leaps up into a tall thin peak. Platykurtic is a flat plateau, like a platypus bill.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why divide by n minus 1`,x:`Dividing the sum of squares by n gives a biased estimate that underestimates the population variance, because deviations are taken from the sample mean rather than the true mean. Using n - 1 degrees of freedom corrects this. Divide by n only when the data are the entire population.`}]},"bs-doe-regression":{f:[{e:`r = covariance of x and y / (SD of x x SD of y)`,c:`Karl Pearson's product moment correlation coefficient, r, ranging from -1 to +1.`},{e:`y = a + bx`,c:`y = dependent variable (response), x = independent variable (dose or concentration), a = intercept, b = slope or regression coefficient.`},{e:`b = sum of (x - x-bar)(y - y-bar) / sum of (x - x-bar)^2`,c:`Least squares estimate of the slope; it minimises the sum of squared vertical deviations from the line.`},{e:`r = square root of (byx x bxy)`,c:`byx = regression coefficient of y on x, bxy = of x on y. The correlation coefficient is the geometric mean of the two regression coefficients.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Correlation is not causation`,x:`A high r only shows that two variables move together. A third confounding variable, or pure coincidence, can produce the same pattern.`},{k:`exam`,t:`r squared is the coefficient of determination`,x:`If r = 0.9 then r squared = 0.81, meaning 81 per cent of the variation in y is explained by x. ICH Q2 linearity is normally reported as r or r squared with the residual plot.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Latin square constraint`,x:`In a Latin square the number of treatments, rows and columns must be equal, so a 4 x 4 square needs exactly 4 treatments and 16 runs. It also assumes there is no interaction between the two blocking factors.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Extrapolation is invalid`,x:`A regression equation is only valid over the range of x actually studied. Predicting a response outside that range, such as an absorbance far above the calibration curve, is not supported by the data.`}]},"bs-hypothesis-tests":{f:[{e:`t = (x-bar1 - x-bar2) / SE of difference`,c:`Unpaired t-test. Degrees of freedom = n1 + n2 - 2.`},{e:`t = d-bar / (sd / square root of n)`,c:`Paired t-test. d-bar = mean of the paired differences, sd = SD of the differences, df = n - 1.`},{e:`F = mean square between groups / mean square within groups`,c:`F ratio. Mean square = sum of squares / degrees of freedom. A large F argues against H0.`},{e:`chi-square = sum of (O - E)^2 / E`,c:`O = observed frequency, E = expected frequency. Degrees of freedom for an r x c table = (r - 1)(c - 1).`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Type I is the false alarm`,x:`Type I error says a drug works when it does not. Type II error misses a drug that really works. One before two: the false positive comes first.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Paired means the same subject`,x:`Use the paired t-test only for before-and-after readings on the same individual or for deliberately matched pairs. Applying an unpaired test to paired data wastes information and lowers power.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Yates' correction`,x:`For a 2 x 2 table with small expected frequencies, apply Yates continuity correction by subtracting 0.5 from each absolute difference. If any expected frequency is below 5, use Fisher exact test instead.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Statistical versus clinical significance`,x:`A very large trial can make a 1 mmHg fall in blood pressure statistically significant while being clinically meaningless. Always read the effect size and confidence interval, not only the p-value.`}]},"bs-probability-distributions":{f:[{e:`P(event) = favourable outcomes / total possible outcomes`,c:`Classical definition, valid when all outcomes are equally likely.`},{e:`z = (x - mu) / sigma`,c:`x = observed value, mu = population mean, sigma = population SD. The standard normal distribution has mean 0 and SD 1.`},{e:`P(x) = nCx x p^x x q^(n-x)`,c:`Binomial probability. n = trials, x = successes, p = probability of success, q = 1 - p.`},{e:`P(x) = (e^-lambda x lambda^x) / x!`,c:`Poisson probability. lambda = mean number of events per interval, e = 2.718.`},{e:`95% CI = x-bar +/- 1.96 x (s / square root of n)`,c:`x-bar = sample mean, s = sample SD, n = sample size. Use the t value instead of 1.96 when n is small.`},{e:`n = (z x sigma / E)^2`,c:`Sample size for estimating a mean. z = 1.96 for 95 per cent confidence, sigma = expected SD, E = acceptable margin of error.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`The 68-95-99.7 rule`,x:`About 68 per cent of values lie within 1 SD of the mean, about 95 per cent within 2 SD, and about 99.7 per cent within 3 SD.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Exact percentage limits`,x:`The precise multipliers are 1.96 SD for 95 per cent and 2.58 SD for 99 per cent. The 2 SD and 3 SD figures are the rounded teaching version.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Poisson from binomial`,x:`The Poisson distribution is the limiting case of the binomial when n is large and p is very small, with lambda = np. It is the distribution of rare events.`},{k:`trap`,t:`What a 95 per cent CI actually means`,x:`It does not mean there is a 95 per cent probability that the true mean lies in this one interval. It means that if the study were repeated many times, 95 per cent of such intervals would contain the true population mean.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Width of the interval`,x:`A 99 per cent CI is wider than a 95 per cent CI for the same data. Higher confidence is bought with lower precision.`}]},"bt-antibodies-vaccines":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Why HAT medium selects hybridomas`,x:`HAT = hypoxanthine, aminopterin, thymidine. Aminopterin blocks dihydrofolate reductase and therefore the de novo synthesis of purines and pyrimidines, so any surviving cell must use the salvage pathway, which needs the enzyme HGPRT. The myeloma partner is HGPRT-deficient, so unfused myeloma cells die. Unfused B lymphocytes have HGPRT but are mortal and die out on their own. Only the hybridoma, which takes HGPRT from the B cell and immortality from the myeloma, survives and grows.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why conjugate vaccines exist`,x:`A bare capsular polysaccharide is a T-independent antigen: it gives a poor IgM-only response with no memory and works badly under two years of age. Coupling it to a protein carrier such as tetanus toxoid makes it T-dependent, giving class switching to IgG and immunological memory in infants.`},{k:`exam`,t:`High-frequency facts`,x:`PEG is the fusing agent in hybridoma production; the myeloma partner is HGPRT-deficient; -ximab is chimeric and -zumab is humanised; rituximab targets CD20 and trastuzumab targets HER2; BCG and oral polio are live attenuated while tetanus is a toxoid.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Suffix ladder, least to most human`,x:`Mouse, Chimera, Humanised, Human = -omab, -ximab, -zumab, -umab. Losing letters means gaining humanity: o, xi, zu, u.`}]},"bt-enzyme-technology":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Five methods`,x:`A CEEC list: Adsorption, Covalent, Entrapment, Encapsulation, Cross-linking. The first two attach the enzyme to something; the next two shut it in somewhere; the last attaches it to itself.`},{k:`trap`,t:`What immobilisation does to the kinetics`,x:`Immobilisation does not change the intrinsic enzyme, but the measured constants change. Because substrate must diffuse through the support, the apparent Km usually rises (the enzyme seems to have lost affinity) and the apparent Vmax usually falls. Km itself is defined as the substrate concentration at half Vmax and its units remain those of concentration, for example mol/L.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The distinctions that get asked`,x:`Entrapment traps the enzyme in a gel lattice while encapsulation shuts it inside a membrane capsule; cross-linking needs no carrier; adsorption is the cheapest but leaches; covalent binding is the strongest but loses the most activity. Streptokinase is antigenic, urokinase is not.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Enzyme units`,x:`One international unit (IU) of an enzyme is the amount that converts one micromole of substrate per minute under defined conditions. The SI unit, the katal, is the amount converting one mole per second, so 1 katal equals 6 x 10^7 IU. Specific activity is units per milligram of protein and is the measure of purity.`}]},"bt-rdna":{f:[{e:`Copies after n cycles = 2^n x (starting copies)`,c:`n = number of completed cycles. Amplification is exponential, so 30 cycles from one template give of the order of 10^9 copies; in practice the yield plateaus as reagents are used up.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Sticky beats blunt`,x:`Sticky ends base-pair with any fragment cut by the same enzyme, so ligation is far more efficient and directional. Blunt ends will join any two fragments but ligate slowly and give no orientation control. T4 DNA ligase can join both; E. coli DNA ligase joins sticky ends only.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`SNoW DRoP`,x:`Read the capitals across: S-N-W matches D-R-P. Southern = DNA, Northern = RNA, Western = Protein. Only Southern is named after a person (Edwin Southern); the rest are jokes on the compass.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Two classic confusions`,x:`RT-PCR (reverse transcription PCR, starts from RNA) is not the same as real-time PCR (quantitative, fluorescence read each cycle), even though both are abbreviated RT-PCR in sloppy writing. And DNA ligase forms a phosphodiester bond, not a hydrogen bond; the hydrogen bonds between sticky ends form on their own by base pairing.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Asked again and again`,x:`Taq polymerase and 72 degrees C for extension; EcoRI gives sticky ends and SmaI blunt ends; Western blot detects protein using an antibody; YAC has the largest insert capacity; hepatitis B vaccine is the first recombinant vaccine.`}]},"cs-hair-oral-products":{f:[{e:`ppm F = percent salt x (atomic mass of F / molecular mass of salt) x 10000`,c:`ppm F = parts per million of fluoride ion; F = 19; NaF = 42 approximately. Multiplying percent w/w by 10000 converts to ppm.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Hair layers`,x:`CCM from outside in: Cuticle, Cortex, Medulla. Cuticle = shine, Cortex = strength and colour, Medulla = mostly nothing.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Three facts that keep appearing`,x:`Shampoo surfactant is anionic (SLS); conditioner surfactant is cationic. Dandruff is linked to the yeast Malassezia furfur (Pityrosporum ovale). Depilatories break disulfide bonds; they do not cut hair.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Never mix the charges`,x:`Cationic conditioning quats are inactivated by anionic surfactants such as SLS. Real two-in-one shampoos get round this using silicones and cationic polymers, not simple quats. Similarly, chlorhexidine (cationic) is inactivated by the SLS in toothpaste, so the rinse should be spaced about 30 minutes from brushing.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Bond chemistry in one line`,x:`Thioglycolate reduces disulfide (reversible, perming and depilation). Hydroxide converts cystine to lanthionine (irreversible, straightening). Peroxide oxidises PPD to build indo dye inside the cortex (permanent colour).`}]},"cs-skin-products":{f:[{e:`SPF = MED of protected skin / MED of unprotected skin`,c:`SPF = sun protection factor; MED = minimal erythema dose, the smallest UV dose producing just-perceptible redness. SPF measures UVB protection only.`},{e:`Percent UVB blocked = (1 - 1/SPF) x 100`,c:`SPF = sun protection factor. The relationship is hyperbolic, so protection rises steeply up to about SPF 30 and then flattens.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Epidermal layers, outside in`,x:`Come Let’s Get Sun Burnt = Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale. Lucidum is present only in thick skin such as palms and soles.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The two facts asked most often`,x:`Cold cream is water-in-oil; vanishing cream is oil-in-water. If you remember nothing else from this topic, remember that pair.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`UVA versus UVB`,x:`A for Ageing (and deeper, all day, through glAss); B for Burning (and Blocked by glass). SPF rates B only.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Deodorant is not antiperspirant`,x:`Every antiperspirant is also a deodorant, but a deodorant alone does not reduce sweat volume. Antiperspirants are aluminium salts; deodorants are antibacterials and perfumes.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Numbers worth memorising`,x:`SPF 15 = 93.3 percent, SPF 30 = 96.7 percent, SPF 50 = 98 percent of UVB blocked. Lead 20 ppm, arsenic 2 ppm, mercury 1 ppm. Hexachlorophene 0.1 percent maximum.`}]},"dp-dosage-forms":{f:[{e:`Quantity of solute (g) = percentage w/v x volume (mL) / 100`,c:`The single equation behind almost every dispensing calculation involving a solid in a liquid.`},{e:`Alligation medial: final strength = sum of (quantity x strength) / total quantity`,c:`A simple weighted average, used when you already know how much of each strength was used.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`w/v is not the same as w/w`,x:`One hundred millilitres of a solution does not weigh 100 g unless its density is exactly 1 g/mL. For anything other than dilute aqueous solutions you must use the density before converting between w/v and w/w.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Remember 57.1 percent v/v`,x:`The single number most asked about proof spirit is that it contains about 57.1 percent v/v ethanol, equivalent to about 49.24 percent w/w. Everything else in this section follows from it.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Geometric dilution`,x:`Whenever a question gives a very small quantity of a potent drug to be mixed into a large bulk of diluent, the required technique is geometric dilution. It is the standard answer for content uniformity of a dispensed powder.`}]},"dp-incompatibility":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The classic eutectic pairs`,x:`Camphor, menthol, thymol, phenol, salol, betanaphthol, chloral hydrate, acetanilide and antipyrine are the substances repeatedly quoted as forming eutectic mixtures when triturated together. Any two of them in a powder prescription should ring an alarm.`},{k:`trap`,t:`A perfectly clear mixture can still be incompatible`,x:`Therapeutic incompatibility produces no visible change at all. Questions that describe a preparation as elegant and stable but harmful to the patient are pointing at therapeutic, not physical or chemical, incompatibility.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Absorbents for eutectics`,x:`Light magnesium oxide, light magnesium carbonate, light kaolin and starch are the absorbents named again and again for eutectic powders. Note the word light: the light grades have a far larger bulk and surface area and take up the liquid better.`}]},"dp-posology":{f:[{e:`Young's rule: child dose = adult dose x age (years) / (age + 12)`,c:`Uses age. Traditionally applied to children between 1 and 12 years.`},{e:`Clark's rule: child dose = adult dose x weight in pounds / 150`,c:`Uses body weight; 150 pounds is the assumed adult weight. In metric form, child dose = adult dose x weight in kg / 70.`},{e:`Dilling's rule: child dose = adult dose x age (years) / 20`,c:`Uses age; a simple linear rule, generally applied to older children.`},{e:`Fried's rule: infant dose = adult dose x age in months / 150`,c:`Uses age in months and is intended for infants under about two years.`},{e:`Body surface area rule: child dose = adult dose x BSA of child (m2) / 1.73`,c:`1.73 m2 is the accepted average body surface area of an adult. This is the most reliable of the rules.`},{e:`BSA (m2) = square root of [height (cm) x weight (kg) / 3600]`,c:`Mosteller formula for body surface area, the form most used in practice because it can be done on a simple calculator.`},{e:`CrCl (mL/min) = (140 - age) x weight (kg) / (72 x serum creatinine in mg/dL)`,c:`Cockcroft and Gault equation. Multiply the result by 0.85 for a female patient.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Therapeutic index is not a dose`,x:`The therapeutic index is a ratio, usually LD50 divided by ED50, describing the margin of safety of a drug. It is not a quantity to be given, and a drug with a narrow therapeutic index simply needs closer dose control.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which rule uses what`,x:`Clark and Weight both have a hard C-K sound of counting kilograms; Fried is for the tiny fry, so it uses months; Young and Dilling both use years, and Young alone adds 12 to the age.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Body surface area beats age and weight`,x:`When a question asks which method of calculating a paediatric dose is the most accurate, the answer is the body surface area method, because surface area tracks metabolic rate, cardiac output and renal function better than either age or weight alone.`}]},"dp-prescription":{f:[],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Subscription and signatura are different`,x:`The subscription talks to the pharmacist (how to make and how much to send). The signatura talks to the patient (how to take it). Swapping the two is the most common mistake in this topic.`},{k:`exam`,t:`o.d. is ambiguous`,x:`In older ophthalmic usage o.d. meant oculus dexter, the right eye, while in general usage it means omni die, once daily. Because of this ambiguity such abbreviations are discouraged in modern safe-prescribing practice and the words should be written out.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The three-point check`,x:`Read the container label when you take it from the shelf, when you pour or count, and when you replace it. This simple discipline is asked about directly and is the standard answer to how dispensing errors are prevented.`}]},"ht-nutraceuticals-cosmetics":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Who regulates what`,x:`Nutraceuticals and health supplements: FSSAI, under the food law. Cosmetics: the Drugs and Cosmetics Act. ASU medicines: Chapter IVA of the same Act with Schedule T for GMP. Getting this triangle right answers most regulatory questions in this topic.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Sunscreen is not a plant job`,x:`Plant polyphenols are photoprotective antioxidants, but the ultraviolet filters that give a measured SPF are zinc oxide, titanium dioxide and the organic filters. A herbal extract alone cannot be claimed to give a stated SPF.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`The bleeding Gs`,x:`Garlic, Ginkgo, Ginger and Ginseng all increase bleeding tendency, so ask about them before surgery or in a patient on warfarin. The odd one out is St John’s wort, which is an inducer rather than an antiplatelet.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Most asked in this topic`,x:`St John's wort induces CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein and causes oral contraceptive failure; ginkgo increases bleeding risk; lawsone is the colouring principle of henna; glabridin from liquorice is a tyrosinase inhibitor; FSSAI regulates nutraceuticals.`}]},"ht-standardisation":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The ash value question`,x:`If the stem asks which value detects adulteration with sand or earth, the answer is acid-insoluble ash, not total ash. Total ash includes the plant own physiological minerals and so is a blunter test.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Identity and potency are two different questions`,x:`DNA methods answer what species is this. Chromatographic and marker assays answer how much active is in it. A genuine species can still be a weak batch, so a full specification always uses both.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Recurring one-liners`,x:`Acid-insoluble ash detects sand and earth. Swelling index applies to isabgol and linseed. Foaming index applies to saponins. Schedule T is GMP for ASU drugs. RAPD and DNA barcoding establish identity, not potency.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Natural does not mean safe`,x:`Herbal products carry real risks: intrinsic toxicity (aristolochic acid nephropathy, pyrrolizidine alkaloid hepatotoxicity), heavy metal content, adulteration with undeclared synthetic drugs such as steroids or NSAIDs, and pharmacokinetic interactions with prescription medicines. Standardisation exists precisely because of these.`}]},"ic-limit-tests":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Limit test versus assay`,x:`A limit test controls an impurity and answers yes or no. An assay measures the active ingredient and gives a percentage. Questions that call a limit test quantitative are wrong; the correct description is semi-quantitative.`},{k:`trap`,t:`The acid used is not interchangeable`,x:`Nitric acid belongs to the chloride test and hydrochloric acid to the sulphate test. Using hydrochloric acid in the chloride test would add chloride, and using nitric acid in the sulphate test would not prevent the interfering precipitates the test is designed to avoid.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Mercuric chloride paper, not lead acetate paper`,x:`The stain in the arsenic limit test is produced on mercuric chloride paper. Lead acetate is present only as the moistened cotton wool that traps hydrogen sulphide. Confusing the two is the classic error in this question.`}]},"ic-official-compounds":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Aluminium constipates, magnesium moves`,x:`Aluminium gives A for arrest of the bowel, magnesium gives M for movement. Combination antacids exist to cancel these two opposite effects.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Fluoride identification`,x:`A fluoride decolorises the red zirconium-alizarin lake, turning it yellow, because fluoride takes the zirconium away as a stable complex. Fluoride also etches glass when warmed with concentrated sulphuric acid.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Ferrous with ferricyanide, ferric with ferrocyanide`,x:`The blue precipitate is given by the crossed pair: ferrous ion with potassium ferricyanide, and ferric ion with potassium ferrocyanide. Matching ferrous with ferrocyanide is the wrong pairing and the usual distractor.`}]},"ic-radiopharma":{f:[{e:`N = N0 x e^(-lambda t)`,c:`N0 = activity at time zero, N = activity at time t, lambda = decay constant with units of reciprocal time.`},{e:`t half = 0.693 / lambda`,c:`Physical half life: the time in which the activity of a radionuclide falls to half its original value. It is a constant for each nuclide and cannot be changed.`},{e:`Activity remaining = N0 / 2^n`,c:`n = number of half lives elapsed. The quickest route through any decay numerical.`},{e:`1/T effective = 1/T physical + 1/T biological`,c:`The effective half life combines physical decay with biological elimination, and is always shorter than either of the two alone.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Penetration and ionisation run opposite ways`,x:`Alpha particles ionise most but penetrate least; gamma rays penetrate most but ionise least. This is exactly why gamma emitters are used for imaging, where the radiation must leave the body, and beta emitters for therapy, where the energy must stay in the tissue.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Gamma for diagnosis, beta for therapy`,x:`A pure beta emitter such as phosphorus-32 cannot be imaged, because the radiation does not leave the body, so it can only be therapeutic. This one principle answers most questions asking whether a given nuclide is diagnostic or therapeutic.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Ten half lives is the working rule`,x:`Storage for ten half lives reduces activity by a factor of 2 to the power 10, that is 1024, which is taken as effectively complete decay. This number is asked directly in questions on radioactive waste disposal.`}]},"mc-ans-agents":{f:[{e:`Ar-O-CH2-CH(OH)-CH2-NH-R    (aryloxypropanolamine)`,c:`Ar is an aryl or heteroaryl ring, R is isopropyl or tert-butyl. Inserting the OCH2 group into the older arylethanolamine (like dichloroisoprenaline) raised potency roughly a hundredfold and gave the modern class.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Bigger N, more beta`,x:`H gives alpha activity (noradrenaline), methyl gives both (adrenaline), isopropyl gives beta (isoprenaline), tert-butyl gives beta 2 (salbutamol, terbutaline).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Atropine is racemic, hyoscyamine is not`,x:`The naturally occurring alkaloid is (S)-hyoscyamine, which racemises during extraction to give atropine. Almost all the antimuscarinic activity resides in the S isomer, so hyoscyamine is about twice as potent as the racemate.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Say the S isomer equals the deoxy compound`,x:`Any question on Easson-Stedman is answered by the statement that the less active enantiomer is equipotent with the analogue lacking the chiral hydroxyl, demonstrating three-point attachment.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Selectivity is not the same as potency`,x:`Atenolol is less lipophilic and less potent per milligram than propranolol but is beta 1 selective. Selectivity comes from the para substituent, not from lipophilicity, and it is always relative and lost at high dose.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Sign of rotation is not configuration`,x:`Both adrenaline and propranolol are laevorotatory in their active form, but adrenaline is R and propranolol is S. The change of descriptor is caused by the ether oxygen changing the Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priorities, not by any change in the spatial arrangement of the pharmacophore.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Neuromuscular blocker chemistry`,x:`Suxamethonium is two acetylcholine molecules joined tail to tail and is hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase. Non-depolarising blockers are bulky bis-quaternary compounds: the aminosteroids (pancuronium, vecuronium, rocuronium) and the benzylisoquinolines (tubocurarine, atracurium, mivacurium). Atracurium is unique in undergoing spontaneous Hofmann elimination at body pH and temperature, giving laudanosine.`}]},"mc-antibiotics":{f:[{e:`PABA + pteridine -> (dihydropteroate synthase) -> dihydropteroate -> dihydrofolate -> (dihydrofolate reductase) -> tetrahydrofolate`,c:`Sulfonamides block the first arrow (dihydropteroate synthase) by mimicking PABA. Trimethoprim blocks the second named enzyme, dihydrofolate reductase. Tetrahydrofolate is the one-carbon carrier needed for thymidylate and purine synthesis.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which ribosome subunit?`,x:`Buy AT 30, CCEL at 50. Aminoglycosides and Tetracyclines bind 30S; Chloramphenicol, Clindamycin, Erythromycin and Linezolid bind 50S.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Two definitions worth marks`,x:`Penicillin G is benzylpenicillin (acid labile, injection only) and penicillin V is phenoxymethylpenicillin (acid stable because the electron-withdrawing oxygen next to the carbonyl of the side chain prevents the side-chain amide from attacking the beta-lactam ring, so it is given orally).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Clavulanic acid is a suicide substrate`,x:`It is an oxapenam (oxygen replaces the sulfur of the penicillin thiazolidine) with no bulky C6 side chain. The enzyme opens it, but the resulting acyl-enzyme rearranges to an irreversibly bound species, so the inhibition is mechanism-based and irreversible. Combinations: clavulanic acid with amoxicillin or ticarcillin, sulbactam with ampicillin, tazobactam with piperacillin.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Chelation is not the same as inactivation by degradation`,x:`Tetracycline taken with milk or an antacid loses absorption because the chelate is not absorbed, not because the drug is chemically destroyed. The genuine chemical destruction routes are C4 epimerisation and C6 dehydration.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why co-trimoxazole is synergistic`,x:`Sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim block two consecutive steps of the same pathway, which is called sequential blockade. Each alone is bacteriostatic; the pair is bactericidal. Trimethoprim is a 2,4-diaminopyrimidine with roughly 50,000 to 100,000 times greater affinity for bacterial than for human dihydrofolate reductase, which is the source of the selectivity. The conventional ratio is 1 part trimethoprim to 5 parts sulfamethoxazole in the formulation, chosen because it gives about 1 to 20 in plasma, the optimum synergy ratio.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Two hydrolysis products with different names`,x:`Alkaline or enzymatic opening of the beta-lactam of penicillin gives penicilloic acid. Acid treatment gives penillic acid via the side-chain amide oxygen attacking the ring. Penicilloyl-protein conjugate is the major antigenic determinant responsible for penicillin allergy.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Glycopeptides and others`,x:`Vancomycin is a glycopeptide that binds the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of the peptidoglycan precursor itself, not the enzyme, so beta-lactamase is irrelevant. Resistance in VRE comes from replacing the terminal D-alanine with D-lactate, which loses one hydrogen bond of the five and drops affinity about a thousandfold. Linezolid (an oxazolidinone) binds the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit and prevents initiation complex formation, and it is a weak reversible monoamine oxidase inhibitor.`}]},"mc-anticancer":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Specific toxicities to attach to names`,x:`Cyclophosphamide gives haemorrhagic cystitis (acrolein, prevented by mesna). Doxorubicin gives cardiomyopathy. Bleomycin gives pulmonary fibrosis. Cisplatin gives nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity. Vincristine gives peripheral neuropathy. Methotrexate gives mucositis and marrow suppression, rescued by folinic acid.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The one drug that does not suppress marrow`,x:`Bleomycin and vincristine are the classic marrow-sparing cytotoxics. Bleomycin is inactivated by a hydrolase that is scarce in lung and skin, which is exactly where its toxicity appears.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Colchicine, vinca and taxol`,x:`Colchicine and the vinca alkaloids both inhibit polymerisation but at different sites (the colchicine site and the vinca site). Paclitaxel does the opposite and stabilises the polymer. Do not group paclitaxel with the vinca alkaloids just because both are spindle poisons.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Vesicant warning`,x:`Vinca alkaloids, doxorubicin and mechlorethamine are vesicants; extravasation causes tissue necrosis. Vincristine must never be given intrathecally, which is invariably fatal.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Rescue and protective agents`,x:`Folinic acid rescues methotrexate. Mesna binds acrolein and prevents haemorrhagic cystitis from cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide. Dexrazoxane protects against doxorubicin cardiotoxicity. Amifostine protects against cisplatin nephrotoxicity. Allopurinol or rasburicase prevents tumour lysis hyperuricaemia. Glucarpidase is the rescue for methotrexate toxicity in renal failure.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Antagonism between two antimetabolites`,x:`Methotrexate given shortly before 5-fluorouracil is synergistic, because raising the phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate pool increases 5-fluorouracil activation. Given in the reverse order it is antagonistic. Sequence matters in combination chemotherapy and is a favourite examination point.`}]},"mc-antivirals-antifungals":{f:[{e:`Acetyl-CoA -> HMG-CoA -> mevalonate -> squalene -> (squalene epoxidase) -> squalene 2,3-epoxide -> lanosterol -> (14-alpha-demethylase) -> ... -> ergosterol`,c:`Terbinafine and naftifine inhibit squalene epoxidase, so squalene accumulates to toxic levels inside the fungal cell, which makes allylamines fungicidal. Azoles inhibit 14-alpha-demethylase further down, so the effect is loss of ergosterol, which is mostly fungistatic. Amphotericin B acts on the finished ergosterol in the membrane.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Azoles block, polyenes poke`,x:`Azoles block the synthesis of ergosterol (an enzyme inhibitor). Polyenes poke holes by binding the ergosterol that is already there (a physical, binding action).`},{k:`exam`,t:`The single most asked antiviral fact`,x:`Acyclovir is selective because the first phosphorylation is done by the viral thymidine kinase, an enzyme that only infected cells contain. Host cell kinases then add the second and third phosphates. So the active triphosphate accumulates only inside infected cells.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why ketoconazole causes endocrine effects`,x:`Ketoconazole is an imidazole and is not selective, so it also inhibits human 17,20-lyase and 11-beta-hydroxylase, giving gynaecomastia, reduced testosterone and adrenal suppression. Fluconazole, a triazole, is far more selective for fungal CYP51 and does not do this at usual doses.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Amphotericin B is not an azole`,x:`It is a polyene macrolide from Streptomyces nodosus with a large lactone ring carrying seven conjugated double bonds on one face and seven hydroxyls on the other, plus a mycosamine sugar and a carboxyl group, so it is amphoteric and amphipathic. It binds ergosterol and forms a pore, and it does not inhibit any enzyme.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Two different fluorinated pyrimidines`,x:`Flucytosine is an antifungal that must be deaminated to 5-fluorouracil inside the fungus. 5-Fluorouracil itself is an anticancer antimetabolite. Giving flucytosine with 5-fluorouracil, or to a patient with an unusual gut flora load, raises the systemic 5-fluorouracil exposure and the marrow toxicity.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Azole drug interactions`,x:`All azoles inhibit human CYP3A4 to some degree; ketoconazole and itraconazole most, voriconazole also inhibits CYP2C9 and CYP2C19, and fluconazole is a notable CYP2C9 inhibitor (raising warfarin and phenytoin). Itraconazole and ketoconazole need gastric acid for dissolution, so proton pump inhibitors and antacids reduce their absorption, whereas fluconazole absorption is pH independent.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Zidovudine specifics`,x:`Zidovudine is cleared mainly by glucuronidation, not by CYP, and its dose-limiting toxicity is bone marrow suppression with macrocytic anaemia. Mitochondrial toxicity from inhibition of DNA polymerase gamma explains the myopathy and the lactic acidosis with hepatic steatosis seen with the older nucleoside analogues, and it is the reason stavudine and didanosine were abandoned.`}]},"mc-cns-agents":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Frequency versus duration`,x:`Benzodiazepines increase Frequency (both start with the same idea of many short events). Barbiturates increase Duration and at high dose open the channel directly, which is why they have no ceiling and are far more lethal in overdose.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Phenytoin is 5,5-diphenylhydantoin`,x:`Aryl groups at position 5 give anticonvulsant activity without hypnotic activity. Compare with barbiturates, where the same aryl group at C5 (phenobarbitone) gives both.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Two carbons or three`,x:`Promethazine and chlorpromazine differ mainly in that promethazine has a branched two-carbon chain. That single difference converts an antipsychotic into an antihistamine. This comparison appears repeatedly.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Ring geometry decides the pharmacology`,x:`A seven-membered central ring makes the tricyclic system non-planar and angular, giving antidepressant activity. A six-membered central ring, as in phenothiazines, holds the molecule nearly planar and gives neuroleptic activity.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Carbamazepine looks like a tricyclic antidepressant`,x:`Carbamazepine shares the iminostilbene tricycle with imipramine but carries a carbamoyl group instead of a basic side chain, so it is an antiepileptic and mood stabiliser, not an antidepressant. Oxcarbazepine is its 10-keto analogue and avoids epoxide formation.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Fluoxetine versus the TCAs`,x:`Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors dropped the tricycle entirely. Fluoxetine is an aryloxypropylamine with a para-trifluoromethylphenoxy group; the trifluoromethyl group and the S enantiomer confer serotonin selectivity, and the absence of the tricycle removes the muscarinic, alpha-1 and histamine H1 blockade responsible for TCA toxicity.`}]},"mc-cvs-agents":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The two that are not prodrugs`,x:`Captopril and lisinopril are active as given. Every other -pril in common use, including enalapril, ramipril, quinapril, perindopril, trandolapril and fosinopril, is an ester prodrug of the corresponding -prilat.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Vessels or ventricle`,x:`Dihydropyridines go to the Vessels; Verapamil goes to the Ventricle (and the AV node); Diltiazem does a bit of both.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Amiodarone is a chemical oddity`,x:`It is a di-iodinated benzofuran with a diethylaminoethoxy side chain. Its iodine content explains the thyroid dysfunction, its extreme lipophilicity explains a volume of distribution of about 60 to 70 litres per kilogram and a half-life of 40 to 60 days, and its class III label hides the fact that it also has class I, II and IV actions.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Verapamil stereochemistry`,x:`Verapamil is used as a racemate. The S(-) enantiomer is roughly 10 to 20 times more potent on the AV node but is also cleared far more rapidly on first pass. Given orally, proportionally more of the weak R isomer survives, so oral verapamil is less negatively dromotropic per plasma concentration than intravenous verapamil.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Digoxin target and interaction`,x:`Digoxin binds the extracellular alpha subunit of Na+/K+-ATPase. Hypokalaemia increases binding and therefore toxicity, because potassium and digoxin compete for the same site. Quinidine, verapamil and amiodarone raise digoxin levels by inhibiting P-glycoprotein.`}]},"mc-metabolism":{f:[{e:`RH + O2 + NADPH + H+  ->  ROH + H2O + NADP+`,c:`The general mono-oxygenase stoichiometry. RH is the drug, ROH the hydroxylated metabolite. One atom of molecular oxygen enters the product and the other becomes water, which is why P450s are called mixed-function oxidases.`},{e:`F(oral) = f(absorbed) x (1 - E(gut)) x (1 - E(hepatic))    with  E(hepatic) = CL(int) / (Q + CL(int))`,c:`F is oral bioavailability, E the extraction ratio, Q hepatic blood flow (about 1.5 litres per minute), CL(int) the intrinsic clearance. High extraction drugs such as propranolol, morphine, lignocaine, glyceryl trinitrate and verapamil are flow-limited; low extraction drugs such as warfarin and phenytoin are capacity-limited and are the ones sensitive to enzyme induction.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The single commonest conjugation`,x:`Glucuronidation is the most common Phase II pathway; the cofactor is uridine diphosphate glucuronic acid and the enzyme is a microsomal UDP-glucuronosyltransferase. It is the only Phase II route that is microsomal; the rest are cytosolic (except some methylation and amino acid steps in mitochondria).`},{k:`trap`,t:`Not every conjugate is more soluble`,x:`Acetylation and methylation usually make the metabolite less water soluble than the parent. Acetylated sulfonamides are the reason older sulfonamides caused crystalluria.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Inducers: CRAP GPS`,x:`Carbamazepine, Rifampicin, Alcohol (chronic), Phenytoin, Griseofulvin, Phenobarbitone, Sulfonylureas and Smoking. Inhibitors: the azoles, the macrolides except azithromycin, the HIV protease inhibitors, cimetidine, ciprofloxacin, valproate and grapefruit juice.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Benzodiazepines that skip Phase I`,x:`Oxazepam, temazepam and lorazepam already carry a 3-hydroxy group, so they go straight to glucuronidation. They are the safe choice in liver disease and in the elderly. Remember them as LOT.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Ethanol kinetics`,x:`Alcohol dehydrogenase saturates at ordinary drinking concentrations, so ethanol follows zero-order (Michaelis-Menten saturation) kinetics at about 10 millilitres per hour. Phenytoin, aspirin at high dose and theophylline show the same capacity-limited behaviour.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Enterohepatic recycling`,x:`Glucuronides above about 300 to 500 daltons are excreted in bile, hydrolysed by bacterial beta-glucuronidase in the gut, and the freed drug is reabsorbed. This prolongs the half-life of oral contraceptives, morphine, rifampicin and phenolphthalein, and explains why broad-spectrum antibiotics can reduce contraceptive efficacy.`}]},"mc-nsaids-steroids":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Alpha is behind, beta is in front`,x:`A substituent drawn with a dashed bond is alpha (below the plane of the ring); a substituent drawn with a bold wedge is beta (above the plane). The C18 and C19 methyl groups are always beta, and they define which face is which.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Aspirin is the odd NSAID`,x:`Aspirin is the only NSAID that inhibits COX irreversibly, by acetylating serine 530 of COX-1 and serine 516 of COX-2. Since a platelet has no nucleus and cannot make new enzyme, the effect lasts the whole 7 to 10 day platelet lifespan, which is why low-dose aspirin works as an antiplatelet drug.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why coxibs raise cardiovascular risk`,x:`Endothelial COX-2 makes prostacyclin, which is antiplatelet and vasodilator. Platelet thromboxane A2 comes from COX-1 and is untouched by a coxib. Removing prostacyclin while leaving thromboxane intact tips the balance towards thrombosis. Rofecoxib was withdrawn for exactly this reason.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Fluorine up, methyl down`,x:`The 9-alpha-fluoro pushes both activities up; the 16-substituent pushes the mineralocorticoid activity back down. Dexamethasone is the end product of doing both.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Ethacrynic acid is the sulfonamide-free loop diuretic`,x:`Furosemide, bumetanide, torasemide, the thiazides and acetazolamide all carry a sulfonamide group and cross-react in sulfa allergy. Ethacrynic acid is a phenoxyacetic acid with an alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone (a Michael acceptor that reacts with sulfhydryl groups), has no sulfonamide, and is therefore the choice in genuine sulfonamide allergy.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Two opposite effects on calcium`,x:`Thiazides reduce urinary calcium and are used in idiopathic hypercalciuria and recurrent calcium stones, and they help preserve bone density. Loop diuretics increase urinary calcium and are used in hypercalcaemia. Both cause hypokalaemia, hyperuricaemia and hyperglycaemia; thiazides cause hyponatraemia more often, and loop diuretics cause ototoxicity.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Spironolactone versus eplerenone`,x:`Spironolactone is a 17-spirolactone steroid and binds androgen and progesterone receptors as well as the mineralocorticoid receptor, which is why it causes gynaecomastia and menstrual irregularity. Eplerenone carries a 9,11-epoxide and a 7-carbomethoxy group in place of the 7-thioacetyl of spironolactone; that change gives far greater selectivity and almost no antiandrogenic effect, at the cost of lower potency.`}]},"mc-physchem-sar":{f:[{e:`P = C(octanol) / C(water)   and   log P = log10 P`,c:`P is the partition coefficient of the unionised drug. C(octanol) is the concentration in the octanol phase, C(water) the concentration in the aqueous phase. Log P above 0 means the drug prefers lipid; below 0 means it prefers water.`},{e:`Weak acid: pH = pKa + log ([ionised] / [unionised])`,c:`pKa is the negative log of the acid dissociation constant. For a weak base the log term inverts to log ([unionised] / [ionised]). At pH = pKa the drug is exactly 50 percent ionised.`},{e:`pi(X) = log P(R-X) - log P(R-H)`,c:`pi is the lipophilicity contribution of substituent X, obtained by subtracting the log P of the parent unsubstituted compound from that of the substituted one.`},{e:`log (Kx / Kh) = sigma . rho`,c:`Hammett equation. Kx is the equilibrium (or rate) constant for the substituted compound, Kh for the unsubstituted, sigma is the substituent electronic constant and rho is the reaction constant, which is 1 for benzoic acid ionisation in water at 25 degrees Celsius.`},{e:`log (1/C) = -k1 (log P)^2 + k2 (log P) + k3 sigma + k4 Es + k5`,c:`C is the molar concentration producing a fixed biological response, so log (1/C) is potency. k1 to k5 are regression coefficients. The negative squared term makes the plot a parabola with an optimum log P (log P0).`},{e:`log (1/C) = sum of (ai . xi) + mu`,c:`ai is the activity contribution of the substituent at position i, xi is 1 if that substituent is present and 0 if it is absent, and mu is the activity of the unsubstituted parent. No physicochemical constants are needed.`},{e:`log (1/C) = -0.24 (log P)^2 + 1.0 (log P) + 2.0 (illustrative form)`,c:`Differentiating a parabolic Hansch equation and setting the derivative to zero gives log P0 = k2 / (2 k1), the lipophilicity that maximises potency. This is the standard way an optimum log P is calculated in an exam.`},{e:`Thermodynamic activity = Ct / Cs  (or pt / ps for a vapour)`,c:`Ct is the molar concentration producing the effect, Cs is the saturation solubility of the same compound; pt is the partial pressure required and ps is the saturated vapour pressure.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Like dissolves like, like stays like`,x:`Acidic drugs stay unionised in acid (stomach, so aspirin is absorbed from the stomach). Basic drugs stay unionised in base (intestine). Ionisation traps the drug in that compartment.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Two facts that repeat`,x:`Optimum log P for oral absorption and for many general purposes is around 2; for entry into the central nervous system the commonly quoted optimum is also about 2 (roughly 1.5 to 2.7). A log P far above 5 usually means poor aqueous solubility and heavy plasma protein binding.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Craig plot versus Topliss tree`,x:`The Craig plot is a graphical selection tool used before synthesis to spread substituents across parameter space. The Topliss tree is an operational scheme applied after each analogue is tested, one compound at a time. Examiners swap the two.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Lipinski rule of five`,x:`Poor oral absorption is likely if two or more of these are violated: molecular weight above 500, log P above 5, more than 5 hydrogen bond donors, more than 10 hydrogen bond acceptors. Veber added rotatable bonds not more than 10 and polar surface area not more than 140 square angstroms.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Hammett constants are not for ortho substituents`,x:`Sigma values are tabulated only for meta and para positions because an ortho substituent adds a steric contribution to the electronic one. Taft separated the two by studying ester hydrolysis under acidic conditions (steric only, giving Es) and basic conditions (steric plus electronic, giving sigma-star).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Numbers worth memorising`,x:`sigma(para-NO2) is +0.78, sigma(para-OCH3) is -0.27, sigma(H) is 0. pi(Cl) is +0.71, pi(CH3) is +0.56, pi(OH) is -0.67, pi(H) is 0. Es(H) is 0 and Es(t-butyl) is -1.54.`}]},"mc-prodrug-design":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Palmitate for the palate, succinate for the syringe`,x:`Chloramphenicol palmitate masks taste for oral suspension; chloramphenicol sodium succinate gives solubility for intravenous use.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The single most asked pair`,x:`Prodrug is inactive and becomes active. Soft drug is active and becomes inactive. Do not confuse either with an antedrug, which is a soft drug intended to act locally and be inactivated as soon as it enters the systemic circulation.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Levodopa is the model answer`,x:`Dopamine is too polar to cross the blood-brain barrier. Levodopa is an amino acid, so it is carried across by the large neutral amino acid transporter LAT1 and then decarboxylated in the brain. Carbidopa and benserazide are peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors that do not cross the barrier, so more levodopa survives to reach the brain.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Not every ester is a prodrug`,x:`Aspirin is an ester but it is not a prodrug of salicylic acid, because aspirin itself acetylates cyclo-oxygenase, which salicylic acid cannot do. A prodrug must be essentially inactive in the form administered.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Prodrug versus active metabolite`,x:`Codeine is often called a prodrug of morphine because CYP2D6 O-demethylates it, but codeine also has weak intrinsic activity. Terfenadine is a true prodrug of fexofenadine and was withdrawn precisely because the parent, when CYP3A4 was inhibited, blocked hERG channels; fexofenadine does not. This is the classic example of the prodrug being the toxic species.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why prodrugs fail`,x:`Incomplete or variable conversion, toxic promoiety (pivalic acid, formaldehyde), first-pass loss of the released drug, species differences in the activating enzyme, and genetic polymorphism in the activating enzyme (clopidogrel needs CYP2C19; poor metabolisers get little effect).`}]},"mm-channels-plc":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The abbreviations to be sure of`,x:`PTS is price to stockist, PTR is price to retailer and MRP is the maximum retail price printed on the pack, inclusive of all taxes. The retailer margin is calculated on PTR, and no one in the chain may sell above MRP.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which stage has peak profit?`,x:`Sales peak in maturity, but profit per unit and total profit usually peak late in growth and are already declining during maturity because of price competition and promotion spend. A question asking where profits are highest is testing exactly this gap.`},{k:`exam`,t:`What gets asked from this topic`,x:`The C&F agent does not take title to the goods; maturity is the longest PLC stage and is where sales peak; test marketing comes immediately before commercialisation; detailing is the core function of a medical representative; prescription drugs cannot be advertised to the general public.`}]},"mm-concepts-mix":{f:[{e:`Break-even quantity = Fixed cost / (Selling price per unit - Variable cost per unit)`,c:`The denominator is the contribution per unit. Below this quantity the firm makes a loss; above it, every extra unit adds its contribution to profit.`},{e:`Market share (per cent) = (Company sales / Total industry sales) x 100`,c:`Sales may be counted in value or in units, and the two can give very different shares for a low-priced brand.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Need, want, demand`,x:`Read the stem carefully. Deprivation means need. A specific product named for a specific need means want. Purchasing power added means demand. This three-way distinction is asked almost every year in some form.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Seven Ps`,x:`Product, Price, Place, Promotion, People, Process, Physical evidence. The first four are goods; the last three are what you add when you are selling a service.`},{k:`exam`,t:`SWOT is half internal and half external`,x:`Strengths and Weaknesses are internal to the firm and are controllable. Opportunities and Threats are external environmental factors and are not controllable. A question that lists a competitor action or a change in law is describing an opportunity or a threat, never a strength or a weakness.`},{k:`trap`,t:`The pharmaceutical buying unit is split`,x:`For a prescription medicine the person who decides (the prescriber), the person who consumes (the patient), the person who dispenses (the pharmacist) and the person who pays (the patient, family or insurer) are usually different people. This is why classical consumer behaviour models fit prescription products so badly and why promotion is aimed at the prescriber.`}]},"nd-controlled-release":{f:[{e:`Zero order: Q = k0 t`,c:`Q = amount released at time t, k0 = zero-order release constant in mg per hour. A plot of cumulative amount released against time is a straight line, and the rate is independent of how much drug remains.`},{e:`First order: log Qr = log Q0 - k t / 2.303`,c:`Qr = amount remaining, Q0 = initial amount, k = first-order rate constant per hour. A plot of log amount remaining against time is linear; the rate falls as the tablet empties.`},{e:`Higuchi: Q = KH x square root of t`,c:`Q = amount released per unit area, KH = Higuchi dissolution constant. A plot of Q against the square root of time is linear. It applies to a non-swelling, non-eroding matrix under sink conditions where the initial drug loading greatly exceeds drug solubility.`},{e:`Korsmeyer-Peppas: Mt / M(infinity) = K t^n`,c:`Mt/M(infinity) = fraction released at time t, K = kinetic constant, n = release exponent. Fit only the first 60 percent of the release curve.`},{e:`Zero-order release rate k0r = Css x CL`,c:`k0r = required release rate in mg per hour, Css = target steady-state plasma concentration in mg per litre, CL = total body clearance in litre per hour. Maintenance dose = k0r x intended duration.`},{e:`Total dose = Di + (k0r x h);  Di corrected = Di - (k0r x Tp)`,c:`Di = loading (initial) dose, h = intended hours of release, Tp = time to peak of the immediate-release fraction. The correction avoids overshooting when the loading dose is still being absorbed as the maintenance portion begins releasing.`},{e:`f2 = 50 x log { [1 + (1/n) x sum of (Rt - Tt) squared] ^ -0.5 x 100 }`,c:`f2 = similarity factor, n = number of sampling points, Rt and Tt = percent dissolved from reference and test at each point. An f2 between 50 and 100 means the profiles are similar; the difference factor f1 should be below 15.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Sustained is not the same as controlled`,x:`Sustained release only means the effect lasts longer, and the release usually follows first-order kinetics that slows as the tablet empties. Controlled release means the rate is predetermined and generally zero-order, so the plasma concentration is held steady. Every controlled release form is sustained, but not every sustained release form is controlled.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The one-line definition to reproduce`,x:`Controlled release delivers drug at a predetermined rate, locally or systemically, for a specified period of time, so that the plasma concentration is maintained rather than allowed to fluctuate.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Absorption window drugs`,x:`Riboflavin, levodopa, ferrous salts, acyclovir, captopril and furosemide are absorbed mainly from the duodenum and upper jejunum. A conventional controlled release tablet simply carries the drug past the window and out, so these drugs need a gastroretentive system instead.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Reading n`,x:`n at the low end means the drug diffuses out of a rigid matrix; n at the high end means the polymer chains relaxing and swelling control the release, which is what gives zero order; anything in between is a mixture of the two.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Worked design numerical`,x:`A drug has CL = 6 L per hour and a target Css of 10 mg per litre, and the product must release for 12 hours. Release rate k0r = 10 x 6 = 60 mg per hour, so the maintenance dose is 60 x 12 = 720 mg. If half-life is 4 h, Kel = 0.693/4 = 0.173 per hour and Vd = CL/Kel = 6/0.173 = 34.6 L, giving a loading dose of 10 x 34.6 = 346 mg. Total about 1066 mg.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Zero order in vitro is not zero order in vivo`,x:`An osmotic pump keeps a constant release rate only while solid drug remains in the core to keep the internal solution saturated; the final 20 to 30 percent is released at a falling rate. Add to that a gastrointestinal transit of only about 8 to 12 hours, and a truly once-daily oral zero-order profile is a design compromise rather than a mathematical certainty.`}]},"nd-targeted-carriers":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which drug goes where in a liposome`,x:`Hydrophilic drug in the aqueous compartments, lipophilic drug tucked into the lipid bilayer, amphiphilic drug at the interface. Cholesterol is added not as a drug but to stiffen the bilayer and stop leakage.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Marketed products to name`,x:`Liposomal amphotericin B for systemic fungal infection, PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin for ovarian cancer and Kaposi sarcoma, and albumin-bound paclitaxel nanoparticles. These three appear again and again as examples of nanocarriers in clinical use.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why the payload must be ultra-potent`,x:`Only a small fraction of an injected antibody dose reaches the tumour, and each antibody carries only a few drug molecules. An ordinary cytotoxic would never reach a cytotoxic intracellular concentration, so ADC payloads are agents far too toxic to give on their own, such as auristatins, maytansinoids and calicheamicin.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Toxicity that follows the carrier, not the drug`,x:`PEGylated liposomal doxorubicin has much less cardiotoxicity than free doxorubicin because it stays in the circulation and avoids the myocardium, but it causes palmar-plantar erythrodysaesthesia (hand-foot syndrome) because it accumulates in skin capillaries. Changing the carrier changes the toxicity profile as much as it changes the efficacy.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Nanospheres versus nanocapsules, niosomes versus liposomes`,x:`A nanosphere is a solid matrix with drug dispersed throughout; a nanocapsule is a reservoir with an oily or aqueous core inside a polymer shell. A niosome differs from a liposome only in that the bilayer is built from non-ionic surfactants rather than phospholipids, which makes it cheaper, more chemically stable and free of the oxidation problem, but it still needs cholesterol.`}]},"nd-transdermal-mucosal":{f:[{e:`J = D K delta C / h`,c:`Fick first law applied to skin. J = steady-state flux in mg per sq cm per hour, D = diffusion coefficient in the stratum corneum, K = stratum corneum to vehicle partition coefficient, delta C = concentration difference across the barrier, h = barrier thickness. The product DK/h is the permeability coefficient.`},{e:`Lag time tL = h squared / (6 D)`,c:`tL = lag time before steady-state flux is reached, h = thickness of the stratum corneum, D = diffusion coefficient within it. This is why a patch takes several hours to reach therapeutic plasma levels and why a loading dose or an initial burst layer is sometimes built in.`},{e:`Required flux J = (Css x CL) / A`,c:`Css = target steady-state plasma concentration, CL = clearance, A = patch surface area. Rearranged, it gives the patch area needed: a drug with high clearance needs an impractically large patch, which is why only potent, low-clearance drugs succeed transdermally.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The limits of the transdermal route`,x:`Only potent drugs qualify: the daily dose must be small, roughly under 10 to 20 mg, molecular weight below about 500 Da, melting point below about 200 degrees C, and log P around 1 to 3 so the drug can both enter the lipid and leave into the aqueous dermis. The drug must not irritate or sensitise the skin.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Enhancement always costs something`,x:`The best enhancers act by disrupting the very barrier that protects the body, so potency and irritancy track each other closely. The ideal enhancer is pharmacologically inert, non-irritant, non-allergenic, cosmetically acceptable, and acts reversibly so that the barrier recovers when the patch is removed.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The rectal first-pass fact`,x:`The lower and middle haemorrhoidal veins drain into the inferior vena cava and bypass the liver, while the upper haemorrhoidal vein drains into the portal vein. A suppository placed low in the rectum therefore escapes a large part, though not all, of first-pass metabolism.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why tolerance forces a patch-free interval`,x:`Continuous nitroglycerin delivery produces nitrate tolerance within about 24 hours, so the patch is worn for 12 to 14 hours and removed for the rest of the day. A steady plasma level is an advantage only when the receptor does not down-regulate; for nitrates and for some agonists, fluctuation is therapeutically necessary.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Distinctions that are asked`,x:`Iontophoresis uses a small continuous current and moves ions; electroporation uses brief high-voltage pulses and makes pores; sonophoresis uses ultrasonic cavitation. Insulin cannot be given transdermally because its molecular weight of about 5800 Da is many times the 500 Da practical limit, which is why the inhaled and injected routes are used instead.`}]},"oc-aromatic":{f:[{e:`Resonance energy of benzene = 150 kJ/mol (about 36 kcal/mol)`,c:`The difference between the observed heat of hydrogenation of benzene (208 kJ/mol) and three times that of cyclohexene (3 x 120 = 360 kJ/mol).`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Count only the electrons in the ring system`,x:`A lone pair counts only if it sits in a p orbital that is part of the cycle. In pyrrole the nitrogen lone pair is in the ring and counts, giving 6 pi. In pyridine the lone pair is in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane and does not count; the 6 pi come from the three double bonds.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Sulfonation is reversible`,x:`Sulfonation is the only common SEAr that can be undone (dilute H2SO4 with steam removes SO3H). That makes the sulfonic acid group a usable blocking group for the para position.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Directing rule in one line`,x:`Activators go ortho-para, deactivators go meta, halogens are the exception that deactivates yet still sends you ortho-para.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Aniline in nitrating mixture`,x:`Aniline in conc H2SO4 is protonated to the anilinium ion, and NH3+ is a strongly deactivating meta director. That is why direct nitration of aniline gives a large amount of m-nitroaniline, and why acetylation to acetanilide is done first to get the para product cleanly.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Sulfonation temperature control`,x:`Naphthalene sulfonated at 80 degrees C gives the 1-isomer (kinetic, faster because the alpha position gives a better-stabilised sigma complex); at 160 degrees C it gives the 2-isomer (thermodynamic, less peri strain). This kinetic-versus-thermodynamic example is asked directly.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Antiaromatic traps`,x:`Cyclopentadienyl cation (4 pi) is antiaromatic and very unstable, whereas the anion (6 pi) is aromatic and easily formed. Tropylium cation (6 pi) is aromatic but the cycloheptatrienyl anion (8 pi) is antiaromatic. Always count electrons after the charge is applied.`}]},"oc-bonding-effects":{f:[{e:`Ka = [H+][A-] / [HA]   and   pKa = -log10 Ka`,c:`Ka is the acid dissociation constant; HA the acid, A- its conjugate base. A lower pKa means a stronger acid.`},{e:`log (K / K0) = rho x sigma`,c:`Hammett equation. K is the rate or equilibrium constant for the substituted compound, K0 for the unsubstituted one, sigma is the substituent constant (positive for withdrawing, e.g. p-NO2 = +0.78; negative for donating, e.g. p-OCH3 = -0.27) and rho is the reaction constant. Benzoic acid ionisation defines rho = 1.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Halogens do two opposite things`,x:`A halogen on benzene is -I (it withdraws through the sigma bond) but +M (it donates a lone pair by resonance). The -I wins for reactivity, so halobenzene is deactivated; the +M wins for orientation, so it is still ortho-para directing. That single sentence is a repeat GPAT question.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Acid strength ladder`,x:`Sulfonic acid > carboxylic acid > carbonic acid > phenol > water > alcohol > alkyne > ammonia > alkene > alkane. Read it as: the more the negative charge can spread, the stronger the acid.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Guanidine and amidines`,x:`Guanidine (pKa of conjugate acid about 13.6) is the strongest neutral organic base commonly examined, because the guanidinium cation is stabilised by three equivalent resonance structures — a Y-delocalised system.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Bredt rule and anti-Bredt alkenes`,x:`A double bond cannot sit at the bridgehead of a small bicyclic system, because the p orbitals cannot align. This kills carbocation and alkene formation at bridgeheads, which is why 1-bromobicyclo[2.2.1]heptane will not undergo SN1 or E1 at all.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Steric inhibition of resonance`,x:`Whenever a question shows a bulky group ortho to a resonance-active substituent, the answer nearly always invokes steric inhibition of resonance — the group is forced out of the ring plane so its +M or -M is switched off, leaving only the inductive effect.`}]},"oc-carbonyl":{f:[{e:`Khydrate = [gem-diol] / [carbonyl]`,c:`Formaldehyde is over 99 percent hydrated in water, acetaldehyde about 50 percent, acetone under 1 percent, and chloral is fully hydrated (chloral hydrate is an isolable solid) because three -I chlorines destabilise the carbonyl.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Distinguishing aldehyde from ketone`,x:`Tollens reagent (ammoniacal silver nitrate) gives a silver mirror with aldehydes only. Fehling and Benedict solutions give a red Cu2O precipitate with aliphatic aldehydes but not with aromatic ones — benzaldehyde is Tollens positive and Fehling negative, which is a favourite question.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Iodoform test`,x:`I2 with NaOH gives a yellow precipitate of CHI3 with acetaldehyde, any methyl ketone (CH3CO-), ethanol and any CH3CH(OH)- alcohol. Acetone is positive, but propanal and benzophenone are negative.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Wittig stereochemistry`,x:`Unstabilised (highly reactive) ylides give mainly the cis or Z alkene; stabilised ylides carrying an ester or ketone give mainly the trans or E alkene. The Schlosser modification gives E from an unstabilised ylide, and Horner-Wadsworth-Emmons phosphonates give E alkenes with a water-soluble by-product.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Aldol versus Claisen`,x:`Aldol keeps the C-O bond and gives an alcohol at the beta carbon; Claisen expels an alkoxide and gives a ketone. Aldol needs only catalytic base; Claisen needs a full equivalent, because the driving force is deprotonation of the acidic beta-keto ester product (pKa about 11).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Aldol variants worth knowing`,x:`Intramolecular aldol of a 1,5-diketone gives a cyclohexenone; the Robinson annulation is a Michael addition followed by intramolecular aldol condensation, and it is the standard route to steroid ring systems.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which reduction reaches which oxidation level`,x:`LiAlH4 takes an ester or acid all the way to the primary alcohol. DIBAL-H at -78 degrees C stops an ester or nitrile at the aldehyde. Rosenmund reduction (H2, Pd-BaSO4 poisoned with quinoline-S) stops an acid chloride at the aldehyde.`}]},"oc-elimination-addition":{f:[{e:`Heat of hydrogenation: ethene 137, propene 126, 2-butene (cis) 120, 2-butene (trans) 116 kJ/mol`,c:`The lower the heat released on hydrogenation, the more stable the alkene. This is the experimental proof of the substitution and trans-beats-cis stability order.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Saytzeff versus Hofmann`,x:`Saytzeff (Zaitsev) rule: the more substituted, more stable alkene is the major product — the normal outcome with small bases and halide leaving groups. Hofmann rule: the less substituted alkene wins when the base is bulky (tert-butoxide), the leaving group is bulky and positively charged (NR3+, SR2+), or the beta carbon carries an electron-withdrawing group.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Markovnikov in one line`,x:`The rich get richer — the carbon with more hydrogens gets the hydrogen.`},{k:`exam`,t:`E2 stereochemical requirement`,x:`The departing H and leaving group must be anti-periplanar, i.e. at a dihedral angle of 180 degrees. In a cyclohexane ring this means both must be axial and trans-diaxial. Neomenthyl chloride eliminates fast to the Saytzeff product, while menthyl chloride is slow and gives only the Hofmann alkene, because only one axial-axial arrangement is available.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Substitution or elimination?`,x:`Heat, a strong bulky base, a tertiary substrate and a less polar solvent all push towards elimination. A small, strongly nucleophilic but weakly basic species (CN-, I-, RS-, N3-) on a primary substrate pushes towards substitution. Alcoholic KOH eliminates; aqueous KOH substitutes.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Bredt rule again`,x:`Elimination cannot place a double bond at the bridgehead of a small bridged bicyclic system, so E1 and E2 simply fail there. Expect this as an except-type question.`}]},"oc-heterocycles":{f:[{e:`Resonance energy (kJ/mol): benzene 150, thiophene 122, pyrrole 90, furan 67`,c:`Explains the aromaticity ordering. Furan is closest to a simple diene and is the only one of the three that readily does Diels-Alder chemistry.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Two orders you must not mix up`,x:`Reactivity towards electrophiles: pyrrole > furan > thiophene > benzene > pyridine. Aromatic character (resonance energy): benzene > thiophene > pyrrole > furan. Furan is the most reactive-looking but the least aromatic.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Pyrrole is not basic — it is acidic`,x:`Pyrrole nitrogen has committed its lone pair to the aromatic sextet, so protonating it destroys aromaticity. Its conjugate acid has a pKa of about -3.8, and the N-H itself is weakly acidic (pKa about 17.5), giving a potassium salt with KOH. Pyridine, with a free lone pair, has a conjugate acid pKa of 5.2.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Basicity ordering of azoles`,x:`Imidazole (conjugate acid pKa 7.0) is more basic than pyridine (5.2), which is far more basic than thiazole (2.5), oxazole (0.8) and pyrrole (-3.8). The imidazolium cation is stabilised by two equivalent resonance forms — this is why histidine buffers at physiological pH.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Pyrrole and acid`,x:`Pyrrole polymerises in strong mineral acid to pyrrole red, so nitration and sulfonation must use mild reagents — acetyl nitrate for nitration and the pyridine-SO3 complex for sulfonation. Furan behaves similarly and undergoes Diels-Alder as a diene, which thiophene and pyrrole normally do not.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Reactions that fingerprint a ring`,x:`Pyrrole gives a red colour with a pine splint moistened with HCl. Indole gives the Ehrlich reaction (violet with p-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde) and the Van Urk reaction is used for ergot alkaloids. Pyridine gives no such colour but is recognised by its foul smell and its use as a base and catalyst.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which nitrogen gets alkylated`,x:`In a ring with both a pyrrole-type and a pyridine-type nitrogen (imidazole, purine, benzimidazole), the electrophile always goes to the pyridine-type nitrogen, because only that one has an available lone pair.`}]},"oc-name-reactions":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Two ways to strip a carbonyl to CH2`,x:`Clemmensen is acidic (Zn-Hg amalgam with conc HCl); Wolff-Kishner is basic (NH2NH2 with KOH in ethylene glycol, Huang-Minlon modification). Use Clemmensen when the molecule has base-sensitive groups and Wolff-Kishner when it has acid-sensitive groups.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Gabriel synthesis limitation`,x:`It gives only primary amines and only from primary or secondary alkyl halides — not from aryl halides (no SN2) and not from tertiary halides (elimination instead).`},{k:`trap`,t:`Hofmann has three meanings`,x:`Hofmann bromamide degradation (amide to amine), Hofmann elimination rule (less substituted alkene), and Hofmann exhaustive methylation (making the quaternary ammonium hydroxide). Read the stem carefully before answering.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Carbylamine test`,x:`A primary amine plus CHCl3 and alcoholic KOH gives an isocyanide with a foul smell. It is specific for primary amines, aliphatic or aromatic, and it also goes through dichlorocarbene.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Nobel-flagged reactions`,x:`Wittig (1979), Sharpless asymmetric epoxidation and Noyori hydrogenation (2001), Grubbs olefin metathesis (2005) and the Heck-Negishi-Suzuki palladium couplings (2010) are the ones GPAT quotes when it wants a recognition question.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Two carbon-count changes`,x:`Arndt-Eistert adds one carbon to an acid; Hofmann, Curtius and Schmidt remove one. If a stem shows a product with one fewer carbon and a nitrogen, the answer is almost always one of the three degradations.`}]},"oc-protection-reagents":{f:[{e:`Overall yield = product of individual step yields`,c:`Two extra steps of protection and deprotection at 90 percent each cost about 19 percent of the overall yield, which is why chemoselective reagents are preferred over protecting groups whenever they exist.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Strength and safety`,x:`LiAlH4 is the bulldozer — powerful, reacts violently with water, needs dry ether. NaBH4 is the scalpel — mild, safe enough to use in methanol or water.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The textbook example`,x:`To make a Grignard from a bromo ketone, first protect the ketone as its ethylene ketal, then form the Grignard, then hydrolyse the ketal back with dilute acid. Without protection the Grignard destroys its own carbonyl.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Chemoselective reduction question`,x:`To reduce a ketone while leaving an ester untouched, use NaBH4. To reduce the ester and not the alkene, use LiAlH4. To reduce the alkene and not the carbonyl, use H2 with Pd-C. To reduce a nitro group with an ester present, use H2/Pd-C or Sn/HCl, not LiAlH4.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Acetal versus thioacetal`,x:`Acetals are stable to base and destroyed by aqueous acid. Thioacetals are stable to both acid and base and need mercury(II) or Raney nickel, which is why a dithiane doubles as an acyl anion equivalent (umpolung) and as the Mozingo route to CH2.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Orthogonal protection in peptide synthesis`,x:`Merrifield solid-phase synthesis uses Boc chemistry (TFA deprotection, HF cleavage) or the now-standard Fmoc chemistry (piperidine deprotection, TFA cleavage). Side chains are protected with tert-butyl or trityl groups removed at the final TFA step.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Benzyl groups and hydrogenation`,x:`Any benzyl-based protecting group (Bn ether, Cbz carbamate, benzyl ester) comes off by catalytic hydrogenolysis. If your molecule also has an alkene or a nitro group, hydrogenation will reduce those too, so a different protecting group must be chosen.`}]},"oc-rearrangements":{f:[{e:`Semipinacol: 1,2-amino alcohol + HNO2 gives ring-contracted or ring-expanded ketone`,c:`The Tiffeneau-Demjanov variant: 1-aminomethylcyclohexanol with nitrous acid expands the ring to cycloheptanone. The diazonium is the leaving group.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which way does the group jump?`,x:`Electrons flow towards the electron-poor atom, so the group with the most available electrons migrates first and it always takes its bonding pair with it.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Beckmann is the most asked of all`,x:`Cyclohexanone oxime with concentrated H2SO4 gives caprolactam, the monomer of nylon-6. That single conversion has appeared repeatedly.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Beckmann is anti-specific`,x:`It is the group anti (trans) to the oxime hydroxyl that migrates, so the geometry of the oxime fixes the amide you get. Acetophenone oxime whose phenyl is anti to OH gives acetanilide (PhNHCOCH3); the syn isomer would give N-methylbenzamide.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Claisen rearrangement is not the Claisen condensation`,x:`The Claisen condensation joins two esters with sodium ethoxide to give a beta-keto ester. The Claisen rearrangement is a thermal [3,3] sigmatropic shift of an allyl vinyl or allyl aryl ether. Same man, different reaction.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Beckmann versus Schmidt on a ketone`,x:`Both convert a ketone to an amide. Beckmann needs the oxime made first; the Schmidt reaction with hydrazoic acid does it in one pot. On a carboxylic acid, Schmidt gives an amine instead.`}]},"oc-stereochem":{f:[{e:`Number of stereoisomers = 2^n  (n = number of stereocentres, no symmetry)`,c:`n is the count of stereocentres. If an internal plane of symmetry is possible the real number is lower, because meso forms collapse pairs into one compound.`},{e:`[alpha] = alpha(observed) / (l x c)`,c:`Specific rotation. alpha(observed) in degrees, l is path length in decimetres, c is concentration in g/mL for solutions (or density for a neat liquid). Quoted as [alpha] at 20 degrees C with the sodium D line, 589 nm.`},{e:`Enantiomeric excess (percent) = (observed rotation / rotation of pure enantiomer) x 100`,c:`Also equals percent of major enantiomer minus percent of minor. A sample with 80 percent ee contains 90 percent of one enantiomer and 10 percent of the other.`},{e:`Optical purity (percent) = 100 x [alpha](sample) / [alpha](pure enantiomer)`,c:`Numerically equal to enantiomeric excess for a two-component mixture. If a mixture shows +10 degrees and the pure (+) form shows +50 degrees, optical purity is 20 percent, i.e. 60 percent (+) and 40 percent (-).`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`D/L is not d/l and neither is R/S`,x:`Capital D and L are configurational labels tied to glyceraldehyde, used for sugars and amino acids. Small d and l (or + and -) describe the measured direction of rotation. R and S come from the CIP rules and describe the actual 3D arrangement. A D sugar can be laevorotatory: D-fructose is D but (-).`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`If the lowest group points at you`,x:`Read the rotation as normal, then reverse the answer. Clockwise seen with H towards you actually means S.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Resolution methods`,x:`Mechanical picking, formation of diastereomeric salts with a chiral acid or base (brucine, quinine, tartaric acid, cinchonidine), biochemical or enzymatic resolution, and chiral chromatography. Pasteur used mechanical separation of sodium ammonium tartrate crystals in 1848.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Optically inactive is not the same as achiral`,x:`A racemate is made of chiral molecules but shows no rotation (external compensation). A meso compound shows no rotation because the molecule itself is achiral (internal compensation). Only the racemate can be resolved into active components.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Drug stereochemistry facts asked repeatedly`,x:`Levodopa is the L (S) isomer and only it is active. (S)-thalidomide is teratogenic while (R) is sedative, but they interconvert in vivo. Esomeprazole is the S enantiomer of omeprazole, levocetirizine the R of cetirizine, dexketoprofen the S of ketoprofen, and levosalbutamol the R of salbutamol. Ethambutol is used as the (S,S) isomer; the (R,R) causes blindness.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Counting stereoisomers with symmetry`,x:`Tartaric acid has two stereocentres, so 2^2 = 4 is predicted, but only three exist: (+), (-) and one meso form. Whenever the two halves of a molecule are constitutionally identical, check for a meso form before answering 2^n.`}]},"oc-substitution":{f:[{e:`log (k / k0) = s x n   (Swain-Scott)`,c:`k is the rate with the nucleophile, k0 with water, n is the nucleophilicity constant (water = 0, CN- about 5.1, I- about 5.0) and s is the substrate sensitivity (1.0 for methyl bromide).`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Remember the numbers`,x:`The 1 and 2 are molecularities, not step counts. SN2 has two species in the rate-determining step; SN1 has one. SN1 has the extra step even though it carries the smaller number.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The classic one-liner`,x:`SN2 gives complete inversion; SN1 gives racemisation with a slight excess of inversion, because the leaving group shields one face of the ion pair before it fully separates.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Neopentyl halide`,x:`Neopentyl bromide is a primary halide but reacts extremely slowly by SN2 because the adjacent quaternary carbon blocks backside attack. Under SN1 conditions it rearranges by a methyl shift to the tert-amyl cation. It is the standard trick question for the primary-equals-SN2 rule.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Ion-pair theory`,x:`Winstein explained partial inversion in SN1 with intimate ion pairs, solvent-separated ion pairs and free ions. Attack at the intimate ion-pair stage is blocked on the leaving-group face, so inversion always slightly exceeds retention.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Ambident nucleophiles`,x:`Cyanide gives nitrile with KCN (attack through carbon, the softer end) but isonitrile with AgCN. Nitrite gives nitroalkane with AgNO2 and alkyl nitrite with KNO2. Silver salts favour the harder atom because they push the reaction towards SN1 character.`}]},"pa-acidbase":{f:[{e:`N1 V1 = N2 V2`,c:`N = normality, V = volume in mL. Valid only when both are expressed as normality (equivalents), because normality already carries the stoichiometry.`},{e:`Normality = Molarity x n-factor   |   Equivalent weight = Molecular weight / n-factor`,c:`n-factor is the number of replaceable H+ for an acid, replaceable OH- for a base, or electrons exchanged in a redox reaction.`},{e:`pH = pKIn + log([In-]/[HIn])`,c:`Henderson-Hasselbalch for an indicator. pKIn = indicator dissociation exponent, HIn = acid colour form, In- = base colour form.`},{e:`At half-neutralisation of a weak acid, pH = pKa`,c:`Half the acid is converted to salt, so [salt] = [acid] and the log term vanishes. This is the standard way of measuring pKa by titration.`},{e:`pH at equivalence (weak acid + strong base) = 7 + 0.5 pKa + 0.5 log C`,c:`C = concentration of the salt formed. The salt of a weak acid hydrolyses to give an alkaline solution.`},{e:`Buffer capacity beta = dB / d(pH)   |   Maximum when pH = pKa`,c:`dB = gram equivalents of strong base or acid added per litre, d(pH) = resulting pH change. Useful buffer range is pKa +/- 1.`},{e:`Van Slyke: beta = 2.303 C Ka [H3O+] / (Ka + [H3O+])^2`,c:`C = total buffer concentration. At pH = pKa this reduces to beta = 0.576 C.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Equivalence point versus end point`,x:`The equivalence point is a calculated fact; the end point is an experimental observation. The gap between them is the titration error. A good indicator makes that gap negligible, it never abolishes it.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Read the definition of an indicator`,x:`An acid-base indicator is itself a weak acid or weak base whose ionised and unionised forms have different colours. That definition, not the colour list, is what most one-mark questions target.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`PWA and MOWB`,x:`Phenolphthalein for Weak Acid, Methyl Orange for Weak Base. The indicator goes with the weak partner because the salt hydrolyses towards that partner.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Double indicator method`,x:`For a carbonate-hydroxide or carbonate-bicarbonate mixture, phenolphthalein volume (P) covers all the hydroxide plus half the carbonate; the further methyl orange volume (M) covers the rest. If P > M there is NaOH + Na2CO3; if P < M there is Na2CO3 + NaHCO3; if P = M it is pure carbonate.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Boric acid receiver needs no standardisation`,x:`Boric acid is too weak to be titrated itself, so an inexact excess is fine; only the ammonium borate formed is titrated with standard acid using methyl red. That is why the boric acid modification is preferred.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why the curve flattens`,x:`The steep jump narrows as the acid gets weaker AND as the solution gets more dilute. Both a low Ka and a low concentration can destroy the end point, which is exactly why non-aqueous titration exists.`}]},"pa-chromatography":{f:[{e:`Rf = distance travelled by solute / distance travelled by solvent front`,c:`Rf is always between 0 and 1 and has no units. It is measured from the origin to the centre of the spot.`},{e:`N = 16 (tR / W)^2 = 5.54 (tR / W1/2)^2`,c:`N = number of theoretical plates, tR = retention time, W = baseline width from tangents, W1/2 = width at half height. Bigger N means a more efficient column.`},{e:`HETP (H) = L / N`,c:`H = height equivalent to a theoretical plate, L = column length in mm or cm. Smaller H means better efficiency.`},{e:`k' = (tR - tM) / tM`,c:`k' = capacity (retention) factor, tR = retention time of analyte, tM = void or dead time of an unretained peak. Ideal k' is 1 to 10; below 1 the peak sits near the void volume.`},{e:`alpha = k'2 / k'1 = (tR2 - tM) / (tR1 - tM)`,c:`alpha = selectivity or separation factor, always taken as greater than 1. alpha = 1 means no separation at all.`},{e:`Rs = 2 (tR2 - tR1) / (W1 + W2)`,c:`Rs = resolution. Rs of 1.5 is baseline resolution; Rs of 1.0 gives about 2 percent overlap. Pharmacopoeial system suitability usually demands Rs of at least 2.`},{e:`van Deemter: H = A + B/u + C u`,c:`H = plate height, u = linear velocity of mobile phase. A = eddy diffusion (multipath), B = longitudinal diffusion, C = resistance to mass transfer. There is an optimum u giving minimum H.`},{e:`Rs = (sqrt(N)/4) x ((alpha - 1)/alpha) x (k'/(1 + k'))`,c:`The master resolution equation: efficiency term, selectivity term and retention term. Quadrupling N only doubles Rs, so changing selectivity is the more powerful lever.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Paper chromatography is partition, not adsorption`,x:`The stationary phase in paper chromatography is the water held by the cellulose, not the cellulose itself, so the mechanism is partition. This is asked almost every year.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Rf and polarity on silica`,x:`Silica is polar, so a polar compound sticks and has a low Rf; a non-polar compound runs with the solvent and has a high Rf. On a reversed-phase plate the order is inverted.`},{k:`exam`,t:`RI detector and gradients do not mix`,x:`A refractive index detector responds to the bulk mobile phase, so any gradient shifts the baseline. It is also temperature sensitive. This is the standard reason given for choosing isocratic conditions with RI.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`FID for organics, ECD for halogens, TCD for everything`,x:`FID cannot see water, carbon dioxide or the permanent gases because they give no CH fragments in the flame; TCD can see them all.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Largest first in gel filtration`,x:`Size exclusion is the one mode where the biggest molecule elutes first, because it is excluded from the pores and therefore travels the shortest path. Every other mode retains by interaction strength.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Do not confuse HPLC pressure with column efficiency`,x:`Back pressure rises as particle size falls (roughly with the inverse square of diameter), which is why UPLC with sub-2-micrometre particles needs a system rated to 1000 bar. The gain is efficiency, not pressure for its own sake.`}]},"pa-complexo-precip":{f:[{e:`K'MY = KMY x alpha4`,c:`K'MY = conditional (effective) stability constant, KMY = absolute stability constant, alpha4 = fraction of EDTA present as the fully deprotonated Y4- form at that pH.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`EDTA is hexadentate and reacts 1:1`,x:`Ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid offers two nitrogen and four carboxylate oxygen donors, so it forms a 1:1 complex with almost every metal ion whatever the metal charge. The 1:1 stoichiometry is what makes the calculation easy and is asked constantly.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which colour is the end point`,x:`With eriochrome black T the solution is wine red while metal is present and turns blue at the end point when EDTA has taken the last metal ion. Students frequently quote the reverse.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why each method has its pH limit`,x:`Mohr fails below pH 6.5 because chromate becomes dichromate, and above pH 10 because silver oxide precipitates. Volhard must be acidic to stop ferric hydroxide forming. Fajans uses fluorescein, which needs enough alkalinity to be ionised, so strongly acid solution kills it.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Mohr Chromate, Volhard Ferric, Fajans Fluorescein`,x:`M-C, V-F(erric), F-F(luorescein). Volhard alone is a back titration and alone works in acid.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Nitrobenzene is only for chloride`,x:`AgBr and AgI are less soluble than AgSCN, so bromide and iodide need no coating step. Only chloride does. For iodide, add the ferric indicator after the silver, or iodide is oxidised to iodine.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Mercurimetry and mercuric acetate`,x:`Chloride can also be titrated with mercuric nitrate using diphenylcarbazone, forming un-ionised HgCl2. Mercuric acetate is separately used in non-aqueous titration to release the base from a halide salt.`}]},"pa-electro":{f:[{e:`E cell = E indicator - E reference   and   E = E0 + (0.0592/n) log [Mn+] at 25 C`,c:`A tenfold change in a monovalent ion changes the potential by 59.2 mV; for a divalent ion it is 29.6 mV.`},{e:`Specific conductance k = (1/R) x (l/a)   and   Equivalent conductance = 1000 k / C`,c:`R = resistance, l/a = cell constant in per cm, C = concentration in equivalents per litre. Specific conductance is in siemens per cm.`},{e:`id = 607 n D^(1/2) m^(2/3) t^(1/6) C`,c:`Ilkovic equation. id = average diffusion current in microampere, n = electrons, D = diffusion coefficient in cm2/s, m = mercury flow rate in mg/s, t = drop time in s, C = concentration in millimole per litre.`},{e:`w = (Q x M) / (n x F)   with   Q = i x t`,c:`w = mass deposited or reacted in g, Q = charge in coulombs, i = current in A, t = time in s, M = molar mass, n = electrons, F = 96485 C per mole of electrons.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`What makes a good reference electrode`,x:`Constant and reproducible potential, insensitive to the composition of the test solution, and unaffected by small currents (non-polarisable). SCE and Ag/AgCl satisfy all three; the glass electrode does not, because it is meant to respond.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`TGA weighs, DTA compares, DSC counts calories`,x:`TGA = mass. DTA = delta temperature. DSC = delta heat, and only DSC gives a quantitative enthalpy in joules per gram.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why conductometry wins`,x:`It works with coloured, turbid and very dilute solutions, with very weak acids, and with mixtures of a strong and a weak acid, because the end point comes from the intersection of two straight lines rather than from a single reading at the equivalence point.`},{k:`trap`,t:`id gives how much, E1/2 gives what`,x:`Diffusion current is the quantitative term (proportional to concentration); half-wave potential is the qualitative term (independent of concentration). Swapping these is a standard distractor.`},{k:`exam`,t:`DSC versus DTA in one sentence`,x:`DTA records the temperature difference between sample and reference and is only semi-quantitative; DSC records the energy required to hold them at the same temperature, so the peak area is directly the enthalpy of the transition.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Glass transition is a step, not a peak`,x:`Tg appears as a shift in the baseline (a change in heat capacity), not as a peak, because no latent heat is absorbed. Melting gives a peak; Tg gives a step. Amorphous solid dispersions are characterised by exactly this feature.`}]},"pa-errors":{f:[{e:`Absolute error E = xi - xt   |   Relative error = (xi - xt) / xt x 100 percent`,c:`xi = measured value, xt = true value. Relative error is often quoted in parts per thousand (ppt) by multiplying by 1000 instead of 100.`},{e:`s = sqrt( sum(xi - xbar)^2 / (n - 1) )`,c:`Sample standard deviation. xi = each result, xbar = mean, n = number of replicates, (n-1) = degrees of freedom.`},{e:`RSD or CV = (s / xbar) x 100 percent`,c:`Relative standard deviation, also called coefficient of variation. Dimensionless, so it lets you compare precision across methods and units.`},{e:`Standard error of mean SEM = s / sqrt(n)`,c:`s = sample SD, n = number of replicates. SEM shrinks with the square root of n, which is why quadrupling replicates only halves the uncertainty of the mean.`},{e:`CI = xbar +/- (t x s) / sqrt(n)`,c:`t = Student t value for (n-1) degrees of freedom at the chosen confidence level, s = sample SD, n = replicates. Use z instead of t only when sigma is known.`},{e:`Q = |suspect value - nearest neighbour| / range`,c:`Dixon Q test. Reject the suspect value if calculated Q exceeds the tabulated Q at the stated confidence for that n.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Precise is not accurate`,x:`A burette with a wrongly etched scale gives four readings that agree to 0.01 mL and are all wrong by 0.5 mL. That is high precision with poor accuracy. Good accuracy without precision is possible too, when large random errors happen to cancel.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Constant versus proportional`,x:`A constant determinate error (say 0.5 mg lost to solubility in a wash) hurts a small sample far more than a large one, so its relative error falls as sample size rises. A proportional error keeps the same relative size at every sample weight.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Round only at the end`,x:`Carry one extra digit through intermediate steps and round once at the final answer. Rounding at every step accumulates a determinate rounding bias. Use the round-to-even rule for a trailing 5: 2.35 becomes 2.4 but 2.45 becomes 2.4.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Weighing by difference`,x:`Two weighings each with SD 0.1 mg give a net weight with SD sqrt(0.1^2 + 0.1^2) = 0.14 mg, not 0.2 mg. The quadrature rule is a favourite one-line numerical.`},{k:`exam`,t:`LOD and LOQ ratios`,x:`The signal-to-noise convention is 3:1 for LOD and 10:1 for LOQ; the calibration-curve convention is 3.3 sigma/S and 10 sigma/S. LOQ is roughly three times LOD. Both forms are examined.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`RAP for random error`,x:`Random errors are Reduced by Averaging and are Positive-and-negative. Determinate errors are Directional and Detectable by a Different method.`}]},"pa-ir-raman":{f:[{e:`Wavenumber (cm-1) = 10000 / wavelength (micrometre)`,c:`IR is expressed in wavenumbers because wavenumber is directly proportional to energy. 1600 cm-1 equals 6.25 micrometre.`},{e:`Degrees of vibrational freedom: 3N - 6 (non-linear), 3N - 5 (linear)`,c:`N = number of atoms. Water (N = 3, bent) has 3 modes; carbon dioxide (N = 3, linear) has 4.`},{e:`nu (cm-1) = (1 / 2 pi c) x sqrt(k / mu)   with   mu = m1 m2 / (m1 + m2)`,c:`Hooke law. k = force constant of the bond, mu = reduced mass, c = velocity of light. Stronger bonds and lighter atoms vibrate faster.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The fingerprint region`,x:`Below 1500 cm-1 the bands come from skeletal bending and coupled vibrations of the whole molecule, so the pattern is unique to that compound. Two spectra matching there prove identity; this is the basis of pharmacopoeial IR identification.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Amide I and amide II`,x:`The amide I band near 1650 cm-1 is mostly C=O stretch; the amide II band near 1550 cm-1 is N-H bend coupled with C-N stretch. Their positions report on protein secondary structure and are asked in biotech-flavoured papers.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Never use glass or quartz optics in IR`,x:`Cells and windows are made of sodium chloride, potassium bromide, caesium iodide or zinc selenide because glass and quartz absorb infrared. Sources are the Nernst glower, globar and nichrome wire; detectors are thermocouple, bolometer, Golay cell and DTGS or MCT in FTIR.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Rule of mutual exclusion`,x:`In a molecule with a centre of symmetry, a vibration that is IR active is Raman inactive and vice versa. Carbon dioxide is the standard illustration: the symmetric stretch is Raman active only.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Overtones, combination bands and Fermi resonance`,x:`Extra bands appear at roughly twice a fundamental (overtone) or at the sum of two fundamentals (combination). When an overtone happens to coincide with a fundamental of the same symmetry they mix and split, which is Fermi resonance, the reason the aldehyde C-H appears as a doublet at 2720 and 2820 cm-1.`}]},"pa-nmr-ms":{f:[{e:`nu = gamma B0 / 2 pi`,c:`Larmor equation. nu = resonance frequency, gamma = magnetogyric ratio of the nucleus, B0 = applied magnetic field. A stronger magnet means a higher operating frequency: 300 MHz for protons at 7.05 tesla.`},{e:`delta (ppm) = (nu sample - nu TMS) x 10^6 / operating frequency in Hz`,c:`delta is expressed in parts per million so it is independent of the magnet strength. TMS is the zero reference.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Shielding and deshielding`,x:`Electron density around a proton shields it, so it resonates upfield at low delta. An electronegative atom or an anisotropic ring current pulls electrons away and deshields the proton, moving it downfield to high delta.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Three things a proton spectrum tells you`,x:`Number of signals = how many kinds of proton. Position (delta) = what chemical environment. Area (integration) = how many protons of each kind. Splitting = how many neighbours.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Integration gives a ratio, not a count`,x:`The integral trace gives relative numbers of protons. Ratios of 2:3 could equally be 4:6 or 6:9. You need the molecular formula to convert the ratio into an absolute count.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The nitrogen rule`,x:`A compound with an even-numbered molecular ion contains zero or an even number of nitrogen atoms; an odd molecular ion means an odd number of nitrogens. Caffeine at m/z 194 has four nitrogens; nicotine at m/z 162 has two.`},{k:`trap`,t:`The nitrogen rule applies to the molecular ion only`,x:`Fragments follow the opposite parity because they are usually even-electron ions. An odd-mass fragment from a nitrogen-free compound is normal; do not apply the rule to fragments.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Which technique answers which question`,x:`UV shows conjugation, IR shows functional groups, MS gives molecular weight and formula, proton NMR gives the hydrogen framework, carbon NMR gives the carbon count. GPAT frequently asks which single technique best answers a stated question.`}]},"pa-nonaqueous":{f:[{e:`HClO4 + CH3COOH -> CH3COOH2+ + ClO4-`,c:`Perchloric acid protonates the solvent to give the acetonium (acetous) ion CH3COOH2+, which is the strongest acid that can exist in glacial acetic acid.`},{e:`CH3COOH2+ + B -> BH+ + CH3COOH`,c:`B = the weak basic drug. The acetonium ion is a far stronger proton donor than H3O+, so it protonates bases that water cannot.`},{e:`2 BH+Cl- + Hg(OAc)2 -> 2 B.HOAc + HgCl2`,c:`BH+Cl- = hydrochloride salt of the drug. Mercuric acetate replaces the strong halide anion with the weakly ionised acetate, so the freed base can be titrated.`},{e:`Autoprotolysis: 2 CH3COOH <-> CH3COOH2+ + CH3COO-,  Ks = 3.5 x 10^-15`,c:`Ks = autoprotolysis constant. The smaller the Ks, the wider the usable acidity range of the solvent and the sharper the potential break.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`One-line reason for non-aqueous titration`,x:`Weak acids and weak bases that cannot be titrated in water become titratable because an aprotic or differentiating solvent removes the levelling effect and raises the apparent strength of the analyte.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Acidic solvent for basic drug`,x:`Opposites attract: an acidic solvent (glacial acetic acid) sharpens a basic drug; a basic solvent (DMF, pyridine) sharpens an acidic drug.`},{k:`trap`,t:`KHP is an acid in water and a base in acetic acid`,x:`In water potassium hydrogen phthalate donates a proton and standardises NaOH. In glacial acetic acid the phthalate accepts a proton, so the very same substance standardises perchloric acid. This role reversal is a classic question.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Blank correction is mandatory`,x:`Glacial acetic acid has a high coefficient of expansion, so the titre must be corrected for temperature difference between standardisation and assay, and a blank titration of the solvent is always run.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Carbon dioxide ruins the alkaline titration`,x:`Titrations with methoxide must be done under nitrogen, because dissolved carbon dioxide behaves as an acid in the basic solvent and is titrated along with the drug.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Potentiometric end point in non-aqueous media`,x:`A glass indicator electrode with a modified calomel reference containing lithium perchlorate in acetic acid (instead of aqueous KCl) is used, because aqueous KCl would leak water and potassium perchlorate would clog the junction.`}]},"pa-redox":{f:[{e:`I2 + 2 Na2S2O3 -> 2 NaI + Na2S4O6`,c:`Thiosulphate is oxidised to tetrathionate. One mole of iodine consumes two moles of thiosulphate, so the thiosulphate n-factor is 1.`},{e:`MnO4- + 8 H+ + 5 e- -> Mn2+ + 4 H2O   (E0 = +1.51 V)`,c:`Acid medium, five-electron change. In neutral or alkaline medium permanganate goes only to MnO2, a three-electron change (equivalent weight 158/3 = 52.7).`},{e:`E = E0 - (0.0592/n) log([reduced]/[oxidised])   at 25 degrees C`,c:`E = observed potential, E0 = standard potential, n = electrons transferred. The Nernst equation gives the shape of the redox titration curve.`},{e:`E at equivalence = (n1 E1 + n2 E2) / (n1 + n2)`,c:`Weighted mean of the two standard potentials when no H+ appears in either half reaction. n1, n2 = electron changes of the two couples.`},{e:`I2 + SO2 + 2 H2O -> 2 HI + H2SO4`,c:`The water-consuming reaction. The classical reagent is iodine, sulphur dioxide, pyridine and methanol; modern reagents replace pyridine with imidazole.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`OIL RIG and LEO the lion says GER`,x:`Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain of electrons. Loss of Electrons is Oxidation, Gain of Electrons is Reduction.`},{k:`exam`,t:`KMnO4 is self-indicating`,x:`One drop of excess permanganate colours the solution pale pink, so no external indicator is needed. This makes permanganometry the classic example of a self-indicating titration.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which way does the blue go`,x:`Iodimetry ends colourless to blue because the first excess of titrant iodine hits the starch. Iodometry ends blue to colourless because thiosulphate consumes the liberated iodine. Getting this backwards is the single commonest error in the topic.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Cerimetry advantages`,x:`Ce(IV) solutions are stable indefinitely, can be boiled, are usable in hydrochloric acid, and involve a simple one-electron change with no intermediate oxidation states, so no side products form. These four advantages are the standard answer.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Diazotisation details`,x:`Sodium nitrite titration of a primary aromatic amine (sulphanilamide, procaine) is done in hydrochloric acid below 5 degrees C with potassium bromide as catalyst; the end point is starch-iodide paper turning blue, or an electrometric dead-stop end point.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Thiosulphate is unstable`,x:`Sodium thiosulphate solution decomposes by acid, by carbon dioxide and by Thiobacillus bacteria, so it is prepared with freshly boiled cooled water, a little sodium carbonate, and restandardised against potassium dichromate or potassium iodate periodically.`}]},"pa-uvvis":{f:[{e:`A = log10 (I0 / I) = 2 - log10 (percent T)`,c:`A = absorbance, I0 = incident intensity, I = transmitted intensity, T = transmittance. 100 percent T is A = 0; 10 percent T is A = 1; 1 percent T is A = 2.`},{e:`A = a b c   |   A = epsilon b c`,c:`a = absorptivity, b = path length in cm, c = concentration. When c is mol/L the constant is epsilon, the molar absorptivity, in L/mol/cm.`},{e:`A(1 percent, 1 cm) = A / (c in g per 100 mL x b in cm)`,c:`Specific absorbance: the absorbance of a 1 percent w/v solution in a 1 cm cell. This is the value tabulated in the IP and BP.`},{e:`epsilon = A(1 percent, 1 cm) x molecular weight / 10`,c:`Converts specific absorbance to molar absorptivity. Remember the factor of 10, not 100.`},{e:`A1 = ax1 b Cx + ay1 b Cy   and   A2 = ax2 b Cx + ay2 b Cy`,c:`Simultaneous equation (Vierordt) method. A1 and A2 are total absorbances at two wavelengths, ax and ay the absorptivities of the two drugs at those wavelengths, Cx and Cy the unknown concentrations.`},{e:`Q-absorbance ratio: Cx = [(Qm - Qy) / (Qx - Qy)] x A(iso) / ax(iso)`,c:`Q = A at lambda max divided by A at the isoabsorptive point. The isoabsorptive (isosbestic) point is the wavelength where both components have equal absorptivity.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The four shift terms`,x:`Bathochromic = red shift to longer wavelength. Hypsochromic = blue shift to shorter wavelength. Hyperchromic = increased intensity. Hypochromic = decreased intensity. Almost every UV question set contains one of these.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`BAtho goes BIG, HYPSo goes SMALL`,x:`Bathochromic - Bigger wavelength. Hypsochromic - Higher energy, shorter wavelength. Hyper - more absorbance; Hypo - less.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Working absorbance range`,x:`Photometric error is minimum near A = 0.4343 (about 36.8 percent T). Practically, absorbance is kept between 0.2 and 0.8, and dilutions are designed to land there. Outside that range the relative concentration error rises sharply.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Glass cuvettes do not work in UV`,x:`Glass absorbs strongly below about 340 nm, so quartz (silica) cells are mandatory for UV. A question that offers glass for a 254 nm measurement is testing exactly this.`},{k:`exam`,t:`pH shifts of phenol and aniline`,x:`Phenol at 270 nm shifts bathochromically to 287 nm in alkali because phenoxide has an extra lone pair for conjugation. Aniline at 280 nm shifts hypsochromically to 254 nm in acid because the anilinium lone pair is protonated and lost to the ring.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Isosbestic point proves a clean interconversion`,x:`A sharp isosbestic point in a series of spectra means exactly two absorbing species are interconverting with no intermediate. It is evidence of a clean two-species equilibrium, not merely a crossing of lines.`}]},"pe-drying-humidity":{f:[{e:`RH = (p / ps) x 100`,c:`p = partial pressure of water vapour in the air, ps = saturation vapour pressure of water at the same dry bulb temperature.`},{e:`Percentage humidity = (H / Hs) x 100`,c:`H = actual absolute humidity, Hs = saturation humidity at the same temperature.`},{e:`H = 0.622 x p / (P - p)`,c:`Absolute humidity of moist air in kg water per kg dry air. 0.622 = 18/29, the ratio of the molar masses of water and air. P = total pressure, p = partial pressure of water vapour, in the same units.`},{e:`Humid heat cs = 1.005 + 1.88 H`,c:`kJ per kg dry air per kelvin. 1.005 is the specific heat of dry air and 1.88 that of water vapour, both in kJ/kg K.`},{e:`Rc = h (T - Tw) / lambda_w`,c:`Constant drying rate per unit area. h = heat transfer coefficient, T = dry bulb temperature, Tw = wet bulb temperature, lambda_w = latent heat of vaporisation at Tw.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Free and unbound are not the same thing`,x:`Free moisture is defined against the equilibrium moisture content, so it is a removable quantity. Bound and unbound are defined against the vapour pressure of pure water, so they describe how the water is held. Some free moisture can still be bound moisture.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Wet bulb temperature in the constant rate period`,x:`During the constant rate period the wet surface stays at the wet bulb temperature of the drying air, because all the heat arriving is used as latent heat of vaporisation. This is asked directly and also as the reason spray drying is safe for thermolabile drugs.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Match the dryer to the feed`,x:`Liquid feed to dry powder in one step means spray drying. Slurry on a heated roll means drum drying. Wet granules before compression means fluidised bed. A sterile biological in a vial means freeze drying. Most dryer questions are simply this match.`}]},"pe-filtration-mixing":{f:[{e:`dV/dt = K A delta P / (mu L)`,c:`Darcy equation. dV/dt = rate of filtration (m3/s), K = permeability of the cake, A = filtration area (m2), delta P = pressure drop across the cake (Pa), mu = viscosity of the filtrate (Pa s), L = thickness of the cake (m).`},{e:`dV/dt = A delta P / (mu (Rc + Rm))`,c:`The Darcy equation written with resistances. Rc = resistance of the cake, which grows as filtration proceeds, Rm = resistance of the filter medium, which is constant.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Clarification is not cake filtration`,x:`Clarification removes a very small amount of solid from a large volume of liquid and the liquid is the product. Cake filtration handles a thick slurry and the solid may be the product. The equipment chosen is completely different, so read what the question calls the product.`},{k:`exam`,t:`0.22 micrometre means sterilising grade`,x:`A question that mentions filtration of a heat-labile injection, or the sterility test, is asking about the membrane filter of 0.22 micrometre nominal pore size. This is one of the most repeated single facts in the whole engineering paper.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Match the mixer to the consistency`,x:`Free-flowing powder means a tumbling blender such as the double cone or V blender. Cohesive powder or a wet mass means a ribbon or planetary mixer. Ointment means a sigma blade mixer or a triple roller mill. Emulsion means a colloid mill or homogeniser.`}]},"pe-fluid-flow":{f:[{e:`Re = D v rho / mu`,c:`D = internal pipe diameter (m), v = average linear velocity (m/s), rho = density (kg/m3), mu = viscosity (Pa s). Re is dimensionless.`},{e:`P/(rho g) + v^2/(2g) + Z = constant`,c:`P/(rho g) = pressure head, v^2/(2g) = velocity (kinetic) head, Z = potential (static) head above the datum, g = 9.81 m/s2.`},{e:`A1 v1 = A2 v2 = Q`,c:`A = cross-sectional area (m2), v = average velocity (m/s), Q = volumetric flow rate (m3/s). It is a statement of conservation of mass for an incompressible fluid.`},{e:`Q = Cd x A0 x sqrt(2 g H)  (approximate form when the approach velocity is negligible)`,c:`Cd = coefficient of discharge, A0 = area of the throat or orifice (m2), H = differential head expressed in metres of the flowing fluid.`},{e:`v = Cv x sqrt(2 g h)`,c:`Pitot tube point velocity. h = difference between impact and static head in metres of flowing fluid; Cv is close to 1 for a well-made tube.`},{e:`delta P = (rho_m - rho_f) x g x R`,c:`rho_m = density of manometric (heavy) liquid, rho_f = density of the flowing fluid, R = manometer reading in metres, g = 9.81 m/s2.`},{e:`F = 4 f L v^2 / (2 g D)`,c:`Fanning equation for frictional head loss in metres. f = Fanning friction factor, L = pipe length (m), D = diameter (m), v = velocity (m/s).`},{e:`Q = pi x delta P x r^4 / (8 mu L)`,c:`Hagen-Poiseuille equation for laminar flow of a Newtonian fluid in a circular tube. Note the fourth power of radius: halving the radius cuts flow to one sixteenth.`},{e:`Re (stirred vessel) = D^2 N rho / mu`,c:`D = impeller diameter (m), N = rotational speed (revolutions per second). Laminar below about 10, turbulent above about 10000 for a mixing tank.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Re is dimensionless`,x:`Reynolds number has no units, because it is the ratio of inertial force to viscous force. Being asked for the units of Re is a standard trick; the answer is that it has none.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Pitot tube versus orifice meter`,x:`A pitot tube gives the velocity at a single point, not the flow through the pipe; an orifice or venturi gives the average velocity over the whole cross-section. Choosing "pitot tube measures rate of flow" is the standard wrong answer.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Rotameter is the odd one out`,x:`Orifice, venturi and pitot all work at constant area with a varying pressure drop. The rotameter works at constant pressure drop with a varying area. Any question asking for the variable-area meter, or for the meter with a constant pressure drop, is pointing at the rotameter.`}]},"pe-heat-evaporation":{f:[{e:`Q = k A (T1 - T2) / x`,c:`Fourier law of conduction. Q = heat flow (W or J/s), k = thermal conductivity (W/m K), A = area normal to flow (m2), (T1 - T2) = temperature difference (K), x = thickness (m).`},{e:`Q = h A (Ts - Tf)`,c:`Newton's law of cooling for convection. h = film or surface heat transfer coefficient (W/m2 K), Ts = surface temperature, Tf = bulk fluid temperature.`},{e:`Q = sigma x epsilon x A x T^4`,c:`Stefan-Boltzmann law for radiation. sigma = 5.67 x 10^-8 W/m2 K4, epsilon = emissivity (1 for a black body), T = absolute temperature in kelvin.`},{e:`1/U = 1/hi + x/k + 1/ho`,c:`U = overall heat transfer coefficient (W/m2 K), hi and ho = inside and outside film coefficients, x/k = resistance of the metal wall. Scale and dirt films add further resistance terms.`},{e:`Q = U A (delta T)lm`,c:`Design equation for an exchanger. (delta T)lm is the log mean temperature difference between the two streams.`},{e:`Capacity = kilograms of water evaporated per hour`,c:`A measure of throughput; it depends on U, area and temperature difference.`},{e:`Economy (steam economy) = kg of vapour produced / kg of steam supplied`,c:`A measure of efficiency of steam use. For a single effect it is a little below 1, typically around 0.8 to 0.9, because some steam heats the feed to its boiling point.`},{e:`(delta T)lm = (delta T1 - delta T2) / ln(delta T1 / delta T2)`,c:`Log mean temperature difference. If the terminal differences are 50 K and 20 K, (delta T)lm = 30 / ln 2.5 = 30 / 0.916 = 32.7 K.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which law goes with which mechanism`,x:`Fourier for conduction, Newton for convection, Stefan-Boltzmann for radiation. Remember the order C-C-R matched to F-N-S.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Evaporation is not distillation or drying`,x:`In evaporation the product wanted is the concentrated liquid and the vapour is discarded. In distillation the vapour is condensed and kept, and it separates two volatile components. In drying the product is a dry solid.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Economy rises, capacity does not`,x:`The single most repeated statement on evaporation is that multiple effect evaporation increases steam economy without increasing capacity. Options that claim capacity is multiplied by the number of effects are wrong.`}]},"pe-size-reduction-separation":{f:[{e:`dE/dL = -K x L^(-n)`,c:`The general differential form. E = energy per unit mass, L = particle size. Putting n = 2 gives Rittinger, n = 1 gives Kick and n = 1.5 gives Bond.`},{e:`Rittinger: E = Kr x (1/d2 - 1/d1)`,c:`Energy is proportional to the new surface area produced. d1 = initial size, d2 = final size, Kr = Rittinger constant. Best for fine grinding.`},{e:`Kick: E = Kk x ln(d1 / d2)`,c:`Energy depends only on the size reduction ratio d1/d2, not on the absolute sizes. Best for coarse crushing.`},{e:`Bond: E = 2 Kb x (1/sqrt(d2) - 1/sqrt(d1))`,c:`Bond law, intermediate between the other two. In the work index form, W = 10 Wi x (1/sqrt(P80) - 1/sqrt(F80)) with sizes in micrometres.`},{e:`Nc = (1 / 2 pi) x sqrt(g / (R - r))`,c:`Critical speed in revolutions per second. R = radius of the mill, r = radius of the ball, g = 9.81 m/s2. At this speed centrifugal force holds the balls against the wall and no grinding occurs.`},{e:`Nc (rpm) = 42.3 / sqrt(D - d)`,c:`The same equation with the mill diameter D and ball diameter d in metres, giving revolutions per minute directly.`},{e:`v = d^2 (rho_s - rho_l) g / (18 mu)`,c:`Stokes law for terminal settling velocity of a sphere. d = particle diameter, rho_s and rho_l = densities of solid and liquid, mu = liquid viscosity. Velocity varies with the square of diameter.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Size reduction is very inefficient`,x:`Only a small fraction of the energy fed to a mill actually creates new surface; most is lost as heat, noise and elastic deformation. This is why heat-sensitive drugs are milled in a fluid energy mill, where the expanding gas cools the material instead of heating it.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Operate at 50 to 80 percent of critical speed`,x:`Too slow and the balls merely roll (cascading, mainly attrition). At or above critical speed they centrifuge and nothing is ground. Optimum grinding, the cataracting regime, is at roughly two thirds of the critical speed.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Fluid energy mill for thermolabile drugs`,x:`Any question about micronising a heat-sensitive or high-potency drug to a few micrometres in one step points to the fluid energy mill, because the expanding compressed air cools rather than heats the charge.`}]},"pf-cell-injury-inflammation":{f:[{e:`Starling forces: Net filtration = Kf x [ (Pc - Pi) - sigma x (pi c - pi i) ]`,c:`Pc capillary hydrostatic pressure, Pi interstitial hydrostatic pressure, pi c plasma oncotic pressure, pi i interstitial oncotic pressure, sigma the reflection coefficient. Oedema follows a rise in Pc, a fall in pi c, lymphatic obstruction or increased permeability.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Five cardinal signs`,x:`Latin quintet: Rubor, Calor, Tumor, Dolor, Functio laesa - Red, hot, swollen, painful, useless.`},{k:`exam`,t:`One-liners`,x:`Neutrophil is the first cell of acute inflammation; macrophage is the chief cell of chronic inflammation; the epithelioid cell and giant cell make a granuloma; oedema is excess fluid in the interstitial space.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Exudate versus transudate`,x:`Exudate has specific gravity above 1.020 and protein above 3 g/dL because vessels are leaky; transudate has specific gravity below 1.012 and protein below 3 g/dL because only hydrostatic or oncotic pressure has changed. Inflammation gives exudate; heart failure and hypoalbuminaemia give transudate.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Repeatedly asked distinctions`,x:`Keloid extends beyond the wound margin and recurs after excision; a hypertrophic scar stays within the margin and regresses. Dystrophic calcification occurs in dead tissue with normal plasma calcium; metastatic calcification occurs in normal tissue with raised plasma calcium.`}]},"pf-cvs-respiratory-disorders":{f:[{e:`Ejection fraction = Stroke volume / End diastolic volume x 100; normal 55 to 70 percent`,c:`An EDV of 120 mL with an ESV of 50 mL gives SV 70 mL and EF 58 percent. Below 40 percent defines heart failure with reduced ejection fraction.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Basic recall`,x:`The commonest cause of myocardial infarction is thrombosis on a ruptured atherosclerotic plaque; the commonest cause of right heart failure is left heart failure; about 90 to 95 percent of hypertension is essential, that is with no identified cause.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Left versus right failure`,x:`Left fails, Lungs flood (breathlessness, orthopnoea). Right fails, Rest of the body swells (ankle oedema, raised jugular pressure, tender liver).`},{k:`trap`,t:`Chronic bronchitis versus emphysema`,x:`Chronic bronchitis is defined clinically - productive cough for 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years - and gives the blue bloater picture with cyanosis and oedema. Emphysema is defined anatomically as permanent dilatation of airspaces distal to the terminal bronchiole with wall destruction, and gives the pink puffer picture with dyspnoea and pursed lip breathing.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Facts that decide the mark`,x:`Virchow triad for thrombosis is endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow and hypercoagulability. Lines of Zahn indicate a thrombus formed in flowing blood, distinguishing it from a post-mortem clot. Subendocardial infarcts spare the outer wall because the subendocardium is the last region to be perfused and the first to suffer.`}]},"pf-endocrine-renal-disorders":{f:[{e:`Anion gap = Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-); normal 8 to 12 mEq/L`,c:`With Na+ 135, Cl- 95 and HCO3- 10, the gap is 30 mEq/L, a high anion gap acidosis consistent with ketoacidosis. Causes are remembered as MUDPILES - methanol, uraemia, diabetic ketoacidosis, propylene glycol, isoniazid, lactic acidosis, ethylene glycol, salicylate.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Recall facts`,x:`Normal fasting plasma glucose is below 100 mg/dL; diabetes is diagnosed at a fasting value of 126 mg/dL or more, a 2-hour post-glucose value of 200 mg/dL or more, or HbA1c of 6.5 percent or more. Normal serum bilirubin is up to 1 mg/dL and jaundice becomes visible above about 2 to 3 mg/dL.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Cushing versus Addison`,x:`CUSHing - Cortisol Up, Sugar High. Addison - Adds pigment, subtracts everything else (low BP, low sodium, low cortisol).`},{k:`trap`,t:`Gastric versus duodenal ulcer`,x:`Duodenal ulcer pain is relieved by food and typically wakes the patient at night, with normal or high acid output; gastric ulcer pain is worsened by food, with normal or low acid output and a defective mucosal barrier. Malignancy must be excluded in a gastric ulcer, but duodenal ulcers essentially never turn malignant.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Sharp distinctions`,x:`Kernicterus occurs only with unconjugated bilirubin because it is lipid soluble and crosses the immature blood-brain barrier. Gilbert syndrome is mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia from reduced UDP-glucuronyl transferase; Crigler-Najjar type I is near total absence of the enzyme; Dubin-Johnson is conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia with a black pigmented liver.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Zollinger-Ellison syndrome`,x:`Recurrent, multiple ulcers in unusual sites such as the jejunum, with diarrhoea and very high acid output, point to a gastrin-secreting tumour rather than ordinary peptic ulcer disease. Here gastrin is high with high acid, whereas in pernicious anaemia gastrin is high because acid is absent.`}]},"pf-neoplasia-infectious":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Straight recall`,x:`Plasmodium falciparum causes the most severe malaria including cerebral malaria; P. vivax and P. ovale have a dormant liver stage (hypnozoite) that causes relapses; HIV is a retrovirus and AIDS is diagnosed when the CD4 count falls below 200 cells per microlitre or an AIDS-defining illness appears.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`-omas that are malignant`,x:`Remember the liars: LyMphoma, Melanoma, Myeloma, Seminoma - all end in -oma but all are malignant.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Grading is not staging`,x:`Grade describes how the tumour looks - the degree of differentiation and mitotic activity under the microscope. Stage describes how far it has spread, using the TNM system of tumour size, node involvement and metastasis. Stage is the stronger predictor of survival.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Facts examiners return to`,x:`Knudson two-hit hypothesis: a tumour suppressor needs both alleles inactivated, so an inherited first hit explains why familial retinoblastoma is early and bilateral while the sporadic form is late and unilateral. Carcinoma in situ shows full-thickness dysplasia with an intact basement membrane and is by definition not yet invasive.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Hamartoma, choristoma, teratoma`,x:`A hamartoma is a disorganised mass of tissue native to that site; a choristoma is normal tissue in an abnormal site; a teratoma is a true neoplasm from germ cells containing tissue from all three germ layers. Only the teratoma is a genuine neoplasm.`}]},"pg-alkaloids":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Reagent metals`,x:`Mayer = Mercury, Dragendorff = Bismuth (D for the deep orange), Wagner = iodine, Hager = picric acid (H for the yellow "Hager gold"). Marme = Cadmium.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The one drug with an inorganic acid`,x:`Opium alkaloids exist mainly as salts of meconic acid. Meconic acid gives a deep red colour with ferric chloride that is not discharged by hydrochloric acid — the classic forensic test for opium.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Thalleioquin is quinine only`,x:`Quinine gives an emerald-green thalleioquin colour with bromine water and ammonia because it carries a 6-methoxy group on the quinoline ring. Cinchonine and cinchonidine lack the methoxy group and give no thalleioquin — a routine way to distinguish them.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Atropine versus hyoscyamine`,x:`The living plant contains l-hyoscyamine. Atropine is the racemic dl-hyoscyamine formed by racemisation during extraction and drying, and it is about half as active as the natural l-isomer. So the alkaloid of belladonna in the field is hyoscyamine, not atropine.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Structure class is not biosynthetic class`,x:`Quinine is classified structurally as a quinoline alkaloid but arises from tryptophan through strictosidine. Similarly caffeine is a purine pseudoalkaloid not derived from an amino acid, and ephedrine is a protoalkaloid with the nitrogen outside any ring.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Vinca alkaloids`,x:`Vincristine and vinblastine are dimeric indole (bisindole) alkaloids of Catharanthus roseus that bind tubulin and arrest mitosis in metaphase. Vincristine is more neurotoxic; vinblastine is more myelosuppressive. The yield is famously low, around a gram of vincristine from half a tonne of leaf.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Solanaceae tropane trio`,x:`Belladonna, Datura, Hyoscyamus — all Solanaceae, all tropane, all anisocytic (cruciferous) stomata. Belladonna is mostly hyoscyamine, Hyoscyamus and Datura metel are richer in hyoscine.`}]},"pg-carbohydrates-lipids":{f:[{e:`Ester value = Saponification value - Acid value`,c:`All three are expressed as mg of potassium hydroxide per gram of oil.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Solubility separates the two big gums`,x:`Acacia dissolves completely in water to give a clear mucilage. Tragacanth does not dissolve — it swells into a stiff gel. That is why acacia is the emulsifying agent and tragacanth the suspending and thickening agent.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Oil vs volatile oil on paper`,x:`Drop both on filter paper and warm. The fixed oil leaves a permanent translucent stain; the volatile oil evaporates and the stain disappears.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Acacia and hydrogen peroxide`,x:`Acacia contains an oxidase (peroxidase) enzyme, so it spoils preparations containing easily oxidised drugs and gives a false peroxidase reaction. Heating the mucilage to 100 degrees C for a short time destroys the enzyme without harming the gum.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Fiehe test`,x:`Fiehe test uses resorcinol in hydrochloric acid. A cherry-red colour indicates hydroxymethylfurfural, which comes from commercial invert sugar or overheated honey — it is a test for adulteration, not for honey itself.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Two castor oil facts`,x:`Castor oil is the only common fixed oil that is soluble in an equal volume of alcohol, because ricinoleic acid carries a hydroxyl group. The same hydroxyl gives it an unusually high acetyl value of roughly 144 to 150.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Ricin and ricinine`,x:`Castor seed contains ricin, a highly toxic toxalbumin, and ricinine, an alkaloid. Neither is soluble in the oil, so both remain in the seed cake after cold expression — that is why the oil is safe and the cake is not fed to animals.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Saponification value and molecular weight`,x:`A high saponification value means SHORT chain fatty acids, because more ester groups exist per gram. Coconut oil, rich in lauric acid, has a high value; linseed and castor, with long chains, have lower values. Students routinely invert this relationship.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Algal colour to phylum`,x:`Agar and carrageenan are RED algae products (Rhodophyceae); alginates are BROWN algae products (Phaeophyceae). Red for the gel you culture on, brown for the salt you thicken with.`}]},"pg-classification-evaluation":{f:[{e:`Stomatal index = [S / (E + S)] x 100`,c:`S = number of stomata per unit area; E = number of ordinary epidermal cells in the same area. Unlike stomatal number, the index is nearly constant for a species and independent of leaf age and growing conditions.`},{e:`Particles per mg = (N x 94,000 x W) / (S x M)`,c:`N = particles of drug counted; S = lycopodium spores counted in the same fields; W = weight in mg of lycopodium taken; M = weight in mg of sample taken. 94,000 is the number of lycopodium spores per mg of standard lycopodium.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Most asked classification fact`,x:`Chemical classification is the system of choice for a phytochemist because it groups drugs by the constituent being isolated. Its drawback is that one drug with two constituent classes (e.g. cinchona has alkaloids and tannins) cannot be placed in one group.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Order of evaluation`,x:`O-M-P-C-B: Organoleptic, Microscopic, Physical, Chemical, Biological — cheapest and fastest first, most expensive last.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Acid-insoluble ash`,x:`Acid-insoluble ash is the single most useful ash value for detecting sand, soil and earthy adulteration, because silica does not dissolve in dilute hydrochloric acid. Total ash alone cannot separate physiological ash (from the plant) from non-physiological ash (added dirt).`},{k:`trap`,t:`Stomatal number vs stomatal index`,x:`Stomatal number changes with leaf age, humidity and light, so it is a poor identity test. Stomatal index is a ratio, so it stays constant for a species — that is why the pharmacopoeia uses the index for evaluation.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Vein-islet number`,x:`Vein-islet number was introduced by Levin. It is constant for a species and unaffected by leaf age, so it distinguishes closely related leaf drugs — most famously the two sennas, and belladonna from its substitutes.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why bioassay survives`,x:`Bioassay is used when the active principle is unknown, a mixture, or chemically unstable — not because it is more accurate. Chemical assay is always preferred when a specific marker can be measured, because bioassay has far wider confidence limits.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which ash for which fault`,x:`Sand and soil — acid-insoluble ash. Exhausted or previously extracted drug — water-soluble ash and low extractive values. Overall inorganic load — total ash.`}]},"pg-extraction-isolation":{f:[{e:`Concentration in extract at equilibrium: C_extract = C_drug x K / (1 + K)`,c:`K is the partition coefficient of the constituent between menstruum and marc. Because a single maceration never drives K to infinity, repeated small volumes of solvent extract more than one large volume — the basis of multiple extraction and of percolation.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Maceration versus percolation`,x:`Maceration is a batch, equilibrium process, so some drug is always left unextracted; it is chosen when the drug is thermolabile or forms a slimy mass on percolation. Percolation is continuous and gives near-complete extraction, which is why potent tinctures such as belladonna tincture are made by percolation.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Water methods by heat`,x:`Infusion = steep briefly. Decoction = boil hard. Digestion = warm and soak. Maceration = cold and soak.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Soxhlet is not always superior`,x:`Soxhlet uses far less solvent than maceration and keeps presenting fresh solvent to the drug, but the extract sits in a boiling flask for hours. For thermolabile glycosides and volatile constituents it is the wrong choice, and cold maceration or supercritical CO2 is preferred.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Salkowski versus Liebermann-Burchard`,x:`Both detect the steroid nucleus. Salkowski uses chloroform plus concentrated sulphuric acid and the red colour appears in the LOWER acid layer; Liebermann-Burchard uses acetic anhydride plus sulphuric acid and gives a blue-green colour that develops through pink and violet.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why countercurrent chromatography for natural products`,x:`Countercurrent chromatography has no solid stationary phase, so there is no irreversible adsorption and sample recovery approaches 100 percent. That matters when only milligrams of a valuable alkaloid or a labile glycoside are available.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Percolation faults`,x:`Channelling, caking and air locks are the three percolation faults. They arise from uneven packing, too fine a powder, or wetting the powder inadequately before packing. All of them let solvent bypass the drug bed, so the percolate is weak even though the marc is not exhausted.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Polarity ladder for fractionation`,x:`Pet ether, chloroform, ethyl acetate, n-butanol, water — Pretty Cool Extraction By Water. Fats first, sugars and saponins last.`}]},"pg-fibres-adulteration":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Cotton versus jute in one test`,x:`Phloroglucinol with concentrated hydrochloric acid gives a pink-red colour only with lignified fibres. Jute is lignified and answers positively; absorbent cotton is pure cellulose and does not. That single test is the standard way to detect jute in a cotton dressing.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Absorbable list is short`,x:`Absorbable sutures: catgut, collagen, polyglycolic acid, polyglactin, polydioxanone. Everything else in the usual list — silk, nylon, linen, polyester, steel — is non-absorbable.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The exhausted drug signature`,x:`An exhausted drug looks right and passes microscopy, because the tissue is untouched. It is caught by the numbers: alcohol- and water-soluble extractive values fall, water-soluble ash falls, and the assay of the marker constituent fails. Sometimes the drug is recoloured, so an added-dye test may also be positive.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Substitution is not always fraud`,x:`Accidental substitution happens through wrong collection, careless drying, imperfect preparation and storage failures. Deliberate substitution for profit is called sophistication. Examiners ask which type an example belongs to, so read the intent in the stem.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Sinking time of absorbent cotton`,x:`The pharmacopoeial sinking test — absorbent cotton must sink within 10 seconds — is a direct measure of how completely the natural cotton wax was removed during purification. Raw cotton floats indefinitely.`},{k:`trap`,t:`High total ash does not prove adulteration`,x:`Total ash includes physiological ash from the plant itself, which varies with soil and season. Only acid-insoluble ash isolates the added earthy matter. Reporting adulteration on total ash alone is the standard wrong answer.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Five types of adulteration`,x:`I-S-E-A-H: Inferior variety, Superficially similar, Exhausted drug, Artificial substitute, Heavy worthless material.`}]},"pg-glycosides":{f:[{e:`Purpurea glycoside A - (glucose, by digipurpidase) = Digitoxin`,c:`Digipurpidase is the endogenous enzyme of Digitalis purpurea; the same enzyme converts purpurea glycoside B to gitoxin. Rapid drying at 60 degrees C inactivates it and preserves the primary glycosides.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Aglycone carries the activity`,x:`Removing the sugar rarely abolishes activity but it changes potency and pharmacokinetics. In cardiac glycosides the sugar increases water solubility and prolongs the duration of action; the steroid-lactone genin alone is far less useful clinically.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Linkage letters`,x:`O-C-N-S: Ordinary (most drugs), Carbon (aloe), Nitrogen (nucleosides), Sulphur (mustard).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Keller-Kiliani is the deoxy-sugar test`,x:`Keller-Kiliani is positive for glycosides carrying a 2-deoxy sugar (digitoxose, cymarose). Ouabain gives a poor Keller-Kiliani response because its sugar is rhamnose, not a 2-deoxy sugar — the classic exception.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why aloe needs modified Borntrager`,x:`Barbaloin is a C-glycoside — the sugar is attached by a carbon-carbon bond, which ordinary acid hydrolysis cannot break, and it exists in the reduced anthrone form. Ferric chloride supplies the oxidation, so only the modified test gives a pink ammoniacal layer.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Saponin one-liners`,x:`Saponins foam like soap, haemolyse red blood cells, and are highly toxic if injected but relatively safe orally because they are poorly absorbed. Fish poisoning by saponin-containing plants is a standard MCQ.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Primary versus secondary glycosides`,x:`The glycoside as it exists in the living plant is the primary glycoside (purpurea glycoside A, sennoside A, lanatoside C). Partial enzymatic loss of one sugar during drying gives the secondary glycoside (digitoxin, rhein anthrone glycosides, acetyldigoxin). Digitoxin is therefore a secondary, not a primary, glycoside of Digitalis purpurea.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Anthracene oxidation states`,x:`Anthraquinone is the oxidised form; anthrone and anthranol are the reduced forms; dianthrone is a dimer of two anthrone units. Sennosides are dianthrone glycosides — this is why fresh senna causes griping and stored, partly oxidised senna is milder.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Lactone ring size`,x:`CARDenolide = 5-membered ring, C23. BUFadienolide = 6-membered ring, C24. Bufa is bigger in both ring and carbon count.`}]},"pg-tannins-resins":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Ferric chloride colour`,x:`Hydrolysable = Blue-Black (both start with B, and hydrolysable tannins hydrolyse to gallic acid). Condensed = Green (Condensed catechins, C for the green Catechu).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Goldbeater skin`,x:`The goldbeater skin test distinguishes true tannins from pseudotannins. True tannins have a molecular weight roughly 500 to 3000 and can cross-link protein; pseudotannins such as gallic acid, catechin and chlorogenic acid are smaller, give a positive ferric chloride reaction but a NEGATIVE goldbeater skin test.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Sumatra versus Siam benzoin`,x:`Siam benzoin (Styrax tonkinensis) contains benzoic acid and its esters but essentially NO cinnamic acid. Sumatra benzoin (Styrax benzoin) contains both cinnamic and benzoic acids. The classic distinguishing test is heating with potassium permanganate: Sumatra gives the odour of benzaldehyde from cinnamic acid, Siam does not.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Resin, oleoresin, gum-resin`,x:`Ginger and capsicum are OLEORESINS (resin + volatile oil, no gum). Asafoetida, myrrh and gamboge are OLEO-GUM-RESINS (they also contain gum, so they form emulsions with water). Colophony is a pure resin, the non-volatile residue left after turpentine oil is distilled off.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Pseudotannins list`,x:`Gallic acid (nutgall), catechin (catechu, cinchona, acacia), chlorogenic acid (coffee, nux vomica), ipecacuanhic acid (ipecac). All give ferric chloride colours but fail the goldbeater skin test because they cannot cross-link protein.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Tannic acid is not one substance`,x:`Pharmacopoeial tannic acid is a mixture of galloyl esters of glucose obtained from nutgalls, not a single compound. Prolonged use on large burn surfaces was abandoned because absorbed tannic acid caused hepatic necrosis.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Balsam checklist`,x:`A true balsam must contain benzoic or cinnamic acid or their esters: Benzoin, Peru, Tolu, Storax. Copaiba and Canada balsam are called balsams by trade custom but are not true balsams — Canada balsam is an oleoresin used as a microscopy mountant.`}]},"pg-volatile-oils":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Counting terpenes`,x:`Mono 10, Sesqui 15, Di 20, Tri 30, Tetra 40. Sesqui means "one and a half" — one and a half monoterpenes, hence C15.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Volatile oil content in a crude drug`,x:`Volatile oil content of a crude drug is determined by hydrodistillation in a Clevenger apparatus and expressed as percent v/w. The graduated receiver lets you read the oil volume directly.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why citrus oils are expressed`,x:`Citrus peel oils contain heat-sensitive aldehydes such as citral and non-volatile coumarins. Distillation would destroy the aroma, so lemon and orange oils are obtained by cold expression. Enfleurage is reserved for flowers such as jasmine and tuberose whose petals go on producing oil after picking.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Cinnamon versus cassia`,x:`Both are rich in cinnamaldehyde, but only true cinnamon (Cinnamomum zeylanicum) contains appreciable eugenol. Cassia oil gives no eugenol reaction and its bark is thicker and coarser. Cinnamon bark is also the only official bark taken from coppiced shoots.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The two carvone drugs`,x:`Caraway contains d-carvone (dextrorotatory) and spearmint contains l-carvone. The two enantiomers smell completely different — caraway versus spearmint — which is the classic teaching example of chirality and odour.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Oil of a drug versus the drug`,x:`Clove yields 15 to 20 percent of volatile oil and eugenol is 80 to 90 percent OF THAT OIL, not of the clove. Students who quote eugenol as 85 percent of clove lose the mark. Always state whether a percentage refers to the crude drug or to the oil.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Umbelliferous fruits`,x:`Coriander, fennel, caraway, dill, anise, ajowan, cumin — all Umbelliferae fruits with vittae (oil ducts). Linalool, anethole, carvone, carvone, anethole, thymol, cuminaldehyde in the same order.`}]},"ph-ans-adrenergic":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Rate-limiting step`,x:`Tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of catecholamine synthesis. This single fact appears almost every year.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`One heart, two lungs`,x:`Beta-1 for the ONE heart, beta-2 for the TWO lungs. So a cardioselective blocker is a beta-1 blocker and a bronchodilator is a beta-2 agonist.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Dopamine is dose dependent`,x:`Low dose (1-2 mcg/kg/min) acts on D1 to dilate renal vessels. Intermediate dose (2-10) adds beta-1 inotropy. High dose (above 10) recruits alpha-1 and causes vasoconstriction, losing the renal benefit. Dobutamine, by contrast, is a selective beta-1 agonist with little effect on rate.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Adrenaline reversal (Dale vinculum)`,x:`Adrenaline normally raises blood pressure through alpha-1. After an alpha blocker such as phentolamine, the unopposed beta-2 vasodilation makes the SAME dose of adrenaline LOWER the blood pressure. Noradrenaline is not reversed because it has negligible beta-2 action.`},{k:`exam`,t:`First-dose phenomenon`,x:`Prazosin causes marked postural hypotension and syncope after the first dose. Start with a low bedtime dose. Ergotamine, in contrast, is a partial alpha agonist and is used in migraine, not hypertension.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Phaeochromocytoma sequence`,x:`Give the ALPHA blocker (phenoxybenzamine) FIRST, then add a beta blocker. Starting with a beta blocker leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed and can precipitate a hypertensive crisis. Diagnostic marker: raised urinary metanephrines and VMA.`},{k:`trap`,t:`False transmitters`,x:`Methyldopa is converted to alpha-methylnoradrenaline, which is a potent alpha-2 agonist and lowers central sympathetic outflow — an effective false transmitter. Tyramine also enters the vesicle but is a WEAK agonist, so it displaces noradrenaline and causes a pressor surge, the basis of the cheese reaction with MAO inhibitors.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Cardioselective beta blockers`,x:`A BEAM of selectivity: Atenolol, Bisoprolol, Esmolol, Acebutolol, Metoprolol. Everything before N in the alphabet (A to M) is broadly the selective group; N onwards (nadolol, oxprenolol, pindolol, propranolol, sotalol, timolol) is non-selective.`}]},"ph-ans-cholinergic":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Two cholinesterases`,x:`True (acetyl) cholinesterase sits at synapses and on red cells and is specific for ACh. Pseudocholinesterase (butyrylcholinesterase) is made in the liver, circulates in plasma, and hydrolyses succinylcholine, procaine and ester local anaesthetics.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Cholinergic excess = DUMBBELSS`,x:`Diarrhoea, Urination, Miosis, Bradycardia, Bronchospasm, Emesis, Lacrimation, Salivation, Sweating. Anticholinergic excess is the exact opposite: dry as a bone, hot as a hare, red as a beet, blind as a bat, mad as a hatter.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`M-receptor coupling`,x:`Odd = Q (M1, M3, M5 are Gq). Even = I (M2, M4 are Gi). Pirenzepine is the M1 blocker, and pirenzepine reduces acid — because M1 is on the parietal cell pathway.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Neostigmine versus physostigmine`,x:`Neostigmine additionally stimulates the nicotinic end plate directly, which is why it is preferred in myasthenia gravis and for reversing non-depolarising blockade. Physostigmine is the one used for central antimuscarinic (atropine or datura) poisoning because it crosses the blood-brain barrier.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Atropine alone is not enough`,x:`Atropine does nothing for muscle fasciculation and respiratory muscle paralysis because those are nicotinic. That is why pralidoxime must be added. Conversely pralidoxime alone does not control secretions.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Succinylcholine hazards`,x:`It releases potassium from muscle, so it is contraindicated in burns, crush injury, spinal cord injury and hyperkalaemia — risk of cardiac arrest. It also triggers malignant hyperthermia with halothane. Prolonged apnoea occurs in atypical pseudocholinesterase (low dibucaine number).`},{k:`trap`,t:`Ageing of the enzyme`,x:`After an organophosphate phosphorylates AChE, one alkoxy group is lost by hydrolysis. The remaining bond becomes irreversible — this is ageing. Once ageing has occurred pralidoxime cannot reactivate the enzyme, and recovery must wait for new enzyme synthesis (weeks). Ageing is fast with soman and slow with parathion.`}]},"ph-autacoids-nsaids":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Aspirin is not for children with fever`,x:`Aspirin given to a child with a viral fever such as chickenpox or influenza can trigger Reye syndrome, a fatal encephalopathy with fatty liver. Paracetamol is the antipyretic of choice in children.`},{k:`exam`,t:`5-HT3 is the odd one out`,x:`Every other 5-HT receptor is G-protein coupled. 5-HT3 is a ligand-gated cation channel. This single-line fact is asked almost every year.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Never start allopurinol during an acute attack`,x:`A sudden fall in plasma urate mobilises urate out of tophi and destabilises crystals in the joint, which prolongs or worsens the attack. Start urate-lowering therapy 2 to 3 weeks after the flare settles, under colchicine or NSAID cover.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why liver damage is delayed`,x:`Transaminases peak at 48 to 72 hours, not on day one. A normal liver panel on admission does not exclude a lethal ingestion, so treatment is decided on the plasma paracetamol level plotted on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram, not on symptoms.`}]},"ph-chemo-antibacterial":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Tetracyclines and children`,x:`Tetracyclines bind calcium in growing teeth and bone, so they stain teeth permanently and are avoided in children under 8 years, in pregnancy and in lactation.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which drugs are bacteriostatic`,x:`ECSTaCLM: Erythromycin, Clindamycin, Sulfonamides, Tetracyclines, Chloramphenicol, Linezolid, Macrolides. Most of these are protein synthesis inhibitors, and aminoglycosides are the famous exception because they are cidal.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Cotrimoxazole is sequential blockade`,x:`Sulfamethoxazole blocks dihydropteroate synthase and trimethoprim blocks dihydrofolate reductase, two consecutive steps of the same folate pathway. The result is synergy and a bactericidal effect from two bacteriostatic drugs, in a fixed 5 to 1 ratio giving about 20 to 1 in plasma.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Vestibular versus cochlear damage`,x:`Streptomycin and gentamicin damage the vestibular apparatus (vertigo, ataxia); amikacin, kanamycin and neomycin are more cochlear (deafness). Neomycin is the most nephrotoxic and is restricted to topical and oral bowel preparation use.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Chemoprophylaxis you must know`,x:`Rheumatic fever: benzathine penicillin. Meningococcal contacts: rifampicin or ciprofloxacin. Surgical: cefazolin within 60 minutes of incision. Recurrent urinary infection: low-dose nitrofurantoin or cotrimoxazole. Post-splenectomy: penicillin V.`}]},"ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno":{f:[{e:`Cells remaining = initial cells x (fraction surviving per cycle)^n`,c:`n = number of treatment cycles. Chemotherapy kills a constant fraction, not a constant number, of cells (first-order kill), which is why cycles are repeated.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Combination chemotherapy`,x:`Drugs are combined so that they attack by different mechanisms, have different toxicities that do not overlap, and act at different points of the cell cycle. This gives a better kill with tolerable side effects and slows resistance.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Three famous organ toxicities`,x:`Doxorubicin hurts the heart, bleomycin hurts the lung, cisplatin hurts the kidney. Heart, lung, kidney: D, B, C.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Vinca and taxane are opposites with the same result`,x:`Vinca alkaloids prevent microtubules assembling; taxanes prevent them disassembling. Either way the mitotic spindle cannot function and the cell arrests in metaphase. The drugs are opposite in mechanism but identical in outcome.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Vincristine and vinblastine differ in toxicity`,x:`They differ by a single formyl versus methyl group but their dose-limiting toxicities are opposite: vincristine causes neurotoxicity and spares the marrow, vinblastine causes myelosuppression and spares the nerves. Both are fatal if given intrathecally.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Folinic acid, not folic acid`,x:`Folic acid still requires dihydrofolate reductase, the very enzyme methotrexate has blocked, so it cannot rescue. Only folinic acid, which enters the pathway beyond the block, works. Examiners test this distinction directly.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Imatinib is the model targeted drug`,x:`It occupies the ATP binding pocket of the BCR-ABL fusion kinase produced by the Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22), and also inhibits c-KIT and PDGFR. It transformed chronic myeloid leukaemia from a fatal disease into a chronically managed one, and is the standard example of rational drug design in every syllabus.`}]},"ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Rifampicin turns body fluids orange`,x:`Urine, sweat, tears and saliva become orange-red. It is harmless but soft contact lenses stain permanently. Warning the patient in advance prevents them from stopping the drug in alarm.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`First-line TB drugs`,x:`RIPE: Rifampicin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol. Add streptomycin and you have the classical five.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Pyridoxine with isoniazid`,x:`Isoniazid is structurally similar to pyridoxine and increases its urinary excretion, causing peripheral neuropathy. Pyridoxine 10 to 25 mg daily prevents it, and is given routinely to malnourished patients, alcoholics, diabetics, pregnant women and those with HIV.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Ritonavir is used as a booster`,x:`At low dose ritonavir is not there for antiviral effect but as a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor that raises the level of the companion protease inhibitor. Cobicistat is a pure booster with no antiviral activity at all.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Rifampicin plus isoniazid hepatotoxicity`,x:`Isoniazid hepatotoxicity is caused by its metabolite acetylhydrazine. Rifampicin induces the enzymes that generate it, so the combination is more hepatotoxic than either drug alone, particularly in slow acetylators and in the elderly.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Chloroquine does not cure vivax`,x:`Chloroquine clears the blood stage and stops the fever, but the hypnozoites in the liver survive and cause relapse weeks or months later. Only primaquine (or tafenoquine) gives radical cure, which is why the two are prescribed together in P. vivax malaria.`}]},"ph-cns-antipsychotics-antidepressants":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Why atypicals cause fewer movement problems`,x:`Atypicals block 5-HT2A as well as D2, and bind D2 more loosely. Serotonin normally inhibits dopamine release in the striatum, so blocking 5-HT2A lets dopamine rise there and protects against extrapyramidal effects.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Antidepressants take weeks`,x:`Reuptake is blocked within hours but mood improves only after 2-4 weeks, because the benefit needs down-regulation of postsynaptic receptors and neuroplastic change. A patient who says the drug is not working after 5 days has not failed treatment.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`ADAPT`,x:`Acute Dystonia, Akathisia, Parkinsonism, Tardive dyskinesia — in that chronological order. Anticholinergics help the first three and worsen the last.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Clozapine`,x:`Clozapine is the only agent proven effective in treatment-resistant schizophrenia and it reduces suicidality, but it causes agranulocytosis in 1-2 percent. Weekly then monthly white cell counts are mandatory. It also causes seizures, myocarditis, sialorrhoea (paradoxical drooling) and marked weight gain, and it has the LEAST extrapyramidal effect.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Fluoxetine is the long one`,x:`Fluoxetine and its active metabolite norfluoxetine have half-lives of days to weeks, so it causes the least discontinuation syndrome but needs the longest washout (5 weeks) before starting an MAOI. Paroxetine and venlafaxine, with short half-lives, cause the worst withdrawal.`},{k:`exam`,t:`TCA overdose: the three Cs`,x:`Coma, Convulsions and Cardiotoxicity. Sodium channel block widens QRS beyond 100 ms and causes ventricular arrhythmia. Treatment is sodium bicarbonate to alkalinise plasma and raise the sodium gradient, plus supportive care. Physostigmine and flumazenil are contraindicated.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Thiazide plus lithium`,x:`Thiazides cause sodium loss, so the proximal tubule reabsorbs more sodium and, with it, more lithium — the level rises and toxicity follows. Loop diuretics and amiloride do this much less.`}]},"ph-cns-opioids-anaesthetics":{f:[{e:`Maximum safe dose of lignocaine = 4.5 mg/kg plain, 7 mg/kg with adrenaline`,c:`A 70 kg adult may receive about 315 mg plain, i.e. roughly 31 mL of 1 percent solution (1 percent = 10 mg/mL).`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Opioid overdose triad`,x:`Pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression and coma. Add needle marks and the diagnosis is certain. Give naloxone IV — the pupils dilate and breathing returns within 1-2 minutes.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Naloxone versus naltrexone`,x:`Naloxone is parenteral, acts in 1-2 minutes and lasts only 1-2 hours, so a long-acting opioid can outlast it and the patient re-sedates. Naltrexone is orally active and lasts 24-48 hours, so it is used for maintenance of abstinence in opioid and alcohol dependence, not for acute overdose.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Second gas effect`,x:`A high concentration of nitrous oxide is taken up so fast that it concentrates the second gas left behind in the alveoli, speeding its uptake. On stopping, the reverse (diffusion hypoxia) occurs, so give 100 percent oxygen for a few minutes after discontinuing nitrous oxide.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why local anaesthetics fail in inflamed tissue`,x:`Local anaesthetics are weak bases with pKa around 8. Inflamed tissue is acidic, so a far larger fraction is ionised. Only the unionised form crosses the nerve membrane, so less drug gets in and the block fails. Once inside, it is the ionised (cationic) form that blocks the sodium channel from the inner side.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Malignant hyperthermia`,x:`An autosomal dominant ryanodine receptor (RYR1) defect. Succinylcholine or a halogenated agent (halothane, sevoflurane, isoflurane) triggers uncontrolled calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum: masseter spasm, rigidity, a rapid rise in end-tidal CO2, hyperthermia, acidosis and rhabdomyolysis. Treatment is dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV, which blocks the ryanodine receptor, plus cooling and stopping the trigger.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Metoclopramide versus domperidone`,x:`Both are dopamine antagonists, but domperidone does not cross the blood-brain barrier appreciably, so it controls levodopa-induced vomiting without worsening parkinsonism. Metoclopramide crosses and would aggravate the disease.`}]},"ph-cns-sedatives-antiepileptics":{f:[{e:`Rate of elimination = Vmax x C / (Km + C)`,c:`Michaelis-Menten. Vmax = maximal metabolic rate; Km = concentration at half Vmax; C = plasma concentration. When C is much greater than Km, rate approaches Vmax, a constant amount per hour — zero order.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Frequency versus duration`,x:`Benzodiazepines increase the FREQUENCY of chloride channel opening. Barbiturates increase the DURATION of opening. Remember: Ben Frequents the Bar for a long Duration.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Absence seizure`,x:`Ethosuximide for absence. If the child also has tonic-clonic seizures, use valproate instead, because ethosuximide covers only absence.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Absence is a T-party`,x:`Absence seizures arise from T-type calcium currents in thalamic neurones. Ethosuximide blocks T-type calcium channels — the mechanism and the indication travel together.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Do not give carbamazepine in absence`,x:`Carbamazepine, phenytoin, gabapentin and vigabatrin aggravate absence and myoclonic seizures. Valproate and lamotrigine are the broad-spectrum agents that cover almost every type.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Phenytoin adverse effects`,x:`Gum hypertrophy, hirsutism, coarsening of facial features, acne, megaloblastic anaemia (folate interference), osteomalacia (vitamin D metabolism), lymphadenopathy, and dose-related nystagmus then ataxia then confusion. IV phenytoin given fast causes hypotension and arrhythmia because of the propylene glycol vehicle, so use fosphenytoin.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Valproate plus lamotrigine`,x:`Valproate INHIBITS the glucuronidation of lamotrigine and roughly doubles its level, sharply raising the risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome. The lamotrigine starting dose must be halved. Almost every other antiepileptic is an inducer that lowers lamotrigine.`}]},"ph-cvs-antihypertensives":{f:[{e:`BP = Cardiac output x Total peripheral resistance`,c:`BP = arterial blood pressure; cardiac output = stroke volume x heart rate; TPR is set mainly by arteriolar tone.`},{e:`Myocardial oxygen demand is proportional to heart rate x systolic BP (rate-pressure product)`,c:`Rate-pressure product is the bedside index of oxygen demand; beta blockers lower both terms, which is why they raise the anginal threshold.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The ACE inhibitor cough`,x:`ACE also destroys bradykinin. Blocking ACE lets bradykinin and substance P accumulate in the airway, causing a persistent dry cough in 10-20 percent and, rarely, angioedema. ARBs do not touch bradykinin, so they do not cause cough — switch the patient to an ARB.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Never give a nitrate with sildenafil`,x:`Both raise cyclic GMP — the nitrate makes it and sildenafil stops it being broken down. Together they cause severe, potentially fatal hypotension. Allow at least 24 hours after sildenafil and 48 hours after tadalafil.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`CCB selectivity`,x:`Dihydropyridines act on Vessels (amlodipine, nifedipine); verapamil acts on the Ventricle (heart) most; diltiazem sits in between. So dihydropyridines cause reflex tachycardia and ankle oedema, while verapamil causes bradycardia and constipation.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Hypertensive emergency`,x:`Sodium nitroprusside IV is the classic agent: it dilates arteries and veins, acts within seconds and stops within minutes. Cyanide accumulates on prolonged infusion, treated with sodium thiosulphate or hydroxocobalamin. Alternatives are labetalol, esmolol, nicardipine and, in phaeochromocytoma, phentolamine. Lower the pressure by no more than 25 percent in the first hour.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Nitrate tolerance`,x:`Continuous exposure depletes tissue thiol (sulfhydryl) groups and impairs ALDH2, so the response fades within 24 hours. The remedy is a nitrate-free interval of 8-12 hours, usually overnight. Note the paradox: an angina patient needs the drug at night in variant angina, so timing must be reversed there.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Variant angina`,x:`Variant (Prinzmetal) angina is coronary SPASM. Calcium channel blockers and nitrates are the treatment. Beta blockers are avoided because blocking beta-2 mediated coronary dilation leaves alpha-1 vasoconstriction unopposed and can worsen the spasm.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Clonidine withdrawal`,x:`Abrupt withdrawal of clonidine causes a surge of sympathetic outflow with severe rebound hypertension, agitation and sweating. It is worst if the patient is also on a beta blocker, because unopposed alpha stimulation results. Withdraw the beta blocker first, or taper clonidine over a week.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Grapefruit juice`,x:`Grapefruit juice inhibits intestinal CYP3A4 and raises the bioavailability of felodipine, nifedipine and other dihydropyridines, producing exaggerated hypotension. It is the classic food-drug interaction question.`}]},"ph-cvs-heart-failure-antiarrhythmics":{f:[{e:`SGLT2 inhibitor benefit is independent of glycaemia`,c:`Dapagliflozin and empagliflozin lower heart failure hospitalisation and cardiovascular death in patients with and without diabetes, through natriuresis, reduced preload and improved myocardial energetics.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Digoxin does two opposite things`,x:`It strengthens contraction directly (positive inotropic) but SLOWS the heart and AV conduction through increased vagal tone (negative chronotropic and dromotropic). That is why it controls the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Vaughan Williams in one line`,x:`Class I blocks Sodium, Class II is Beta blockers, Class III blocks Potassium, Class IV blocks Calcium. Some Block Potassium Channels: Sodium, Beta, Potassium, Calcium.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Beta blockers in heart failure`,x:`It sounds contradictory to give a negative inotrope in a failing heart, but chronic sympathetic overdrive is what destroys myocardium. Only bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and nebivolol are proven, must be started at a very low dose in a stable patient, and are contraindicated in acute decompensation.`},{k:`exam`,t:`What precipitates digoxin toxicity`,x:`HYPOkalaemia (thiazide and loop diuretics), hypomagnesaemia, HYPERcalcaemia, renal impairment, old age, hypothyroidism, and drugs that raise digoxin levels (quinidine, verapamil, amiodarone, erythromycin). Hypokalaemia is the single commonest cause because low potassium increases digoxin binding to the pump.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Class I subclasses`,x:`Double Quarter Pounder for IA (Disopyramide, Quinidine, Procainamide); Lettuce Mayo Pickle for IB (Lignocaine, Mexiletine, Phenytoin); Fries Please for IC (Flecainide, Propafenone).`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why AV nodal blockers are banned in WPW with AF`,x:`Digoxin, verapamil and adenosine block the AV node, so atrial impulses divert down the accessory pathway. The ventricular rate can accelerate into ventricular fibrillation. Use a drug that blocks the accessory pathway itself, such as procainamide.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Amiodarone versus sotalol`,x:`Both prolong QT, but amiodarone causes torsades de pointes far LESS often than expected because it prolongs repolarisation homogeneously across the myocardium. Sotalol shows reverse use-dependence and readily causes torsades. Dronedarone is the de-iodinated analogue without thyroid toxicity but is contraindicated in severe heart failure.`}]},"ph-cvs-lipids-coagulation":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which drug for which lipid`,x:`Statin for high LDL. Fibrate for high triglyceride. Niacin raises HDL the most. Match the drug to the abnormal number.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Antidotes to memorise`,x:`Heparin is reversed by protamine sulphate. Warfarin is reversed by vitamin K (phytonadione) plus fresh frozen plasma or prothrombin complex concentrate when bleeding is severe. Dabigatran is reversed by idarucizumab.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Statin adverse effects and interactions`,x:`Myopathy risk rises with fibrates (especially gemfibrozil), macrolides, azole antifungals, ciclosporin, verapamil and grapefruit juice, all of which inhibit CYP3A4. Pravastatin, rosuvastatin and pitavastatin are least CYP3A4 dependent. Check creatine kinase if muscle pain occurs; new-onset diabetes is a recognised class effect.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why heparin overlaps with warfarin`,x:`Warfarin first depletes protein C, whose half-life (about 8 hours) is shorter than that of prothrombin (about 60 hours), producing a brief PROcoagulant state that can cause skin necrosis. Heparin must therefore be continued for at least 5 days and until the INR has been in range for 2 days.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT)`,x:`Type II HIT is immune, appears 5-10 days after starting heparin, drops the platelet count by more than 50 percent and paradoxically causes THROMBOSIS, not bleeding. Antibodies target the heparin-platelet factor 4 complex. Stop all heparin including flushes and LMWH, and switch to a direct thrombin inhibitor such as argatroban or bivalirudin. Warfarin must not be started until platelets recover.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Aspirin resistance and dosing`,x:`Higher aspirin doses inhibit endothelial COX-2 and therefore prostacyclin as well, which is antithrombotically counterproductive. That is why the antiplatelet dose stays at 75-150 mg daily, while analgesic doses are 10-50 times higher.`}]},"ph-diuretics-renal":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Potassium is the thing to watch`,x:`Loop diuretics and thiazides make the body lose potassium, which can cause muscle weakness and dangerous heart rhythms. Spironolactone and amiloride do the opposite and can cause potassium to build up. That is why the two groups are often combined.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Spironolactone spares potassium`,x:`Spare rhymes with spironolactone and amiloride is in the same group. Everything else in the diuretic list wastes potassium.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Loop loses calcium, thiazide takes it back`,x:`Loop diuretics cause hypocalcaemia and are used in hypercalcaemia; thiazides cause hypercalcaemia and are used to prevent calcium oxalate stones and in idiopathic hypercalciuria.`},{k:`trap`,t:`A diuretic that treats too much urine`,x:`Thiazides paradoxically reduce urine volume in nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Sodium depletion increases proximal reabsorption of salt and water, so less fluid reaches the collecting duct where the concentrating defect lies.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The loop diuretic also kills the countercurrent gradient`,x:`Blocking NKCC2 abolishes the medullary hypertonicity that concentrates urine, so a loop diuretic impairs both concentration and dilution of urine. A thiazide only impairs dilution, which is why thiazides are the classic cause of drug-induced hyponatraemia.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Acetazolamide is self-limiting`,x:`Its diuresis stops after two or three days because bicarbonate is depleted and the resulting metabolic acidosis removes its own substrate. Efficacy returns after a few days off the drug, so intermittent dosing is used.`}]},"ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Levothyroxine on an empty stomach`,x:`Levothyroxine is taken in the morning at least 30 to 60 minutes before food. Calcium, iron and antacids bind it in the gut and must be separated by about 4 hours.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which ones lose weight`,x:`The two classes that push glucose or calories out of the body cause weight loss: gliflozins (glucose out in urine) and GLP-1 agonists (less eaten). Sulfonylureas, meglitinides and glitazones store it, so they cause weight gain.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Iodide has opposite effects`,x:`Small doses of iodide support hormone synthesis, but large doses acutely inhibit release and organification (the Wolff-Chaikoff effect). The block lasts only 10 to 14 days, so Lugol iodine is used for pre-operative preparation and thyroid storm, never as long-term therapy.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Euglycaemic ketoacidosis`,x:`SGLT2 inhibitors can produce ketoacidosis with only mildly raised or even normal glucose, because urinary glucose loss masks the hyperglycaemia. Suspect it on the acid-base picture, not the glucose reading, and stop the drug before surgery or prolonged fasting.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Order matters in thyroid storm`,x:`Give the thioamide before the iodide. If iodide is given first it is trapped and organified into fresh hormone, making the storm worse. Steroids are added because they block peripheral T4 to T3 conversion and cover relative adrenal insufficiency.`}]},"ph-general-principles":{f:[{e:`Therapeutic Index (TI) = LD50 / ED50`,c:`LD50 = dose lethal to 50 percent of animals; ED50 = dose producing the desired effect in 50 percent. In humans TD50 replaces LD50. Higher TI = safer drug.`},{e:`Certain Safety Factor (Standard Safety Margin) = LD1 / ED99`,c:`LD1 = dose lethal to 1 percent; ED99 = dose effective in 99 percent. A more conservative index because it uses the overlapping tails, not the midpoints.`},{e:`E / Emax = [D] / ([D] + KD)`,c:`E = effect at concentration [D]; Emax = maximal effect; KD = dissociation constant, the concentration occupying half the receptors. When effect is directly proportional to occupancy, EC50 equals KD.`},{e:`Dose ratio - 1 = [B] / KB     and     pA2 = -log KB`,c:`Schild equation. Dose ratio = agonist EC50 with antagonist divided by EC50 without; [B] = antagonist concentration; KB = antagonist dissociation constant. pA2 is the negative log of the antagonist concentration that makes the dose ratio 2, i.e. that requires a doubling of agonist dose.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Speed of response`,x:`Ion channel = milliseconds, GPCR = seconds, kinase-linked = minutes, nuclear = hours. The deeper into the cell the receptor sits, the slower the response.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Potency is almost never what matters`,x:`Students pick the more potent drug as the better drug. Clinically efficacy matters; potency only decides the size of the tablet. A question asking for the more useful diuretic in oedema wants the high-efficacy loop diuretic, not the more potent thiazide.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Partial agonist in the presence of a full agonist`,x:`A partial agonist behaves as an ANTAGONIST when a full agonist is already present, because it occupies receptors but produces less than the maximal response. This is why pindolol reduces the effect of endogenous adrenaline yet still supports resting heart rate.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Physiological versus pharmacological antagonism`,x:`Adrenaline reversing histamine-induced bronchospasm is PHYSIOLOGICAL (functional) antagonism — two different receptors producing opposite effects. Chlorpheniramine blocking histamine at H1 is pharmacological antagonism. Protamine neutralising heparin is CHEMICAL antagonism.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Tachyphylaxis`,x:`Tachy = fast. Tachyphylaxis is the FAST one, classically with indirectly acting sympathomimetics such as ephedrine and tyramine, whose effect fades as the noradrenaline stores empty.`},{k:`exam`,t:`pA2 interpretation`,x:`A higher pA2 means a more potent competitive antagonist. A Schild plot slope of 1 confirms simple competitive antagonism; a slope other than 1 indicates non-competitive or allosteric behaviour.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`A to F`,x:`Augmented, Bizarre, Chronic, Delayed, End-of-use, Failure.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Enzyme induction takes time`,x:`Induction requires new protein synthesis, so it develops over days to two weeks and wears off equally slowly. Inhibition is immediate — competitive occupancy of the enzyme. A question describing INR rising within 24 hours of adding a drug points to inhibition, not induction.`}]},"ph-git-respiratory":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Magnesium moves, aluminium anchors`,x:`Magnesium-containing antacids cause diarrhoea; aluminium-containing antacids cause constipation. They are combined so the two effects cancel out.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Rinse the mouth after a steroid inhaler`,x:`Inhaled corticosteroid left in the mouth causes oral thrush and hoarseness. Rinsing and spitting after each dose, or using a spacer, prevents both.`},{k:`exam`,t:`PPIs are acid-activated prodrugs`,x:`Omeprazole is an inactive prodrug that must be protonated in the acidic canaliculus to form the sulfenamide that binds cysteine residues of the pump. This is why it is enteric coated, why it is taken 30 to 60 minutes before a meal, and why it works best when pumps are actively secreting.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Metoclopramide versus domperidone`,x:`Both are D2 blockers and prokinetics, but metoclopramide crosses the blood-brain barrier and causes extrapyramidal reactions and acute dystonia, especially in the young. Domperidone does not cross appreciably, so it has no extrapyramidal effect but does prolong the QT interval.`},{k:`exam`,t:`LABA monotherapy is contraindicated`,x:`A long-acting beta-2 agonist used alone relieves symptoms while inflammation progresses silently, and is associated with increased asthma deaths. LABAs must always be given with an inhaled corticosteroid, usually in a single combination inhaler.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Aminophylline is not theophylline`,x:`Aminophylline is theophylline with ethylenediamine, added purely for water solubility so it can be injected. Roughly 100 mg of aminophylline supplies about 80 mg of theophylline, so doses are not interchangeable milligram for milligram.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Acetylcysteine has two lives`,x:`The same molecule is a mucolytic by cleaving mucus disulfide bonds and the antidote in paracetamol poisoning by replenishing hepatic glutathione. Examiners like this dual role.`}]},"ph-steroids-reproductive":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Never stop a steroid suddenly`,x:`Steroids given for more than about two weeks switch off the pituitary and shrink the adrenal cortex. Stopping abruptly leaves the patient unable to make cortisol, causing an acute adrenal crisis with vomiting, low blood pressure and shock. Always taper.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Aldosterone keeps salt, loses potassium`,x:`Mineralocorticoid means mineral. Aldosterone retains sodium and water and throws out potassium and hydrogen ions, so excess causes high blood pressure with low potassium.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Alternate-day therapy uses short and intermediate drugs only`,x:`Giving a double dose every second morning lets the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis recover on the off day. It only works with prednisolone-type intermediate-acting steroids. Long-acting dexamethasone suppresses the axis continuously and defeats the purpose.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Ergometrine versus oxytocin`,x:`Methylergometrine causes a sustained tonic contraction, so it is never used before delivery of the baby. Oxytocin gives rhythmic contractions with relaxation between and is the drug for augmenting labour.`},{k:`trap`,t:`The GnRH agonist flare`,x:`A GnRH agonist first stimulates the pituitary and causes a testosterone surge that can worsen bone pain or cause cord compression in metastatic prostate cancer. Cover the first two weeks with an antiandrogen such as bicalutamide, or use the antagonist degarelix which has no flare.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Continuous PTH resorbs, pulsed PTH builds`,x:`Continuous parathyroid hormone excess causes bone resorption, but once-daily teriparatide injection produces a short pulse that favours osteoblast survival and net bone formation. This paradox is a repeated GPAT question.`}]},"ph-toxicology":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Never lavage a corrosive or a kerosene`,x:`Acids, alkalis and petroleum products must not be removed by gastric lavage. Corrosives can perforate the already damaged oesophagus, and kerosene can be aspirated into the lung causing chemical pneumonitis.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Charcoal does not bind PHAILS`,x:`Pesticides, Hydrocarbons, Acids and alkalis, Iron, Lithium, Solvents and alcohols. These pass straight through.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Flumazenil is not routine`,x:`In a mixed overdose or in a chronic benzodiazepine user, flumazenil can precipitate uncontrollable seizures by removing the anticonvulsant cover, especially when tricyclic antidepressants are also present. It is used mainly for reversing procedural sedation.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Give pralidoxime early`,x:`Once ageing has occurred the enzyme cannot be reactivated by any oxime and recovery must wait for synthesis of new cholinesterase over days to weeks. Ageing is fast with some agents, so oxime therapy is started as early as possible alongside atropine.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Use disodium calcium EDTA, not disodium EDTA`,x:`Plain disodium EDTA avidly chelates plasma calcium and can cause fatal hypocalcaemic tetany. The calcium salt exchanges its calcium for lead and is the only form used for chelation.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Toxic alcohols share the same enzyme`,x:`Methanol, ethylene glycol and ethanol all compete for alcohol dehydrogenase. That single fact explains why ethanol is an antidote, why fomepizole replaced it, and why both poisonings present with a high anion gap and a raised osmolar gap.`}]},"pj-dc-act":{f:[],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Misbranded vs spurious`,x:`Misbranding is a labelling defect - the product is genuine but the label lies. Spurious is an identity fraud - the product pretends to be another manufacturer’s product or is an imitation. Adulteration is a quality defect in the substance itself.`},{k:`exam`,t:`One-liners that repeat`,x:`Act 1940, Rules 1945. Central Drugs Laboratory is at Kolkata. DTAB is the technical advisory body; DCC exists to secure uniformity of administration between the Centre and the States.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Personnel rule that is asked`,x:`Retail sale of drugs must be made by or under the personal supervision of a registered pharmacist. For wholesale, a competent person is accepted - a graduate with about one year of experience in dealing in drugs, or a person with a pass in the school leaving examination and about four years of such experience.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Cognizable and non-bailable`,x:`The serious spurious and adulterated drug offences were made cognizable and non-bailable by the 2008 amendment, and Special Courts may be designated to try them. Do not assume every offence under the Act is bailable - the gravity of the drug offence decides.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Sampling arithmetic`,x:`When an inspector takes a sample for test, the quantity is divided into portions - one is given to the person from whom it is taken, one is sent to the Government Analyst and one is retained for production before the court. If the Analyst report is disputed, the court portion goes to the Central Drugs Laboratory and that report is conclusive evidence.`}]},"pj-ndps-and-others":{f:[],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Bhang`,x:`Cannabis or hemp under the Act means charas, ganja and any mixture or drink prepared from them. The leaves and seeds of the plant, when not accompanied by the tops, are outside that definition - which is why bhang is treated differently from ganja.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Do not mix the Acts up`,x:`A false claim on a label is D and C Act misbranding. A newspaper advertisement claiming a cure for diabetes is the Magic Remedies Act. A tincture containing alcohol attracts the Medicinal and Toilet Preparations Act. Morphine stock control is NDPS plus Schedule X.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Four prohibited purposes`,x:`Miscarriage, Menstrual disorder, Manhood (sexual pleasure) and Malady on the Schedule - the four Ms of Section 3.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Bonded versus non-bonded`,x:`Bonded manufacture uses duty-free spirit and is preferred for large-scale production of preparations with high alcohol content, because duty is paid only on the finished dutiable goods that are actually cleared. Non-bonded manufacture uses duty-paid alcohol and suits small-scale work.`}]},"pj-pharmacy-act":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The single most asked provision`,x:`Section 42 - no person other than a registered pharmacist shall compound, prepare, mix or dispense any medicine on the prescription of a medical practitioner. Contravention attracts imprisonment which may extend to six months, or fine, or both.`},{k:`trap`,t:`PCI is not the licensing authority`,x:`PCI sets education standards and approves courses. It does not issue drug licences and it does not inspect chemist shops - that is the State Drugs Controller under the D and C Act.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Reciprocity and the Central Register`,x:`The Central Register maintained by PCI is a compilation of the State registers, which is what makes reciprocal recognition between States workable. Questions sometimes ask who maintains the Central Register - the answer is the Pharmacy Council of India, not the State Council.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Who approves a pharmacy college?`,x:`For the pharmacy qualification to count for registration, PCI approval under the Pharmacy Act is what matters. AICTE approval and university affiliation address technical education and degree award respectively - they do not by themselves make a graduate registrable.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Numbers worth carrying`,x:`Six teacher members and six pharmacist members elected, eight Central Government nominees of whom at least four are registered pharmacists, one representative per State, five-year term, and a six-month maximum imprisonment under Section 42.`}]},"pj-price-control":{f:[{e:`P(ceiling) = Average Price to Retailer x (1 + M / 100)`,c:`P(ceiling) = ceiling price of the scheduled formulation. Average Price to Retailer = simple average of the price to retailer of all brands of that formulation having a market share at or above the notified threshold of total market turnover. M = notified retailer margin, taken as 16 per cent.`},{e:`R.P. = (M.C. + C.C. + P.M. + P.C.) x (1 + MAPE / 100) + ED`,c:`R.P. = retail price. M.C. = material cost. C.C. = conversion cost. P.M. = cost of packing material. P.C. = packing charges. MAPE = maximum allowable post-manufacturing expenses. ED = excise duty where applicable. This cost-plus approach belonged to the DPCO 1995 regime.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Two dates and two names`,x:`DPCO is issued under the Essential Commodities Act 1955. The current order is the DPCO 2013, which replaced the cost-based DPCO 1995 with market-based ceiling pricing. NPPA is the implementing authority.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Ten per cent is annual, not per revision`,x:`For a non-scheduled formulation the cap is a maximum increase of ten per cent over the preceding twelve months, taken as a whole. Several small increases within a year are added together against the same cap.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Paragraph 19 power`,x:`DPCO 2013 contains an extraordinary power under which the Government may fix the ceiling or retail price of any drug, including a non-scheduled drug, in the public interest and in circumstances it considers extraordinary. This power has been used for cardiac stents and knee implants.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Central versus State licensing`,x:`Manufacturing and sale licences are issued by the State Licensing Authority. The Centre, acting as the Central Licence Approving Authority, gives concurrence for specified classes such as vaccines, sera, blood products and large volume parenterals, and it alone approves new drugs, clinical trials and imports.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Three-body separation`,x:`PCI regulates the pharmacist, CDSCO regulates the drug, NPPA regulates the price. A question naming any one function should let you name the correct body immediately.`}]},"pj-schedules":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`For the letters that clash`,x:`M is Manufacturing practice, N is Necessary equipment for a pharmacy, P is Period of life, U is Upkeep of records, V is Vitamin and other limits in patent medicines, X is eXtra control (narcotics), Y is clinical trial studY.`},{k:`trap`,t:`H versus X versus G`,x:`Schedule G is not a prescription schedule - it only needs a caution statement. Schedule H needs a prescription and the red Rx symbol. Schedule X needs a special licence, a prescription in duplicate and the red NRx symbol.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Schedule J is a labelling ban, not a drug list`,x:`Schedule J lists diseases and ailments which a drug may not purport to prevent or cure on its label, for example cancer, cataract, diabetes and deafness. It is the labelling counterpart of the schedule under the Drugs and Magic Remedies Act.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Schedule D versus Schedule K`,x:`Both are exemption schedules but they sit in different chapters. Schedule D exempts specified classes from provisions of Chapter III, which is import. Schedule K exempts specified classes from provisions of Chapter IV, which is manufacture, sale and distribution.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Three-line answer that covers most schedule questions`,x:`If a question names a list of drugs, think C, C1, G, H, H1 or X. If it names a standard, think F, FF, O, R, S or V. If it names a requirement of premises or records, think M, N, T or U. If it names a prohibition, think J.`}]},"pm-basics-staining":{f:[{e:`n = (log Nt - log N0) / log 2 = 3.3 (log Nt - log N0);  g = t / n`,c:`n = number of generations, Nt = final count, N0 = initial count, t = time elapsed, g = generation (doubling) time. E. coli g is about 20 min, M. tuberculosis about 18 to 20 h.`},{e:`log Nt = log N0 + (t / g) x log 2`,c:`A rearrangement of the generation-time equation. Example: 10^3 cells with g = 20 min grown for 4 h give n = 240/20 = 12 generations, so Nt = 10^3 x 2^12 = 4.096 x 10^6 cells.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Flagellar arrangements`,x:`Mono = one at one end; Amphi = one at each end; Lopho = a tuft at one end; Peri = all over the cell (as in Salmonella and E. coli).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Gram stain in one line`,x:`Gram positive cells hold crystal violet and appear purple; Gram negative cells lose it during decolourisation and take up the safranin counterstain, appearing pink or red.`},{k:`trap`,t:`The step that decides everything`,x:`Decolourisation is the critical and most error-prone step. Over-decolourisation makes Gram positives look Gram negative; under-decolourisation makes Gram negatives look Gram positive. Old cultures of Gram positives also decolourise easily, so always Gram stain a young culture (18 to 24 h).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Aseptic technique`,x:`Aseptic technique means preventing entry of contaminants: work in a laminar air flow cabinet supplied through a HEPA filter, flame the loop to red heat before and after use, disinfect surfaces with 70 percent isopropyl alcohol (not absolute alcohol, which fixes rather than kills), and never pass a hand over an open plate.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Antibiotics act only on growing cells`,x:`Cell-wall-active agents such as penicillins and cephalosporins are bactericidal only against actively dividing cells, which is why they work in the log phase and why they cannot kill dormant spores or non-growing persisters.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Classic single-fact questions`,x:`Volutin (metachromatic, Babes-Ernst) granules stain with Alberts stain and are typical of Corynebacterium diphtheriae. Poly-beta-hydroxybutyrate is a lipid storage inclusion. Gas vacuoles occur in aquatic bacteria. Bacterial flagella are made of flagellin and rotate; eukaryotic flagella have a 9 + 2 microtubule pattern and beat.`}]},"pm-fermentation":{f:[{e:`mu = mu(max) x S / (Ks + S)`,c:`The Monod equation. mu = specific growth rate per hour, mu(max) = maximum specific growth rate, S = limiting substrate concentration, Ks = substrate concentration at half of mu(max). It has the same form as the Michaelis-Menten equation.`},{e:`D = F / V; at steady state in a chemostat D = mu`,c:`D = dilution rate per hour, F = medium flow rate, V = culture volume. If D is raised above mu(max) the culture is washed out of the vessel.`},{e:`OTR = kLa x (C* - CL)`,c:`OTR = oxygen transfer rate, kLa = volumetric mass transfer coefficient per hour, C* = saturation dissolved oxygen concentration, CL = actual dissolved oxygen in the broth. Oxygen is poorly soluble in water, so kLa usually limits an aerobic fermentation.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Why baffles matter`,x:`Without baffles the whole liquid rotates with the impeller as a solid body, a vortex forms, air is drawn in unevenly and mixing collapses. Baffles convert rotational flow into top-to-bottom turbulence, which is what actually transfers oxygen.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Primary versus secondary metabolite`,x:`Primary metabolites appear in the growth phase (trophophase) and the cell needs them to live: ethanol, lactic acid, amino acids, vitamins. Secondary metabolites appear after growth slows (idiophase) and the cell can live without them: antibiotics, alkaloids, pigments, toxins.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why glucose is fed slowly`,x:`A high glucose concentration represses penicillin synthetase, so batch culture on excess glucose gives good growth and almost no penicillin. Lactose is used because it is consumed slowly, or glucose is fed continuously at a low rate: this is exactly what fed-batch operation is for.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Facts that decide a mark`,x:`Penicillin acylase converts penicillin G to 6-aminopenicillanic acid, the nucleus for all semisynthetic penicillins. Cephalosporin C comes from Acremonium chrysogenum (formerly Cephalosporium acremonium) and yields 7-aminocephalosporanic acid. Ergot alkaloids come from Claviceps purpurea and gibberellic acid from Gibberella fujikuroi.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Contamination in continuous culture`,x:`Continuous fermentation gives the highest volumetric productivity but is rarely used for antibiotics. Over weeks of operation a single contaminant or a faster-growing non-producing mutant will out-compete the production strain, so batch and fed-batch remain the industrial standard.`}]},"pm-immunology-vaccines":{f:[],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Fab and Fc`,x:`Fab = fragment antigen binding (two per IgG). Fc = fragment crystallisable, the effector end. Papain gives two Fab plus one Fc; pepsin cuts lower down and gives one F(ab)2 that is still bivalent.`},{k:`trap`,t:`IgM versus IgG in diagnosis`,x:`A rise in specific IgM means recent or current infection because IgM appears first and disappears in weeks. IgG persists for years and only indicates past exposure or vaccination. IgM cannot cross the placenta, so IgM in cord blood proves intrauterine infection.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`ACID`,x:`Type I Anaphylactic, II Cytotoxic, III Immune complex, IV Delayed. Types I to III are antibody mediated and can be transferred by serum; only type IV is cell mediated and needs lymphocytes.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Live versus killed in one line`,x:`Live attenuated vaccines give both humoral and cell-mediated immunity, often after a single dose, but need a cold chain and can revert. Killed vaccines give mainly humoral immunity, need multiple doses and an adjuvant, but are stable and safe in the immunocompromised.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Drug allergy mechanisms`,x:`Penicillin is a hapten: its beta-lactam ring opens and the penicilloyl group binds lysine residues of serum proteins, and this conjugate provokes IgE. Penicillin can also cause type II haemolytic anaemia and type III serum sickness, so one drug can produce three different hypersensitivity types.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which vaccines must never be frozen`,x:`Aluminium-adsorbed vaccines (DPT, DT, TT, hepatitis B) lose potency permanently on freezing because the adsorbed antigen is released from the adjuvant. Freezing does not harm live attenuated oral polio vaccine.`}]},"pm-microbial-assay":{f:[{e:`x squared = 4 D t (ln C - ln C0)`,c:`The Cooper-Woodman diffusion equation. x = zone radius, D = diffusion coefficient, t = time before growth becomes visible, C = applied concentration, C0 = critical inhibitory concentration. It shows why the square of the zone radius, not the diameter itself, is strictly proportional to log concentration.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The relationship to remember`,x:`In agar diffusion the zone diameter is proportional to the logarithm of the concentration, not to the concentration itself. Plotting zone diameter against log dose gives a straight line.`},{k:`trap`,t:`When turbidimetry cannot be used`,x:`The turbidimetric method reads light scattering, so a coloured, turbid or particulate sample gives a false reading. Such samples must go to the cylinder-plate method.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Direction of the response`,x:`In an antibiotic assay growth falls as dose rises; in a vitamin assay growth rises as dose rises. The vitamin assay is therefore a growth-promotion assay, the antibiotic assay a growth-inhibition assay.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why a chemical assay is not enough`,x:`A chemical assay counts molecules whether or not they are active. If an antibiotic has partly degraded into an inactive but chemically similar product, HPLC may still report full content while the microbial assay correctly reports reduced potency. That is why potency of antibiotics is defined biologically, in units.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Units worth knowing`,x:`One international unit of penicillin G equals 0.6 microgram of the sodium salt, so 1 mg equals about 1667 IU. Potencies of streptomycin and tetracycline are quoted in micrograms of base per milligram. Always state whether the potency refers to the base or to a salt.`}]},"pm-sterilisation":{f:[{e:`D value = time in minutes at a stated temperature to kill 90 percent (one log) of the population`,c:`Written D121 when measured at 121 degrees C. It is the reciprocal of the slope of the survivor curve.`},{e:`log N0 - log Nt = t / D`,c:`N0 = initial count, Nt = count after time t, D = D value. Rearranged: t = D x (log N0 - log Nt).`},{e:`Z value = degrees C rise needed to reduce the D value tenfold`,c:`For saturated steam Z is taken as 10 degrees C; for dry heat it is about 20 degrees C.`},{e:`F0 = delta t x sum of 10 raised to ((T - 121) / 10)`,c:`F0 = lethality of the whole cycle expressed as equivalent minutes at 121 degrees C, with Z = 10 degrees C. T = product temperature at each interval, delta t = length of each interval in minutes. Pharmacopoeial minimum F0 is 8 minutes; 12 is commonly targeted.`},{e:`Inactivation factor IF = 10 raised to (t / D)`,c:`IF is the factor by which the population is reduced. Nt = N0 / IF. A 15 min cycle with D121 = 1 min gives IF = 10^15.`},{e:`log D1 - log D2 = (T2 - T1) / Z`,c:`The Z-value relationship. D1 and D2 are D values at temperatures T1 and T2. Example: D110 = 20 min and D120 = 2 min gives Z = 10 degrees C.`},{e:`Bubble point P = 4 gamma cos(theta) / d`,c:`P = bubble point pressure, gamma = surface tension of the wetting liquid, theta = contact angle, d = pore diameter. Smaller pores give a higher bubble point, so a low reading means an oversized pore or a damaged filter.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The single most asked autoclave fact`,x:`Standard autoclave cycle: 121 degrees C at 15 psi (about 1.05 kg per sq cm gauge) for 15 minutes with saturated steam.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Air is the enemy of the autoclave`,x:`Residual air in the chamber lowers the actual steam temperature even though the pressure gauge reads 15 psi. That is why air must be displaced downward or removed by a vacuum pump before the timed holding period begins.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Worked numerical 1`,x:`Bioburden 10^6 per container, D121 = 1.5 min, required sterility assurance level 10^-6. Total log reduction needed = 6 - (-6) = 12 logs. Time = 12 x 1.5 = 18 minutes at 121 degrees C.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Worked numerical 2`,x:`A load holds at 115 degrees C for 30 min. F0 = 30 x 10^((115-121)/10) = 30 x 10^-0.6 = 30 x 0.251 = 7.5 minutes. That is below 8, so the cycle would fail a pharmacopoeial F0 requirement.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Order of resistance to sterilisation`,x:`Prions are more resistant than bacterial spores, which beat Mycobacteria, which beat non-lipid viruses, which beat fungi, which beat vegetative bacteria; lipid-enveloped viruses are the easiest to kill.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Worked numerical 3`,x:`D121 of Geobacillus stearothermophilus spores is 2 min and the biological indicator carries 10^6 spores. To achieve a probability of a surviving organism of 10^-6 you need 12 log reductions, so the cycle must deliver 12 x 2 = 24 minutes at 121 degrees C, i.e. an F0 of 24.`},{k:`trap`,t:`What filtration does not remove`,x:`A 0.22 micrometre sterilising filter removes bacteria and fungi but not viruses, not mycoplasma reliably, and not pyrogens, because endotoxin molecules are far smaller than the pore. Depyrogenation needs dry heat at 250 degrees C, ultrafiltration, or adsorption on activated charcoal.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Fine distinctions that get asked`,x:`Steam must be saturated and dry: superheated steam behaves like dry heat and is much less lethal, while wet steam wets the load. Formaldehyde is used as low temperature steam-formaldehyde at about 73 degrees C. Beta-propiolactone is highly virucidal but carcinogenic. Peracetic acid leaves no toxic residue, breaking down to acetic acid, water and oxygen.`}]},"pm-sterility-validation":{f:[{e:`Endotoxin limit = K / M`,c:`K = threshold pyrogenic dose, 5.0 EU per kg per hour for parenteral routes and 0.2 EU per kg for intrathecal; M = maximum human dose per kg per hour. Example: a drug with M = 1 mg per kg gives a limit of 5 EU per mg.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which medium at which temperature`,x:`Thioglycollate is Thirty-five (30 to 35 degrees C, anaerobes); Soybean is the cooler one at 20 to 25 degrees C for fungi.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Two different pore sizes`,x:`Sterilising filtration uses 0.22 micrometre, but the sterility test membrane is 0.45 micrometre. The test membrane only needs to retain any contaminant present, and the larger pore filters viscous products faster.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Numbers to carry into the hall`,x:`Grade A unidirectional air velocity 0.36 to 0.54 metre per second (usually quoted as 0.45). Air changes of not less than 20 per hour in Grades B, C and D. Pressure differential between adjacent grades of 10 to 15 pascals. Media fill acceptance: with 5000 units or more filled, not more than one contaminated unit, i.e. a contamination rate below 0.1 percent.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why the sterility test is weak`,x:`If only 0.1 percent of a batch is contaminated and 20 units are tested, the probability of detecting the contamination is only about 2 percent. The test can therefore condemn a bad batch but can never certify a good one; assurance comes from validation and parametric release.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Removing pyrogens`,x:`Endotoxin is destroyed by dry heat at 250 degrees C for 30 minutes (glass and metal), by strong alkali or oxidising agents, and removed by ultrafiltration, reverse osmosis or adsorption onto activated charcoal. Autoclaving and 0.22 micrometre filtration do not remove it.`}]},"pp-buffers-isotonicity":{f:[{e:`pH = pKa + log ( [salt] / [acid] )`,c:`Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for an acidic buffer. [salt] = concentration of the conjugate base; [acid] = concentration of the undissociated weak acid. For a basic buffer, pH = pKw - pKb + log ( [base] / [salt] ).`},{e:`beta = dB / d(pH)`,c:`Buffer capacity (Van Slyke index) = gram equivalents per litre of strong base or acid (dB) needed to produce one unit change in pH (d(pH)). It is a dimensionless-looking but concentration-based measure of buffer strength; the larger the value, the more resistant the buffer.`},{e:`beta = 2.303 x C x [ Ka x (H3O+) ] / [ Ka + (H3O+) ]^2`,c:`Van Slyke equation. C = total buffer concentration (sum of salt and acid, mol/L); Ka = dissociation constant of the weak acid; (H3O+) = hydrogen ion concentration of the buffer.`},{e:`W = (0.52 - a) / b`,c:`Freezing point depression method. W = grams of adjusting substance per 100 mL; 0.52 = freezing point depression of blood in degrees Celsius; a = freezing point depression produced by the unadjusted drug solution; b = freezing point depression of a 1 per cent w/v solution of the adjusting substance (0.576 for sodium chloride).`},{e:`E = 17 x Liso / M      and      V = w x E x 111.1`,c:`E = sodium chloride equivalent, the weight of sodium chloride that produces the same osmotic effect as 1 g of the drug; Liso = the molar freezing point depression constant for that ionic class; M = molecular weight of the drug. In the White-Vincent equation V = volume in mL of isotonic solution obtainable from w grams of drug; 111.1 is the volume of isotonic solution produced by 1 g of sodium chloride.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Isotonic is not the same as isosmotic`,x:`Isosmotic means the same measured osmotic pressure as blood, a purely physical statement. Isotonic means it does not damage red cells - a biological statement that also depends on whether the solute crosses the cell membrane. A 1.9 per cent w/v urea solution is isosmotic with blood but haemolyses red cells, because urea crosses the membrane freely. Boric acid 1.9 per cent is isosmotic and haemolytic for blood but is safe for the eye.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Hypo swells, hyper shrinks`,x:`Hypotonic - too little solute outside, so water rushes in and the cell bursts (haemolysis). Hypertonic - too much solute outside, so water is pulled out and the cell shrivels (crenation).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Sanity check on E values`,x:`Sodium chloride itself must have E = 1. Check: E = 17 x 3.4 / 58.5 = 57.8 / 58.5 = 0.99. Most drug E values fall between 0.1 and 0.35, so a calculated value far outside that range means an arithmetic slip.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Constants to have by heart`,x:`Freezing point of blood and tears -0.52 C; freezing point depression of 1 per cent w/v sodium chloride 0.576 C; isotonic sodium chloride 0.9 per cent w/v; isotonic dextrose 5.0 per cent w/v; 1 g of sodium chloride makes 111.1 mL of isotonic solution; beta(max) = 0.576 x C.`},{k:`trap`,t:`When the drug is already hypertonic`,x:`If the sodium chloride equivalent contributed by the drug exceeds 0.9 g per 100 mL, no tonicity agent can be added - the solution is already hypertonic and must be diluted, or accepted as hypertonic if the route allows.`}]},"pp-colloids":{f:[{e:`v = d^2 x (rho(s) - rho(l)) x g / (18 x eta)`,c:`Stokes law. v = sedimentation (settling) velocity, cm/s; d = particle diameter, cm; rho(s) and rho(l) = densities of solid and liquid, g/cm3; g = 981 cm/s2; eta = viscosity of the medium, poise. Settling is slowed by reducing d, reducing the density difference, or raising viscosity.`},{e:`V(T) = V(R) + V(A)`,c:`DLVO theory. V(T) = total potential energy of interaction between two particles; V(R) = electrostatic repulsion, falls off exponentially with distance; V(A) = van der Waals attraction, dominant at very short and at long distances. The curve shows a primary maximum (energy barrier), a deep primary minimum (caking, irreversible) and a shallow secondary minimum (loose flocculation, reversible).`},{e:`F = Vu / Vo      and      beta = F / F(infinity)`,c:`F = sedimentation volume = final sediment volume Vu divided by original suspension volume Vo. F ranges from near 0 to 1 and above (F greater than 1 means the sediment swells above the original volume). beta = degree of flocculation = F of the flocculated suspension divided by F(infinity), the sedimentation volume of the same suspension when completely deflocculated.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Stokes law is a squared law`,x:`Halving the particle diameter cuts the settling velocity to one quarter, not one half. This is the single most commonly examined consequence of the equation.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which phase is which`,x:`In O/W the first letter is the internal (dispersed) phase and the last is the external (continuous) phase. O/W means oil droplets floating in water, so it is water-washable, non-greasy and conducts electricity.`},{k:`exam`,t:`What inverts an emulsion`,x:`Adding an electrolyte with a polyvalent cation (calcium chloride to a sodium stearate stabilised O/W emulsion converts it to calcium stearate, a W/O emulsifier), changing the phase volume ratio beyond about 74 per cent, temperature change, or adding an opposite-type emulsifier.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Zeta potential zero is not the goal`,x:`At zero zeta potential (the isoelectric point) attraction is maximal and the suspension crashes into the deep primary minimum, giving a hard cake. Controlled flocculation aims at a small but non-zero zeta potential, around plus or minus 20 mV, so particles sit in the shallow secondary minimum and redisperse.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Numbers worth memorising`,x:`Colloidal size range 1 nm to 0.5 micrometre; maximum theoretical close packing of uniform spheres 74 per cent by volume (above this an emulsion inverts); a good pharmaceutical suspension has F approaching 1; degree of flocculation beta is always greater than 1 for a flocculated system.`}]},"pp-complexation-protein-binding":{f:[{e:`K = slope / [ S0 x (1 - slope) ]`,c:`Stability (formation) constant of a 1:1 complex from a phase-solubility diagram. slope = gradient of the initial linear portion of total drug solubility against ligand concentration; S0 = intrinsic solubility of the drug in the absence of ligand. A larger K means a more stable complex.`},{e:`r = (nu x K x [Df]) / (1 + K x [Df])      Klotz:  1/r = 1 / (nu x K x [Df]) + 1/nu`,c:`r = moles of drug bound per mole of protein; nu = number of binding sites per protein molecule; K = association (binding) constant; [Df] = free drug concentration. The Klotz reciprocal plot of 1/r against 1/[Df] gives intercept 1/nu and slope 1/(nu x K).`},{e:`Scatchard:  r / [Df] = nu x K - r x K`,c:`Scatchard plot of r/[Df] against r: slope = -K, y-intercept = nu x K, and the x-intercept gives nu directly. A curved Scatchard plot indicates more than one class of binding site.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Only free drug works`,x:`A highly bound drug has a small volume of distribution, a long half-life and slow clearance, because the bound fraction is not available for distribution, metabolism or glomerular filtration. It can, however, still be cleared by active tubular secretion, which strips drug off the protein.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Acid to albumin`,x:`Acidic drugs to Albumin (both start with A); basic drugs to alpha-1 acid Glycoprotein.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Equilibrium dialysis in one line`,x:`Plasma (or protein solution) is placed inside a semipermeable membrane bag and buffer outside; free drug crosses until equilibrium, bound drug cannot. The concentration outside is the free concentration, and inside minus outside gives the bound. Watch for the Donnan effect and for drug adsorption to the membrane.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Tissue binding is not protein binding`,x:`Extensive plasma protein binding keeps the drug in the plasma and gives a small volume of distribution (warfarin, about 8 L). Extensive tissue binding pulls the drug out of plasma and gives a very large volume of distribution (digoxin about 500 L, chloroquine over 10,000 L). Do not use one to explain the other.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Complexation numbers`,x:`Higher stability constant means more of the drug is tied up, so complexation for solubility enhancement needs a moderate K - too high and the drug is not released at the absorption site. Iodine with polyvinylpyrrolidone (povidone iodine) is the classic example of a complex used deliberately to hold and slowly release an active.`}]},"pp-kinetics-stability":{f:[{e:`t(90) for first order = 0.105 / k      t(90) for zero order = 0.1 x C0 / k`,c:`Shelf life t(90) is the time for the drug to fall to 90 per cent of its labelled amount. For first order it comes from 2.303/k x log(100/90) = 0.105/k.`},{e:`k = A x e^(-Ea / RT)      log k = log A - Ea / (2.303 x R x T)`,c:`A = frequency (Arrhenius) factor; Ea = activation energy in J/mol; R = 8.314 J/mol/K; T = absolute temperature in kelvin. A plot of log k against 1/T is a straight line of slope -Ea/(2.303 R).`},{e:`log (k2 / k1) = [ Ea / (2.303 x R) ] x [ (T2 - T1) / (T1 x T2) ]`,c:`The two-temperature form used in accelerated stability work: measure k at high temperature, extrapolate to room temperature and hence to shelf life.`},{e:`Q10 = k(T + 10) / k(T)      and      t90(T2) = t90(T1) / Q10 ^ [ (T2 - T1) / 10 ]`,c:`Q10 is the factor by which the rate constant changes for a 10 degree rise. Q10 is 2 for Ea about 12.2 kcal/mol, 3 for about 19.4 kcal/mol and 4 for about 24.5 kcal/mol. Values of 2 and 3 are the ones used in problems.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Zero order does not mean no reaction`,x:`Zero order means the rate is constant and independent of concentration, giving a straight-line fall in concentration. Suspensions of a poorly soluble drug degrade by apparent (pseudo) zero order because the dissolved concentration is held at saturation by the reservoir of undissolved solid.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Hydrolysis is the commonest`,x:`Hydrolysis is the most frequent degradation route for drugs in solution, and esters and amides (including the beta-lactam ring) are its usual victims. If a question gives a drug with an ester group in an aqueous solution, hydrolysis is the answer.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Significant change on accelerated testing`,x:`A 5 per cent loss of assay from the initial value, any degradant exceeding its limit, failure of pH, dissolution or appearance specifications counts as significant change. If it occurs during accelerated testing, intermediate condition testing (30 C / 65 per cent RH) is required.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Solid state and complex kinetics`,x:`Solid-state decomposition often follows a sigmoidal (S-shaped) curve with an induction period, an acceleration phase and a decay phase, and is described by the Prout-Tompkins equation. It cannot be treated with simple solution kinetics.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Half-life language`,x:`For zero order, half-life falls as the drug is used up because it is proportional to C0. For first order, half-life is constant. For second order, half-life is inversely proportional to C0, so a more concentrated solution has a shorter half-life. Confusing these three costs an easy mark.`}]},"pp-micromeritics":{f:[{e:`Porosity (e) = (Vb - Vp) / Vb = 1 - (Vp / Vb) = 1 - (bulk density / true density)`,c:`Vb = bulk volume of the powder bed; Vp = true volume of the particles themselves. Porosity is usually reported as a percentage of void space.`},{e:`d(mean) = [ sum(n x d^(p + f)) / sum(n x d^f) ] ^ (1/p)`,c:`Edmundson general equation. n = number of particles in a size range; d = mid-point diameter of that range; p = an index for the property (1 for length, 2 for surface, 3 for volume); f = a frequency index (0 for number distribution, 3 for volume weighting).`},{e:`Carr index (%) = [ (tapped density - bulk density) / tapped density ] x 100      Hausner ratio = tapped density / bulk density      tan(theta) = h / r`,c:`Carr compressibility index and Hausner ratio use bulk and tapped density (or, equivalently, bulk and tapped volume). In the angle of repose, h = height of the powder heap and r = radius of its base.`},{e:`Sv = 6 / d(vs)      and      Sw = 6 / (rho x d(vs))`,c:`Specific surface area for spherical particles. Sv = surface area per unit volume of particles (cm2/cm3); Sw = surface area per unit weight (cm2/g); rho = true density; d(vs) = volume-surface (Sauter) mean diameter. For non-spherical particles a shape factor is applied.`},{e:`N = 6 / (pi x rho x d(vn)^3)`,c:`Number of particles per gram of powder. rho = true density (g/cm3); d(vn) = volume-number mean diameter (cm). It follows from the fact that one sphere has volume pi x d^3 / 6.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Flow in one sentence`,x:`Good flow means a low angle of repose, a low Carr index and a low Hausner ratio. Everything about powder flow follows from that one relationship.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Tapped over bulk`,x:`Hausner ratio = Tapped divided by Bulk - "T over B". Since tapped is always the larger, the ratio is always above 1; the closer to 1, the better the flow.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which volume goes on the bottom`,x:`Carr index uses tapped density (or the tapped volume equivalent) in the denominator, not bulk. Using bulk in the denominator gives a bigger, wrong number. Hausner ratio, in contrast, puts bulk on the bottom.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Improving flow`,x:`Add a glidant (colloidal silicon dioxide, talc), granulate to make larger spherical particles, remove fines, control moisture, and use spray-dried or directly compressible excipients. Lubricants (magnesium stearate) reduce die wall friction, not flow - keep the two apart.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Number versus weight distributions`,x:`A number distribution is dominated by the many small particles; a weight or volume distribution is dominated by the few large ones. The same powder therefore gives a much larger mean diameter by sieving or Coulter counter than by microscopy. Always state which distribution the mean came from.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Facts that recur`,x:`Specific surface area is inversely proportional to particle diameter. Number of particles per gram is inversely proportional to the cube of the diameter. Porosity of a powder bed is independent of particle size for identical spheres, since it depends only on the packing arrangement.`}]},"pp-rheology":{f:[{e:`eta = F / G = shear stress / shear rate`,c:`Newton law of flow. For a Newtonian liquid eta is a constant at a given temperature, so a plot of shear rate against shear stress is a straight line through the origin.`},{e:`Plastic:  U = (F - f) / G      Pseudoplastic:  F^N = eta x G  (N greater than 1)      Dilatant:  F^N = eta x G  (N less than 1)`,c:`U = plastic viscosity or mobility; f = yield value, the stress that must be exceeded before flow begins; F = shear stress; G = shear rate; N = an index of deviation from Newtonian flow (N = 1 is Newtonian).`},{e:`eta = (pi x r^4 x dP x t) / (8 x l x V)`,c:`Poiseuille equation, the basis of capillary viscometry. r = capillary radius; dP = pressure difference driving flow; t = time of flow; l = capillary length; V = volume flowing. Note the fourth-power dependence on radius.`},{e:`eta1 / eta2 = (rho1 x t1) / (rho2 x t2)`,c:`Working comparison used with an Ostwald viscometer: the unknown liquid (1) and a reference such as water (2) run through the same capillary under their own heads, so viscosity ratio equals the product of density and flow time ratio.`},{e:`eta = eta(0) x (1 + 2.5 x phi)`,c:`Einstein equation for a dilute dispersion of rigid spheres. eta = viscosity of the dispersion; eta(0) = viscosity of the pure continuous phase; phi = volume fraction of the dispersed phase (valid up to about 2 per cent). Viscosity depends on volume fraction, not on particle size.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Pseudoplastic versus dilatant`,x:`Pseudoplastic = shear thinning = gets thinner when you stir (most polymer gels, "shake it and it pours"). Dilatant = shear thickening = gets thicker when you stir (a concentrated starch or zinc oxide slurry that sets solid if stirred fast).`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why we want shear thinning`,x:`A pseudoplastic or plastic vehicle is thick on the shelf (holds particles up), thin during shaking and pouring (dosable), and thick again in the bottle. That combination is the whole reason rheology is on the syllabus.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Thixotropy is not pseudoplasticity`,x:`Pseudoplasticity is shear-rate dependence (thins when sheared harder, recovers instantly). Thixotropy is time dependence (thins when sheared for longer, recovers slowly). A system can be pseudoplastic without being thixotropic, and thixotropy is always superimposed on plastic or pseudoplastic flow.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Plug flow`,x:`In a cup-and-bob viscometer a plastic material near the bob may be sheared while material further away stays unyielded and moves as a solid plug. This falsely lowers the apparent yield value. Cone-and-plate avoids it because the shear rate is uniform throughout the gap.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Units that catch people out`,x:`1 poise = 1 dyne s/cm2 = 0.1 Pa.s, so 1 Pa.s = 10 poise and 1 centipoise = 1 mPa.s. Kinematic viscosity in stokes = viscosity in poise divided by density in g/cm3; 1 stoke = 100 centistokes.`}]},"pp-solubility":{f:[{e:`dC/dt = (D x A) / (h x V) x (Cs - C)`,c:`Noyes-Whitney equation (Nernst-Brunner form). dC/dt = dissolution rate; D = diffusion coefficient of drug; A = effective surface area of the solid; h = thickness of the stagnant diffusion layer; V = volume of dissolution medium; Cs = saturation solubility; C = concentration in bulk at time t.`},{e:`Weak acid:  S = S0 (1 + Ka / [H+])      Weak base:  S = S0 (1 + [H+] / Ka)`,c:`S = total solubility; S0 = intrinsic solubility of the unionised species; Ka = ionisation constant; [H+] = hydrogen ion concentration of the medium.`},{e:`pHp (salt of weak acid) = pKa + log [ (S - S0) / S0 ]`,c:`pHp = the pH below which the free acid precipitates from a solution of its salt. S = concentration of salt in solution; S0 = intrinsic solubility of the free acid. For a salt of a weak base, pHp = pKw - pKb + log [ S0 / (S - S0) ] and precipitation occurs above this pH.`},{e:`log S(mix) = f1 log S1 + f2 log S2 + ... + fn log Sn`,c:`Log-linear cosolvency model. S(mix) = solubility in the blend; f = volume fraction of each solvent; S = solubility of the drug in that pure solvent. Solubility rises roughly exponentially with cosolvent fraction.`},{e:`P = C(oil) / C(water)      and      log P = log [ C(oil) / C(water) ]`,c:`P = partition (distribution) coefficient at equilibrium between two immiscible phases, conventionally n-octanol and water. Higher log P means more lipophilic. Solutes distribute in a fixed ratio at constant temperature - the Nernst distribution law.`},{e:`log Xi = - (dHf / 2.303 R) x [ (T0 - T) / (T0 x T) ]`,c:`Ideal solubility (van’t Hoff) equation. Xi = ideal solubility as mole fraction; dHf = molar heat of fusion (J/mol); R = 8.314 J/mol/K; T0 = melting point of the solute in kelvin; T = experimental temperature in kelvin. Ideal solubility depends only on the solid, never on the solvent.`},{e:`- log gamma2 = (V2 x phi1^2 / 2.303 RT) x (d1 - d2)^2`,c:`Regular (Hildebrand) solution theory. gamma2 = activity coefficient of solute; V2 = molar volume of solute as a liquid; phi1 = volume fraction of solvent; d1 and d2 = solubility parameters of solvent and solute. Solubility is maximum when d1 = d2, that is when the two solubility parameters match.`},{e:`K(true) = C(oil, monomer) / C(water)      K(apparent) = C(oil, total) / C(water)`,c:`Benzoic acid dimerises in benzene, so the apparent partition coefficient measured from total oil-phase concentration overestimates the true value and is not constant with concentration. Correcting for the degree of dimerisation restores a constant K.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Remember the ladder by its numbers`,x:`The boundaries run 1, 10, 30, 100, 1000, 10,000. Only one boundary is not a power of ten - the 30 that separates soluble from sparingly soluble. Fix that one and the rest fall into place.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Solubility is not dissolution rate`,x:`Solubility is an equilibrium value (how much). Dissolution rate is a kinetic value (how fast). Micronising a drug does not appreciably change its equilibrium solubility but it multiplies surface area A and therefore the dissolution rate.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The Noyes-Whitney terms you must be able to attack`,x:`A formulator cannot change D or h much. The two workable handles are A (increase by micronisation, wetting agents, effervescence) and Cs (increase by salt formation, metastable polymorph, amorphous form, cosolvent, complexation). Sink conditions mean C is kept below 15 to 20 per cent of Cs so the rate stays maximal.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Which polymorph goes into the tablet`,x:`The metastable polymorph dissolves faster but converts on storage; the stable polymorph is the least soluble but the safest. Amorphous chloramphenicol palmitate (form B) is absorbed, the stable crystalline form A is essentially inactive orally - a classic asked pair.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Solubility of gases and the small print`,x:`Gas solubility follows Henry law: solubility is proportional to partial pressure. Gas solubility falls with rising temperature and with added electrolyte (salting out), which is why water for injection is boiled and cooled under carbon dioxide free conditions before dissolving oxygen-sensitive drugs.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Salting in and salting out`,x:`Salting out is the fall in solubility of a non-electrolyte caused by an added electrolyte that competes for water of hydration (sodium chloride precipitating a protein). Salting in is the opposite, seen with large anions such as benzoate, salicylate and iodide, which open water structure and raise solubility.`}]},"pp-surface":{f:[{e:`HLB = 20 x (Mh / M)      and for polyhydric alcohol fatty acid esters:  HLB = 20 x (1 - S/A)`,c:`Griffin scale. Mh = molecular weight of the hydrophilic portion; M = total molecular weight; S = saponification value of the ester; A = acid value of the recovered fatty acid. Davies method: HLB = 7 + sum of hydrophilic group numbers - sum of lipophilic group numbers.`},{e:`HLB (blend) = fA x HLB(A) + fB x HLB(B),  where fA + fB = 1`,c:`Blending rule. f = weight fraction of each surfactant in the mixed emulsifier. Solve for fA when the required HLB of the oil phase is known.`},{e:`Gamma = - (1 / RT) x (d gamma / d ln C) = - (C / RT) x (d gamma / dC)`,c:`Gibbs adsorption equation. Gamma (capital) = surface excess concentration, mol/cm2; gamma (small) = surface tension; C = bulk concentration; R = gas constant; T = absolute temperature. A negative d gamma/dC (tension falls as concentration rises) gives a positive surface excess, that is positive adsorption.`},{e:`S = gamma(S) - [ gamma(L) + gamma(LS) ]      equivalently  S = Wa - Wc`,c:`S = spreading coefficient of liquid L on sublayer S; gamma(S) = surface tension of the sublayer; gamma(L) = surface tension of the spreading liquid; gamma(LS) = interfacial tension between them. Wa = work of adhesion = gamma(L) + gamma(S) - gamma(LS); Wc = work of cohesion of the spreading liquid = 2 x gamma(L).`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`What happens exactly at the CMC`,x:`Surface tension stops falling and plateaus, turbidity and light scattering rise sharply, solubilisation of water-insoluble material begins, osmotic pressure levels off, and equivalent conductivity of an ionic surfactant drops abruptly. A break in any of these plots against concentration marks the CMC.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Adsorption versus absorption`,x:`Adsorption is a surface phenomenon (ad = at the surface, molecules stick on). Absorption is bulk uptake (throughout). Sorption is when both happen together.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Capillary rise cannot do interfaces`,x:`Capillary rise is the most accurate method but it is restricted to liquid-air surface tension and needs a zero contact angle. If the question says interfacial tension between two liquids, capillary rise is the wrong answer - choose the du Nouy ring, drop weight or Wilhelmy plate.`},{k:`exam`,t:`A blend beats a single surfactant`,x:`A mixture of a high-HLB and a low-HLB emulsifier giving the required HLB produces a more stable, more closely packed interfacial film than a single surfactant of the same HLB. Tween 80 with Span 80 is the classic pair.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Wetting is a contact angle question`,x:`Young equation: gamma(S) = gamma(SL) + gamma(L) cos theta. Complete wetting is theta = 0 degrees; no wetting is theta = 180 degrees; a powder with theta above 90 degrees floats. Wetting agents (HLB 7 to 9) work by lowering gamma(L) and gamma(SL), thereby lowering theta - they do not change the solid.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Adsorption at solid surfaces`,x:`Physisorption is weak (van der Waals), reversible, multilayer, low heat of adsorption (under 40 kJ/mol) and increases as temperature falls. Chemisorption is strong, specific, monolayer, irreversible, with a high heat of adsorption. Activated charcoal in poisoning is physisorption.`}]},"pr-clinical-services":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Clinical pharmacy versus hospital pharmacy`,x:`Hospital pharmacy is a place and a set of functions including purchase, storage, manufacture and dispensing. Clinical pharmacy is an orientation, practised wherever the patient is, and its output is a recommendation about therapy rather than a dispensed product.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Warfarin is not monitored by concentration`,x:`Warfarin is monitored by its effect, the international normalised ratio, usually targeted at 2 to 3. If a question lists warfarin among drugs requiring plasma concentration monitoring, that is the distractor; the same logic applies to heparin, which is followed by APTT.`},{k:`trap`,t:`PubMed is secondary, Micromedex is tertiary`,x:`A database that gives you citations and abstracts so that you can find articles is secondary. A database that gives you a written, evaluated answer is tertiary. A review article is tertiary even though it is printed in a journal, because it summarises other work rather than reporting new work.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Reference ranges are laboratory-specific`,x:`Always interpret a value against the range printed by the reporting laboratory and against the clinical picture. In the exam, choose the option nearest the conventional textbook range; in practice, a value just outside the range in a well patient is rarely by itself an indication to act.`}]},"pr-pharmacovigilance":{f:[],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Serious is not severe`,x:`Severity describes intensity: a severe headache is very painful but is not a serious event. Seriousness is a regulatory category defined by outcome, that is death, threat to life, hospitalisation, disability or congenital anomaly. A mild rash that leads to admission is serious; a severe headache that does not is not.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`A to F`,x:`Augmented, Bizarre, Chronic, Delayed, End of use, Failure. Type A is too much of the expected effect; type B is something the pharmacology never predicted.`},{k:`exam`,t:`How the two scales differ`,x:`WHO-UMC is a category-based assessment resting on expert judgement of the whole case; Naranjo is a numerical algorithm whose total score decides the category. Both use dechallenge and rechallenge as the strongest evidence, and rechallenge is rarely done because it deliberately re-exposes the patient to harm.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The recurring one-liners`,x:`Phase I is healthy volunteers and safety; phase III is the confirmatory comparison that supports approval; phase IV is post-marketing. Naranjo score 9 or more is definite. PvPI is coordinated by the Indian Pharmacopoeia Commission, Ghaziabad. Spontaneous reports cannot give incidence because there is no denominator.`}]},"pt-aerosols":{f:[{e:`da = dg x (rho / rho0 x 1/chi)^0.5`,c:`Aerodynamic diameter da relates to the geometric diameter dg through the particle density rho (relative to unit density rho0 of 1 g/cm3) and the dynamic shape factor chi. A porous low density particle can be geometrically large yet aerodynamically small.`},{e:`Pa = Pa(pure) x mole fraction of A     (Raoult law)`,c:`The partial vapour pressure of a propellant in a blend equals its pure vapour pressure times its mole fraction. The total pressure in the can is the sum of the partial pressures, which is how a blend of propellant 12 and 114 is designed to a target pressure.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Particle size decides where the drug lands`,x:`Particles of 1 to 5 micrometres aerodynamic diameter deposit in the bronchi and alveoli, which is the therapeutic target. Above about 10 micrometres they impact in the mouth and throat and are swallowed. Below about 0.5 micrometres they stay suspended and are simply breathed out again.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Aerosol components`,x:`C-P-C-V-A: Container, Propellant, Concentrate, Valve, Actuator. Five components, in the order you would build the can.`},{k:`exam`,t:`What changed when MDIs moved to HFA`,x:`HFAs are poor solvents for the traditional sorbitan and lecithin surfactants, so ethanol had to be added as a cosolvent and new surfactants or solution formulations were developed. The plume is warmer and softer, the taste is different, and some solution HFA products give a much finer particle size and therefore higher lung deposition at the same nominal dose. Doses are not interchangeable between CFC and HFA products.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Spacers solve coordination, not everything`,x:`A spacer removes the need to synchronise actuation with inhalation and traps large particles that would otherwise hit the throat, which cuts oral candidiasis with inhaled steroids. It does not help a dry powder inhaler, which needs the patient inspiratory flow to disperse the powder in the first place.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why compressed gas systems behave differently`,x:`A liquefied propellant maintains constant pressure throughout the life of the can, because vapour is replenished from the liquid reservoir as it is used. A compressed gas system has no reservoir, so pressure falls steadily as the container empties, the spray becomes coarser and wetter, and the last portion may not expel at all.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Carrier lactose is not inert bulk`,x:`In a dry powder inhaler the micronised drug is blended with coarse lactose so that the mixture flows and can be metered. The drug must then detach from the carrier during inhalation. Lactose surface roughness, fines content and the drug to carrier adhesion force therefore control the fine particle fraction directly, so a change of lactose grade is a formulation change, not an excipient substitution.`}]},"pt-capsules":{f:[{e:`Fill weight (mg) = capsule body volume (mL) x tapped density (g/mL) x 1000`,c:`Used to select a capsule size. The tapped or plug density is used rather than bulk density because both dosator and tamping machines consolidate the powder before ejecting it.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Where gelatin comes from`,x:`Gelatin is obtained by the partial hydrolysis of collagen from animal skin, bone and connective tissue. Type A is produced by acid hydrolysis, usually of pork skin, with an isoelectric point around pH 7 to 9. Type B is produced by alkaline (lime) hydrolysis, usually of bone, with an isoelectric point around pH 4.7 to 5.2.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Capsule size direction`,x:`Numbers go up as the capsule goes down. 5 is the tiniest, 000 the biggest. Read the zeros as "extra large".`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why basket and not paddle for capsules`,x:`A gelatin capsule shell is less dense than the medium, so it floats up against the vessel wall under a paddle and dissolution becomes erratic. The basket holds it in the flow. If a paddle must be used, a sinker is added, and the sinker type has to be specified because it changes the profile.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Pre-lock versus final lock`,x:`Hard capsules carry two sets of indentations. The pre-lock position keeps the empty capsule assembled for transport without gripping the body tightly, so the filling machine can separate the halves. The final lock, engaged after filling, prevents accidental opening.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Choosing the size is a density problem, not a weight problem`,x:`Two powders of the same 300 mg dose may need different capsule sizes if their densities differ. A light, fluffy powder of tapped density 0.4 g/mL needs about 0.75 mL and therefore size 0, while a dense powder at 0.9 g/mL needs only about 0.33 mL and fits a size 3.`}]},"pt-liquid-orals":{f:[{e:`v = d^2 (rho(s) - rho(l)) g / (18 x eta)`,c:`Stokes law. v is the sedimentation velocity in cm/s, d the particle diameter in cm, rho(s) and rho(l) the densities of solid and liquid in g/cm3, g the acceleration due to gravity and eta the viscosity of the medium in poise.`},{e:`F = Vu / Vo     and     beta = F / F(infinity)`,c:`F is the sedimentation volume, the ratio of the final sediment volume Vu to the original suspension volume Vo. Beta is the degree of flocculation, comparing F with the sedimentation volume of the deflocculated suspension. F equal to 1 is ideal.`},{e:`Schulze-Hardy: flocculating power increases sharply with counter-ion valency`,c:`A trivalent counter-ion such as aluminium is far more effective at flocculating a negatively charged sol than a divalent or monovalent ion at the same concentration. The critical coagulation concentration falls steeply as valency rises.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Why a saturated syrup needs no preservative`,x:`At about 65 percent w/w sucrose the water in the syrup is so heavily bound as sucrose hydrate that the water activity is too low for microbial growth. A syrup that is diluted, or one where some sucrose has been replaced, loses that self-preserving property and must have a preservative added.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Elixir versus syrup versus linctus`,x:`All three are sweet liquids, but an elixir always contains alcohol and is always a clear solution, a syrup is aqueous and sugar-based, and a linctus is defined by its viscosity and its purpose, which is to coat the throat. A sugar free elixir still contains alcohol; a sugar free syrup does not.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Creaming is not cracking`,x:`Creaming is the upward or downward movement of droplets under gravity, and it is reversible by shaking. Cracking, also called breaking, is coalescence of the droplets into a separate continuous layer, and shaking will not restore it. Phase inversion is a third failure, where an oil in water emulsion turns into water in oil, usually because the emulsifier type changed or the phase volume exceeded about 74 percent.`},{k:`exam`,t:`DLVO in one line`,x:`The total interaction energy is the sum of electrical repulsion and van der Waals attraction. The primary minimum at very short distance is deep and irreversible (caking); the secondary minimum at larger distance is shallow and reversible (flocculation). Good suspension design keeps particles in the secondary minimum.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Sedimentation volume calculation`,x:`A 100 mL suspension leaves a sediment of 30 mL after standing. F = Vu/Vo = 30/100 = 0.3. If the deflocculated control gives 15 mL, then F(infinity) = 0.15 and the degree of flocculation beta = 0.3/0.15 = 2, meaning the sediment volume has doubled through flocculation.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Alcohol strength in an elixir`,x:`To make 500 mL of an elixir containing 20 percent v/v alcohol from 90 percent v/v alcohol: alcohol needed = 500 x 20/100 = 100 mL of absolute equivalent; volume of 90 percent alcohol = 100 x 100/90 = 111.1 mL. The rest of the vehicle makes up to 500 mL.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Why a suspension should be pseudoplastic and thixotropic, not Newtonian`,x:`On the shelf you want a high apparent viscosity so particles do not settle. When the bottle is shaken and the dose is poured you want low viscosity. Only a shear thinning system gives both, and thixotropy adds the useful delay in structure recovery that keeps the product pourable while it is being measured.`}]},"pt-ophthalmics":{f:[{e:`E = 17 x Liso / M`,c:`E is the sodium chloride equivalent, Liso the molar freezing point depression constant for the ionic class (1.9 for non-electrolytes, 3.4 for uni-univalent electrolytes, 4.3 for uni-divalent, 5.2 for di-univalent) and M the molecular weight in g/mol.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The tonicity range the eye tolerates`,x:`The eye is comfortable with solutions equivalent to 0.9 percent w/v sodium chloride, and tolerates roughly 0.5 to 1.8 percent w/v equivalents without pain. Tears freeze at minus 0.52 degrees C, the same as blood, and their normal pH is about 7.4.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`The five checks on an eye drop`,x:`S-T-P-C-V: Sterility, Tonicity, pH, Clarity, Viscosity. Run through those five and you have covered the formulation requirements.`},{k:`exam`,t:`When preservative is prohibited`,x:`No preservative is permitted in intraocular solutions, in preparations for use during eye surgery, or in products applied to an eye whose cornea has been injured. These must be supplied in single dose, single use containers. Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection of an abraded cornea can destroy the eye within 48 hours, which is why sterility here matters more than convenience.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Eye lotions and irrigations are not eye drops`,x:`Large volume eye lotions and intraocular irrigating solutions carry no preservative at all and must be discarded within 24 hours of opening. Only small multi-dose drop containers are preserved.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Differential solubility of the cornea`,x:`Because the epithelium is lipid and the stroma is aqueous, a purely hydrophilic drug is stopped at the epithelium and a purely lipophilic one is stopped at the stroma. Drugs that exist in equilibrium between ionised and unionised forms, such as pilocarpine, penetrate best.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Worked isotonicity numerical`,x:`Make 30 mL of 1 percent w/v pilocarpine nitrate drops isotonic. The sodium chloride equivalent of pilocarpine nitrate is 0.23. NaCl represented by the drug is 0.23 x 1 = 0.23 g per 100 mL. NaCl still required is 0.9 minus 0.23 = 0.67 g per 100 mL, that is 0.67 x 30/100 = 0.201 g, about 201 mg for the 30 mL batch.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Viscosity has diminishing returns`,x:`Raising viscosity increases contact time only up to about 20 to 25 mPa s. Beyond that the reflex blink rate rises and lacrimation increases, so the extra drug is washed away faster and vision is blurred. More viscosity is not automatically more bioavailability.`}]},"pt-packaging":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Why glass is graded at all`,x:`Ordinary soda-lime glass releases sodium ions into an aqueous product, raising the pH and possibly degrading the drug. Boric oxide in borosilicate glass ties up that alkali in the network, which is why Type I is the only glass permitted for alkaline and highly sensitive injections.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`The functions of packaging`,x:`C-P-P-C: Contain, Protect, Present, Convenience. Anything a package does falls under one of those four.`},{k:`trap`,t:`The five problems of plastic containers`,x:`Permeation of gases and moisture through the wall; leaching of plasticisers, stabilisers and antioxidants into the product; sorption of drug or preservative onto the plastic; chemical reaction between product and polymer; and alteration of the physical properties of the container itself, such as panelling and stress cracking. Sorption of preservative is why a preservative efficacy test must be repeated in the final container.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Maximum allowable leakage limit`,x:`Microbial ingress becomes a real risk at a defect size of roughly 0.2 to 0.3 micrometres and above, which is why a sterile product package is normally required to have no leak path larger than that. A test method must be shown capable of detecting a defect at or below the maximum allowable leakage limit set for the product.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Cold-formed Alu-Alu blisters trade visibility for barrier`,x:`Cold forming stretches an aluminium laminate into a cavity, giving an almost perfect barrier to moisture, oxygen and light. The price is that the pack is opaque, the cavities are larger and shallower so fewer fit on a card, and the process is slower than thermoforming PVC.`}]},"pt-parenterals":{f:[{e:`Sodium chloride required (g per 100 mL) = 0.9 - (E x C)`,c:`E is the sodium chloride equivalent of the drug, the weight of NaCl with the same osmotic effect as 1 g of drug. C is the drug concentration in g per 100 mL. The answer is the extra NaCl needed to make the solution isotonic.`},{e:`W = (0.52 - a) / b`,c:`Freezing point method. W is grams of adjusting substance per 100 mL, 0.52 is the freezing point depression of blood and tears in degrees C, a is the depression produced by the drug already present, and b is the depression produced by 1 percent w/v of the adjusting substance (0.576 for sodium chloride).`},{e:`Endotoxin limit = K / M`,c:`K is the threshold pyrogenic dose, 5 EU per kg body weight for most routes and 0.2 EU per kg for the intrathecal route. M is the maximum human dose per kg administered in one hour. The result is in EU per mg or EU per mL.`},{e:`F0 = sum of 10^((T - 121)/z) x delta t`,c:`F0 is the equivalent time in minutes at 121 degrees C delivered to the product. T is the product temperature in degrees C, z is 10 degrees C for moist heat, and delta t is the time interval in minutes. An F0 of at least 8 to 12 minutes is normally required for terminal sterilisation.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Only IV gives 100 percent bioavailability`,x:`There is no absorption step, so the whole dose reaches the systemic circulation immediately. That is also why an IV dose cannot be recalled and why IV products must never contain particles or air.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Preservatives are forbidden in some injections`,x:`No antimicrobial preservative may be used in single dose products for the intrathecal, intracisternal, epidural or intraocular route, nor in any injection intended to be given in a volume above 15 mL, nor in a large volume parenteral.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`What every injection must be`,x:`S-P-P-I: Sterile, Pyrogen free, Particle free, Isotonic where possible. Four words that cover most of the quality control chapter.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Glass type selection for injections`,x:`Type I borosilicate for all parenterals, and mandatory for alkaline or highly sensitive products. Type II surface-treated soda-lime for acidic and neutral aqueous parenterals. Type III soda-lime for non-aqueous parenterals and for powders for injection. Type NP is for oral and topical use only, never for injections.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Worked endotoxin limit`,x:`For a drug whose maximum dose is 10 mg per kg in one hour by the IV route, the limit is 5 divided by 10, which is 0.5 EU per mg of drug. If the injection contains 20 mg/mL, the solution limit is 10 EU per mL.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Eutectic point versus glass transition`,x:`A crystalline solute such as sodium chloride has a eutectic point, the temperature at which the last liquid solidifies, and the product must be frozen below it. An amorphous solute such as sucrose has no eutectic point; it has a glass transition temperature of the maximally freeze-concentrated solution, and collapse occurs just above it. Applying the eutectic rule to an amorphous formulation is a common error.`}]},"pt-preformulation":{f:[{e:`P = C(oil) / C(water)   and   log P = log10 P`,c:`P is the partition coefficient, usually measured in n-octanol and water at 25 degrees C. C(oil) and C(water) are the equilibrium concentrations of the unionised drug in each phase.`},{e:`pH = pKa + log ([ionised] / [unionised])   for a weak acid`,c:`Henderson-Hasselbalch. For a weak base the ratio inverts: pH = pKa + log ([unionised] / [ionised]). At pH equal to pKa the drug is 50 percent ionised.`},{e:`dC/dt = D A (Cs - C) / (h V)`,c:`Noyes-Whitney. D is diffusion coefficient (cm2/s), A the effective surface area (cm2), Cs the saturation solubility, C the bulk concentration, h the diffusion layer thickness (cm) and V the dissolution medium volume (mL).`},{e:`IDR = (dm/dt) / A   in mg min-1 cm-2`,c:`Intrinsic dissolution rate, measured on a compressed disc of constant surface area in a Wood apparatus. dm/dt is mass dissolved per minute; A is the exposed disc area.`},{e:`CRH(AB) = CRH(A) x CRH(B) / 100`,c:`Elder equation for the critical relative humidity of a mixture of two water-soluble salts that do not interact. CRH values are percentages. The mixture CRH is always lower than either component, so blends pick up moisture more readily than either powder alone.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The single most asked preformulation idea`,x:`A drug must dissolve before it can be absorbed. So anything that raises dissolution rate (smaller particles, a more soluble salt, a metastable polymorph, an amorphous form) usually raises oral bioavailability, and anything that lowers it does the opposite.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`What preformulation looks at`,x:`S-P-P-P-S: Solubility, pKa, Partition coefficient, Polymorphism, Stability. Get these five and you can already sketch a dosage form.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The classic polymorphism example`,x:`Chloramphenicol palmitate exists as forms A, B and C plus an amorphous form. Only the metastable form B gives useful blood levels; the stable form A is essentially inactive because it does not dissolve fast enough for the esterase to act. Ritonavir is the famous industrial disaster, where a late-appearing, less soluble form II forced the product off the market.`},{k:`trap`,t:`DSC alone is not proof`,x:`A shifted or broadened melting endotherm in a DSC thermogram only suggests an interaction; simple eutectic formation or dissolution of the drug in a molten excipient gives the same picture without any chemical change. A positive DSC finding must always be confirmed by isothermal stress testing with chromatographic assay.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Metastable does not mean usable`,x:`A metastable polymorph gives a real bioavailability gain only if it survives processing and shelf life. Milling, wet granulation, drying and compression all supply the energy and the moisture that trigger conversion back to the stable form, which then shows up as a slowing dissolution profile on stability.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Worked CRH point`,x:`If salt A has CRH 80 percent and salt B has CRH 70 percent, the mixture has CRH = 80 x 70 / 100 = 56 percent. A room controlled at 60 percent RH is safe for either powder alone but will make the blend damp.`}]},"pt-semisolids":{f:[{e:`Base required = (N x M) - (N x D / DV)`,c:`N is the number of suppositories, M the mould calibration weight of pure base in g, D the weight of drug per suppository in g and DV the displacement value. The answer is grams of base to weigh out.`},{e:`DV = (b x x / 100) / [a - b + (b x x / 100)]`,c:`Determining an unknown displacement value. a is the weight of six suppositories of pure base in g, b the weight of six suppositories containing x percent w/w of the drug, and x the percentage of drug. The numerator is the weight of drug in the six units.`},{e:`Q = [D Cs (2A - Cs) t]^0.5`,c:`Higuchi equation for release from an ointment in which the drug is suspended. Q is the amount released per unit area, D the diffusion coefficient in the base, Cs the drug solubility in the base, A the total drug concentration and t the time. A plot of Q against the square root of t is linear.`},{e:`Required HLB = sum (fraction of each oil x its required HLB)`,c:`Used when blending emulsifiers for a cream. Fractions are of the total oil phase. A blend of a high and a low HLB surfactant is then made to hit the required value: 3 to 6 gives water in oil, 8 to 18 gives oil in water.`}],c:[{k:`mnemonic`,t:`The four base classes`,x:`O-A-W-W: Oleaginous, Absorption, Water-removable, Water-soluble. The order also runs from greasiest to least greasy, and from least washable to most washable.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Why a paste is not an ointment`,x:`A paste carries a much higher proportion of insoluble powder, typically 20 to 50 percent. That makes it stiffer, less greasy, less occlusive and more absorptive of exudate, and it stays exactly where it is put. Pastes are therefore protective, used on weeping lesions and as a barrier around ulcers.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Worked displacement value numerical`,x:`Prepare 12 suppositories, each 1 g mould, containing 300 mg zinc oxide (DV 4.7). Total drug = 12 x 0.3 = 3.6 g. Base displaced by that drug = 3.6 / 4.7 = 0.766 g. Base required = (12 x 1) - 0.766 = 11.234 g. In practice you calculate for 14 or 15 units to allow for losses in the mould.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Carbomer needs neutralisation`,x:`Carbomer (Carbopol 934, 940, 941) is dispersed in water as an acidic, low viscosity slurry. It only gels when neutralised to about pH 6 to 7 with triethanolamine or sodium hydroxide, which ionises the carboxyl groups and makes the coiled polymer chains uncoil by charge repulsion. Excess electrolyte or a strongly cationic drug will collapse the gel again.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Dilution of creams is not harmless`,x:`Diluting a preserved cream lowers the preservative below its effective concentration and can also break the emulsion by changing the phase volume ratio. Diluents must be specified by the manufacturer, prepared with boiled cooled water, and the diluted product given a short expiry, typically two to four weeks.`}]},"pt-tablet-coating":{f:[{e:`Coat weight gain (percent) = [(Wc - Wu) / Wu] x 100`,c:`Wu is the mean weight of uncoated tablets and Wc the mean weight after coating, both in mg. This is the routine in-process control for a coating run.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Why enteric coating exists`,x:`Two reasons only: to protect an acid-labile drug such as omeprazole or erythromycin from gastric acid, and to protect the stomach from an irritant drug such as aspirin or diclofenac. It is delayed release, not sustained release.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Film coating formula`,x:`P-P-C-S: Polymer, Plasticiser, Colourant, Solvent. Four ingredients, and only the solvent leaves.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Sugar coating order`,x:`Seal, Sub, Smooth, Colour, Polish, Print. The first three all begin with S and come before any colour is added.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Blushing versus blooming`,x:`Blushing is a whitish haze appearing during coating because the polymer precipitates at too high a temperature. Blooming is a dull, hazy film that develops on storage, usually because a low molecular weight plasticiser migrates to the surface. Same look, different timing and cause.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Minimum film forming temperature`,x:`An aqueous polymeric dispersion will only coalesce above its minimum film forming temperature, which is close to the plasticised glass transition temperature. Coating below MFFT gives a porous, non-continuous film and a sustained release product whose dissolution keeps drifting on stability.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Compression coating is not film coating`,x:`Compression coating uses no solvent at all. A preformed core is centred in a die and coating granulation is compressed around it on a special press such as the Drycota. It suits moisture sensitive drugs and physically separates incompatible ingredients, but core centring is difficult and any off-centre core gives a thin wall that fails.`}]},"pt-tablets":{f:[{e:`ln (1 / (1 - D)) = K P + A     (Heckel equation)`,c:`D is the relative density of the compact at applied pressure P (MPa). K is the slope; its reciprocal is the mean yield pressure Py. A low Py means the material deforms plastically at low pressure.`},{e:`Dissolution efficiency = (area under the dissolution curve up to time t / (100 x t)) x 100`,c:`DE is expressed as a percentage of the rectangle described by 100 percent dissolution over the same time. It compares whole profiles rather than a single time point.`},{e:`f2 = 50 x log { [1 + (1/n) x sum(Rt - Tt)^2]^-0.5 x 100 }`,c:`Similarity factor. Rt and Tt are percent dissolved for reference and test at each of n time points. Values of f2 between 50 and 100 mean the profiles are similar.`}],c:[{k:`trap`,t:`Lubricant, glidant, antiadherent are three different jobs`,x:`A lubricant reduces die wall friction so the tablet ejects; a glidant reduces particle to particle friction so the powder flows; an antiadherent stops material sticking to the punch tip. Talc and magnesium stearate can do more than one, which is exactly why examiners use them as distractors. The definition, not the substance, is the answer.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`The three strokes of a press`,x:`F-C-E: Fill, Compress, Eject. Every tablet press, single punch or rotary, is just those three strokes repeated.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Work hardening in dry granulation`,x:`Material compacted once loses some of its ability to bond a second time, because the plastic deformation capacity has already been used. That is why roller compacted granules often give softer tablets than wet granulated ones at the same compression force.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Friability sample size`,x:`For tablets weighing 650 mg or less, take a sample of whole tablets nearest to 6.5 g. For tablets above 650 mg, take 10 tablets. Any tablet that caps, cracks or splits during the test fails outright, whatever the weight loss.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Hardness is not crushing strength divided by anything`,x:`Tablet hardness readings from different testers are not interchangeable. Strong-Cobb units are roughly 1.4 times Monsanto kilogram readings for the same tablet, so a specification must name the instrument. Tensile strength, calculated as 2F divided by pi times diameter times thickness, is the instrument-independent quantity.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Magnesium stearate, the classic double-edged excipient`,x:`It is the most effective common lubricant but it is hydrophobic and forms a film over granule surfaces. Over-blending, typically beyond about five minutes, lowers tablet hardness, slows disintegration and retards dissolution. This is why the lubricant is always added last and blended for the shortest validated time.`}]},"qa-documentation-qms":{f:[{e:`Six sigma target = not more than 3.4 defects per million opportunities`,c:`A six sigma process keeps six standard deviations between the process mean and the nearest specification limit; the 3.4 figure allows for the conventional 1.5 sigma long-term shift of the mean.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Correct GMP record-keeping habits`,x:`Write in indelible ink at the time of the activity, never in pencil and never in advance. To correct an entry, strike it through with a single line so the original stays readable, write the correct value, then sign and date the change. Never overwrite, erase or use correction fluid.`},{k:`trap`,t:`You may not average an OOS away`,x:`Averaging a failing result with passing results to bring the mean into specification, or retesting until a passing result appears, is testing into compliance and is unacceptable. The original result stands unless a genuine assignable cause is documented. An out-of-trend result is different: it is within specification but drifting from the historical pattern, and it too must be investigated.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`ICH letters`,x:`QSEM: Quality, Safety, Efficacy, Multidisciplinary. Safety is the animals, Efficacy is the humans, Quality is the product, Multidisciplinary is everything left over.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The distinctions that carry marks`,x:`ISO 9001 is the certifiable standard; corrective action follows an event while preventive action anticipates one; internal audit is also called self-inspection; the MFR has no batch number and the BMR does.`}]},"qa-gmp":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`One-line discriminators`,x:`QC tests the product; QA builds the system that makes testing predictable. QC is a department; QA is a philosophy that runs through every department. No batch may be released by QC alone: release is a QA responsibility.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The three clean room numbers`,x:`0.3 micrometre and 99.97 per cent for HEPA efficiency, 20 air changes per hour for grade C and D, and 10 to 15 pascals between adjacent grades. These three come up again and again.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Accelerated data do not set the shelf-life`,x:`Accelerated studies at 40 degrees C / 75 per cent RH support a limited extrapolation and cover short-term excursions during shipping, but the labelled shelf-life must be justified by real-time long-term data. Six months of accelerated data can never by itself justify a two-year expiry.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Frequently confused pairs`,x:`Q7 is GMP for APIs, not for finished products. Q9 is risk management and Q10 is the quality system. E6 is good clinical practice. M4 is the Common Technical Document format for submissions.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Schedule M is not the only schedule that matters`,x:`Schedule M covers GMP for drugs; separate schedules cover other ground, notably Schedule Y for clinical trials and new drug approval, and Schedule U for the manufacturing and analytical records that must be maintained. Do not answer Schedule M for a question about clinical trial requirements.`}]},"qa-validation":{f:[{e:`LOD = 3.3 x (sigma / S)     and     LOQ = 10 x (sigma / S)`,c:`sigma = standard deviation of the response (of the blank, of the y-intercept, or of the residuals of the regression line); S = slope of the calibration curve. The only difference between the two formulae is the multiplying factor, 3.3 versus 10, so LOQ is about three times LOD.`},{e:`MACO = (limit per dose of previous product x minimum batch size of next product) / (maximum daily dose of next product)`,c:`MACO = maximum allowable carryover of the previous product into the whole next batch, in the same mass units. Dividing MACO by the total shared equipment surface area gives the permitted residue per square centimetre, which is what the swab result is compared against.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Validation or qualification?`,x:`Qualification applies to equipment, instruments, utilities and facilities; validation applies to processes, methods, cleaning and computer systems. Equipment is qualified so that a process can be validated. Calibration, by contrast, compares an instrument reading against a traceable standard.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`DIOP, in order`,x:`Design it, Install it, Operate it, Perform with it. DQ before purchase, IQ on delivery, OQ empty, PQ with product.`},{k:`trap`,t:`LOD versus LOQ in words`,x:`At the LOD you can say the analyte is present but you cannot put a number on it. At the LOQ you can report a figure with acceptable accuracy and precision. Both come from the same slope and standard deviation; only the factor changes.`},{k:`exam`,t:`Accuracy versus precision`,x:`Accuracy is how close you are to the true value; precision is how close repeated results are to one another. A method can be precise but consistently wrong. Accuracy is reported as per cent recovery, precision as per cent RSD.`},{k:`exam`,t:`High-yield answers`,x:`Three consecutive batches for prospective validation; DQ, IQ, OQ, PQ in that order; OQ is done without product and PQ with product; LOD = 3.3 sigma/S and LOQ = 10 sigma/S; the 10 ppm and one-thousandth-of-a-dose rules for cleaning.`}]},"sp-national-programmes":{f:[],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`The population norms are free marks`,x:`5000 for a sub-centre, 30 000 for a PHC and 120 000 for a CHC in the plains, each reduced to 3000, 20 000 and 80 000 in hilly and tribal areas. One ASHA per 1000 rural population.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Names change; targets do not`,x:`Programme names in India are revised frequently, so an old textbook and a new one may disagree. Answer with the disease and the strategy, and give both names where you know them. Do not attach a confident year to a renaming unless you are certain of it.`},{k:`exam`,t:`High-yield facts from this topic`,x:`DOTS means directly observed treatment, short course. Cataract is the leading cause of blindness in India. Leprosy elimination means prevalence below one per 10 000. NCD screening starts at age 30. The NDPS Act of 1985 governs narcotic and psychotropic substances.`}]},"sp-public-health":{f:[{e:`Incidence rate = (Number of NEW cases in a specified period / Population at risk in that period) x 1000`,c:`Counts only new events, and always carries a time dimension, for example per 1000 persons per year. It measures the risk of developing the disease.`},{e:`Point prevalence = (Number of ALL existing cases at a point in time / Population at that same point) x 1000`,c:`Counts old and new cases together and has no time dimension. It measures the burden of disease on the health system at a moment.`},{e:`Prevalence = Incidence x Mean duration of the disease`,c:`Valid when both incidence and duration are reasonably stable. A treatment that cures faster shortens duration and therefore lowers prevalence even though incidence has not changed at all.`},{e:`Relative risk = Incidence among the exposed / Incidence among the non-exposed`,c:`RR = 1 means no association, RR greater than 1 means the exposure is a risk factor, and RR less than 1 means it is protective. Obtainable only from a cohort study or a trial.`},{e:`Attributable risk = Incidence in exposed - Incidence in non-exposed;  AR per cent = (that difference / Incidence in exposed) x 100`,c:`Attributable risk is the part of the disease in the exposed group that the exposure accounts for, so it is the measure that matters for public health action.`}],c:[{k:`exam`,t:`Primordial versus primary`,x:`Primordial prevention stops the risk factor from ever appearing; primary prevention accepts that the risk factor exists and stops it causing disease. Teaching schoolchildren never to start smoking is primordial. Giving a smoker a vaccine, or persuading him to quit before disease appears, is primary.`},{k:`trap`,t:`Case fatality rate is not a mortality rate`,x:`The denominator of a case fatality rate is the number of people who have the disease, so it measures how deadly the disease is once you get it, that is its virulence. A cause-specific mortality rate uses the whole population as the denominator and therefore also reflects how common the disease is.`},{k:`mnemonic`,t:`Which design gives which number`,x:`Case-control gives the Odds ratio; Cohort gives the Relative risk. C before C, O before R alphabetically in both pairs. A cross-sectional study gives neither, only prevalence.`},{k:`exam`,t:`The numbers most often asked`,x:`Incidence counts only new cases and carries a time unit; prevalence counts all existing cases at a point. Prevalence equals incidence times duration. Case-control yields the odds ratio and cohort yields the relative risk. Infant mortality rate is per 1000 live births and maternal mortality ratio is per 100 000 live births.`}]}},t={formulas:337,callouts:769};export{t as REVISION_TOTALS,e as revisionByTopic};